      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <head>PRESERVING EGGS.</head>
        <p>PRESERVING EGGS.</p>
        <p>Effectually to preserve. eggs is a cheap_ and simple process. There are .various methods, and I have had experience of three, 'says a writer in the "Feathered World." I have used respectively water-glass, salt and lime, and the latter has. Proved not only cheapest but most successful. • Threepennyworth of lime will pre- serve hundreds of eggs, and all that is necessary are a fcw ordinary .lard bucket's thoroughly cleansed, or a roomy earthenware jar. Vessels of the latter Character are made for the pur- pose, and may be obtained cheaply at any stores. The water from the lime should be placed in the preserving vessel, and the eggs placed in each day as they are laid, taking care that they are perfectly Clean and .undamaged. In this way I have succeeded in keeping eggs in perfectly sound 'condition for a -period of nine, and even twelve months, and, apart from a certain brittleness of the shell, it was quite impossible to .recogniselhem from fresh-laid. If the practice of preserving eggs in this way became general the law of supply and demand would have its uSual levelling effect, and the prices would not vary in the same ratio accord- ' ifig to the seasons.</p>
        <p>BLACK Leghorn Eggs, living egg machines (defy comparison), 45.; Anconas (all winners), is.; Campines. 3s. 6d. All eggs guaranteed. Liv- ing testimonials.—Wilcox, Toll Bat, Grappenhall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <p>BUFF ROCK EGGS, 3s. 6d.; the hest pen liv- ing'; real tested layers.—Ruddock, Howley- hall Farm, Batley.</p>
        <p>CIIIC—KENB, Sevenpeuee. Make your liad hatches good. Eggs, as. dozen. Leghorns, Minorcas, Wyandottes, Rhode Islands. Hens with chickens, Broodies.—Gill, Heath-road, Penketh, Warrington. -</p>
        <p>EOGS ALL THE YEAR ROUND IF YOU Breed from our Trap-nested Stock of several years' testing. Send at once to THOXAS MULLOCK, Pedigree Poultry Farm, Malpas, Cheshire, breeder of high-class utility Poultry, who has the largest poultry farm in Cheshire, with upwards of 30() trap-nests. Breeds kept: Buff and White Orping- tons, Barred and Buff Plymouth Rocks, Black and White Leghorns, White Wyandottes, Houdans, Black Minorcas, Anconas, Rhode Island Reds, Blue Andalusians, Salmon Faverolles, Indian Game. Light and Speckled Sussex, Indian Runner, Buff Orpington, and Aylesbury /ducks and drakes; selected cockerels and pullets from Gs. to 21s. each; eggs for hatching, 55., 7s. 6d. and 108. 6d. per dozen; unfertiles replaced; 15s. 50, and larger quan- tities for incubators, usual reduction. Chicks to order. Poultry houses, all sizes and prices. In- cubators, foster-mothers, grit and oyster shell, fish meal, poultry rings. etc. Inspection and inquiries cordially invited. Vacancy,for pupil. For Sale, several Tons of 'Poultry Manure.</p>
        <p>EGGS.—White Leghoriis, Cam's direct. 3/6 13, unfertile replaced.—E. Earnshaw, Haslingden- road Poultry Farm, Oswaldtwistle.</p>
        <p>EGGS.—White Wyandotte, White Leghorn, Simon Hunter, Barron blend direct; 3s. doz.; carriage paid on 2 dozen; extra layers.—Griffin, Poultryman. Hopwas. Tamworth, Staffs.</p>
        <p>EGGS for Sitting.—Rhode Island Reds, S.C. White Wyandottes, White Leghorns, Black Minorca,s; good laying strains; 2s. 6d. for 13.— Daniel Snape, Slack Farm, Skelmersdale</p>
        <p>EGGS.--Blaek Minoreas from my prize win- ners; White Wyandottes, Cock Ist and special only time shown; Hens, Barron's strain; 35. 6d. dozen, clear replaced once, or 14 unreplaced.— Renshaw, Binge Vicarage, Bury.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>WOUNDED iN BARRACKS Brighten Their Lives ' •</head>
        <p>WOUNDED iN BARRACKS Brighten Their Lives ' •</p>
        <p>Miss Mary Gomall, who is actively engaged .ia looking after the welfare of the wounded soldiers who are receiving attention at the Barracks Hospital, and are having a somewhat dismal time , compared with others who are accommodated ,at other hospitals, makes the fol- lowing timely appeal on their behalf :---- To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,--May I. through the medium of your columns, make an appeal for the loan of a piano/for the Darracks Hospital for the dura- tion of the war?</p>
        <p>With the permission of the Medical Officer in charge, it i proposed that this piano should be placed in an outside recreation room at th 4 Barracks (Hospital), so that entertainments could be given there for those patients well enough to walk across from the hospital. . I should also like to remind your readers that the visiting hours are from 2 to 4 and 5 to 7 in the afternoon, and also that fruit, flowers and Papers, &amp;c.. are always very acceptable .to the patients. By helping them to keep cheer- ful we shall also be assisting them to „regain their' health.—Yours, &amp;c., MARY GORNALL, • 'Member of Voluntary, Aid Detachment, Cheseire 22, , Daresbury, Division, B.R.C.S. Ribblesdale. Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>It has proved that the proportionate amounts• paid for' meat and dairy produce, in com- parison to bread, per head•is four to one, and this is conclusive of an ever, constant market for any increase in Cattle breeding. The effects of this form of farming would be two-fold; the production of more manures, enriching and rendering more productive the land to which it has been. applied; And the production of food, for less fortunate countries. Moreover, it would Create an all round demand for a greater number of hands, both .male and female. It is submitted that the return would prove to 13:1 highly successful, the more so where there exists a mutual sympathy between landowner, farmer and labourer.—" Agricultural' Econo- mist and Horticultural Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <p>THE WEEK'S WORK IN BRIEF. Collect eggs .regularly just now, so a not to encourage broodiness. •</p>
        <p>Well air the poultry houses during the day. Observe utter, cleanliness in the poultry house and yard.</p>
        <p>See that all stock have their breakfast early, especially the youngsters., Push hatching operations forward otherwise the rearing work will get disorganised. elean drinking ICater is 'essential for all stock.</p>
        <p>flive the , hens a good dusting before setting 3.-em.-" Poultry World."</p>
        <p>EXERCISE AND HEALTH.</p>
        <p>xereise• is essential to health, and health to</p>
        <p>productivity, writes Edward Brown, in the " Feathered World." In this direction the farmer and larger operator have a distinct advantage, though that is often sacrifieed to the practice of providing all too abundant supplies of food, so that the bird have no need to take •-• exercise in -finding .nutrition. A further point is that the cost of feeding is thus enormously enhanced, although that is probably of second- ary importance so far as the vigour and laying qualities are concerned. Hv much is lost by these means it is impossible to 'realise. That it must be considerable is. unquestionable. Nor is that limited to the birds thus affected. Where these are used as breeding stock succeeding generations are weakened. My purpose is to suggest that all feeding should be designed to</p>
        <p>encourage exercise, and not, as is to frequently - the case, to check what is not only necessary for complete digestion, but also to; develop the bodily "vigour. Where birds are out on range I</p>
        <p>'4ln. inclined to think that, in the great majority of cases, the quantity of food supplied might be reduced considerably in quantity and in cost,</p>
        <p>and that the birds would be as productive and more profitable than at present. .</p>
        <p>THE SCRATCHING METHOD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <p>In this respect introduction of the scratching method of feeding has undoubtedly been of marked benefit by compelling exercise in order to .secure food, and has retarded tendencies which would otherwise have been harmful in the •extreme. Probably this method of feeding has' not been utilised to the extent which is desirable. It would be a great gain if it were possible to insist that .no -particle of . food .Should be obtainable unless it were worked for. That is a .standard -which it is difficult to attain, for reasons named below, but we are far from having realised our possibilities in this direction. -In Only too"many cases are fowls fed in accordance with the convenience -of owners or attendants, and not that of their 'welfare. I •am coming more and more to the view that the more food is given in a form which compels exercise, and the less in a soft form, the better it will be in every Way, and mainly by reaSon of the use of the muscles required in seeking for .it.. Fowls and turkeys obtain such exercise by scratching more than by walking, ducks by swimming, and geese by wandering. Nothing should t hinder what are wisely designed pro- cesses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>CHICKENS HATCH OUT EIGHT DAYS</head>
        <p>CHICKENS HATCH OUT EIGHT DAYS</p>
        <p>LATE.</p>
        <p>A correspondent in. the " Poultry World" (Annie E. Sutton) says :—I znought you Would like to hear of an experience of mine in won- derful chick hatching. , On the 13th of March I gave a hen a sitting of eggs from my best pen of R.I. Reds,. she proved unsatisfactory, so I procured another brood)r Which pursued her cOnrse faithfully to the,end.:. On the 3rd of this month (when.they were due to hatch) I doubtfully looked to see if any chicks had arrived but none had. I looked again ,on the next day and found two strong chicks, a day only late. I then tested the remaining seven eggs in warm water, and found them all slightly lively. There- was another two days' wait, but still no more chicks. . tested them again with the same result, but no qgn of chipping. I then, placed them in the. incubator, marking the -eggs distinctly, when yesterday, eight days' late., every one of the seven eggs hatched out, the biggest chicks I have had this year. .</p>
        <p>ENGLAND'S Premier laving strains, 283 White Wyandottesn 282 White Leghorns, Tom Barron's best, mated with view to producing com- petition laying winners, unlimited range 4 Eggs 45., selected 55., special 7s. 6d. dozen; unfertiles re- placed.—Reverend liewetson, Beeley Vicarage, Rowsley. EGGS from best laying strains—White Leg= horns," 260-egg strain; Buff Rocks, 245 Tom Barron's direct. Perfect in colour. Large egg: e. dozen, unlimited range; unfertiles replaced.—G Calvert, Aiskew Bedale, Yorkshire.</p>
        <p>LEGHORNS, White, Brown, Black; finest lay- ing strains in England; 3s. 12, 55. 6d. 24, lus. 6d. 50, 20s. 100; prompt dispatch.—John Livesey, Blackburn Small Holdings; Blackburn. PADMAN'S 285-egg strain White Leghorns; eggs, 3s: dozen; 2 dozen, ss. 6d.; Chicks, Is. dozen.—Bromfield, Matlock-road Farm, Coventry. 1111EDIGREE -Winter Layers.—White Wynn- dottes. Single Rhode Island Reds and ,White Leghorns, bred from 288, .280, 279, 260, etc., egg record stock for five generations. Many of my pullets this year have laid 100 eggs each in five winter months; three of my pens have won medals and special prizes in laying competitions; Reds from my stock are now leading in ten months' laying • competition. Eggs, 3s. Gd. dozen; Chickens, Bs. dozen. Full particulars.—W. Measures, Hargrave • Poultry Farm, near Huntingdon.</p>
        <p>ROSECOMB Black Leghorn Eggs.—Many winners in pens; splendid layers, 4s. to Oii. a sitting.—Miss Barkermill, Langley Manor, "etton, Southampton. SIJCCESSFUL CHIcKEN REARING de- pends in a great measure upon careful atten- tion to detail, as clean drinking pots and fresh water at least twice daily, clean litter in coops,, a con- stant supply of small grit and shell, and newly- limewashed coops and brooders before and after each hatch. Good sound wholesome food is also of equal importance. ENTWISTLE BROS.' DRY CHICK FOOD has long been recognised as the sweetest, by far the most nutritious, and evenly balanced ration offered to Poultry Keepers, and the one most suitable for young .Chickens. It is sold in .311). and 71b. bags at Bd. and is. 4d. Ask your Grocer or Corn Dealer for Entwistle's, and see that you get it. Agents:—CARTER &amp; SON. LTD., Bridge-street, Warrington; BARLOW &amp; SONS, Winwick-street, Warrington; and T. GREGORY, Bridge-street, Earlestown.</p>
        <p>rilL7ltliEl( Eggs, 6d. each, from lai•ge second season Bronze birds. White Leghorn Eggs, from Barron and Cam's best laying strains, 2s. 6d. dozen; Chicks, day old. Bs. dozen; all guaranteed strong and healthy.—James Barton, 27, Water- street, Ribchester. UTILITY Black Leghorn' Eggs, laid by pullets hatched from Burn Murdock's 15s. pen, mated to son of hen with record of 251 eggs, ss. per sitting.—Peele, Church House, Ludlow. WinTE—Orpington Stock Cockerels for Sale, Martin Burnham, Esq., Pedigree Stock, 10/6, 12/6 each.—Mr. B. J. Parsonage, Willow Bank Farm, Whitley, Northwich.</p>
        <p>WHITE Leghorn Eggs, Padman's, Barron's strain, record layers, 3s. dozen; unfertiles re- placed.—Harry Phillips. -Studley, Swindon.</p>
        <p>WHITE Wyandotte Eggs, Barron's 283, Cam's 288, Dora Rea 292 strain, 35. 15.—W. Cole. Newton St. Cyres, Exeter, Devon.</p>
        <p>NETlTlTE—Wyandottes, same strain as my V world's record breaker, mated to Cockerels with records from 252 to 292; Eggs, 3s. per dozen. White Leghorns, bred for years for egg production, mated to brothers to Sutton's record hen; Black Leghorns, sisters to my pen competing at Burnley; Eggs, 3s. per dozen.—D. H. Clarke, Fairfield, Foul- ton-le-Fylde.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>CHANGE OF COLOURt</head>
        <p>CHANGE OF COLOURt</p>
        <p>My attention has recently been called, says -Quill, in the Feathered World," to an instance which may be termed the fallacy of the value of the brown egg shell. A certain pullet of a certain well-known pure breed commenced to-lay deep-coloured brown .egg c in accordance with the custom of her breed. When she had laid about sixteen eggs she suddenly reverted ;to a pure while shell, and up to date of information has continued to do so. There could be no mistake, as I. understand she was carefully watched and trapped. Now then, were those brown-shelled eggs ,any richer or more nutritive than the subsequent white ones? And what was the - cause of -the sudden change in shell colour?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>REARING THE CHICKENS.</head>
        <p>REARING THE CHICKENS.</p>
        <p>The rearing of chickens is really, after all, a very tedious job, and the feeding must be at- tended to with the correct am6unt of food Riven and .at the proper time, so if you are away at your work and the lady in charge has promised to feed your chicks then they'll thrive all right. However, my Bantam did lay all right, and the eggs were stored away in a nice cool place in a bo'c of bran. Of course, the two hens very quicl7ly laid sufficient eggs .to place under a small crossbred hen, one that my mother owned; this hen was a half-bred Old English Game, by the way. As luck would have it, she proved a reliable sitter, and you can imagine my delight when she hatched off eleven pretty little chicks.</p>
        <p>Now they must be reared, for they were, so valuable, and I think still, my mother's favour- • ite fad, of _giving, hard-boited egg with stale bread-crumbs an admirable idea. This was given to them, but mind you, only one egg for one brood. You in4.,,ht -ask: '"Why only one?" but here it is. 'for r have proved it since. If you continue the egg-feeding too' ong you will have trouble. Diarrhoea and other ailments arise, and your chicks are no more. The chicken coep has been placed in a small planta-. tion with .any amount of litter and scratching material. The little chicks were fed..on stale bread7soaked in Milk, with coarse oatmeal and canary seed mixed for the last feed at 'night, and they thrived splendidly. 'Water they (lid not have; perhaps this sounds rather queer ,to you, but still it was so, and I am convinced that ir-ehicks were not given water in the early stages, there would be less mortality; mind you, I mean where chicks are, able to get on the grass.—" Poultry. World." •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <p>- -</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 24, 1915.</p>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>TRANSPORT DELAYS.</p>
        <p>Considerable delays are likely to arise under present conditions in the transport of agricill- tural .machinery by rail, and the Board of Agriculthre and. Fisheries desire to call the attention of .farmers tb the desirability of making early arrangements for the purchases and repairs of such machinery so as to allow ample time for delivery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>GOOSEBERRY MILDEW.</head>
        <p>GOOSEBERRY MILDEW.</p>
        <p>The Board of Agriculture and FiAheries have received information that the summer stage of American gooseberry mildew (Sphaerotheca. Mors tivve) was discovered in a Cambridgeshire garden on the 10th inst. All gooseberry growers are advised to examine their bushes carefAlly and should any sign of disease; be found; to spray their bushes with a solution of liver 1 sulphur (one pound to 32 gallons of water). A leaflet describing the disease and giving direc- tions for dealing with it can be obtained • from Lthe Secretary, Board of Agriculture and Fisheries, 4, Whitehall-place, London, S.W., gratis and post free. Letters so addressed need</p>
        <p>• not be stamped. Growers are reminded that by Article 3 of the merican 'GOOseberry- Mildew Order •of 1911, they are required to report the •presence of this tliease on their • premises to, the Board; or the Clerk of the Local Authority for the district, either directly or through,an inspector, and that he failure to report is punishable by a fine.</p>
        <p>TOP DRESSING OF AUTUMN WHEAT. According to the -reports furnished to the Board by their Crop Reporters On the con- dition of autumn-sown wheat in England and Wales at the beginning of April, early-sown wheat on light lands promised well, but in many districts wheat on heavy land, and. also late-sown -autumn wheat on light' land, was backward and had lost colour. • The Board of Agriaulture and Fisheries desire to impress upon, farmers the benefit to be obtained from top-dressing in most of the. cases where the wheat is backward. A suitable manurial dressing would be from one to newt. of nitrate of soda or from 1- to licwit. sulphate of ammonia per acre; if more than lewt. of either is applied, it is desirable to give the manure in two dressings at an interval of not less than a • fortnight. • The later dressing should usually be applied early,. in May. In view of possible 'delays in transit manures required for top-dressing should be ordered at once. _</p>
        <p>The Attention of farmers is drawn to the Board's Special Leaflet No. 23 (The Manuring of Corn in Spring), copies of which may be obtained free of charge and post free on applica- tion to the Secretary, Board of Agriculture and Fisheries, Whitehall-place, London, S.W. Letters so addressed need not be stamped.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>GOOD SHEPHERD.</head>
        <p>THE GOOD SHEPHERD.</p>
        <p>Experience tells me that a good shepherd is half the battle. Such-a man knows every ewe as well .as her constitution, the lambs she has borne, and her value as a breeder. His in- cessant watch-fulness prevents, losses in various ways, and it must be admitted that losses occur too frequently where the sheep are left to them- selves. This is one of the difficulties ex- perienced with a small flock. It is so often in trouble. . The shepherd weighs out the cake and corn, takes care to crush both to his liking, and refeises to fill the troughs when they are dirty.' Troughs half filled with water after a night's 'heavy .rain he turns over to empty, and keeps them turned over, if he is able, until food time comes round. , He chooses the best hay he can find for his lambs. takes care to let them run forward to eat, the young sprouts of the Swedes, and protects them from cold, driving winds. It is, too', the shepherd who keeps the feet right, cutting and dressing the hoof of each ewe as he finds her .suffering from 'footrot, and not allow- ing it to get too - far under the horn. In summer each 'sheep. is carefully watched for maggots and iirompfly cleaned and dressed to prevent a form of damage 'which ,spoils the appearance and reduces the value of the sheep. A 'good 'shepherd, then, is divindisPensable.help to the •flockmas.ter.—Jas. Long. in the "Farm and Home."</p>
        <p>INCREASE OF CATTLE BREEDING.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>POTATO CULTIVATION.</head>
        <p>POTATO CULTIVATION.</p>
        <p>It should be the aim of all growers to keep the land in - which potatoes are growing frequently stirred during the period of growth, and all weeds should be kept down.- The plants should be earthed up the fikt time when they are about 61n. high, and a second time about three weeks later. •</p>
        <p>This drawing up of the soil to the plants pro- motes the formation of tubers, prevents the soil from 'getting. too wet, .and supports the haulm. If the soil has'a, tendency to get wet the ridges should be made as .stepp as possible, but the covering of soil should only be sufficiently • deep to prevent the potatoes as they form from being exposed to-the air and light. Small growers when. lifting a few potatoes at a time should lift alternate. plants or 'pairs of plants, or alternate rows, as may be convenient, in prefer- ence to working straight, through the plot. By doing so the ground is- -stirred and the plants that are left often. bear a larger' crop in cell- sequence, while an additional advantage is that cauliflowers, cabbages, broccoli, Brussels sprouts, kale,, &amp;c., • may at once. be planted in the vacant spaces or opposite them in the, furrows between the rows. It is desirable to lift all potatoes as soon as ripe in order to avoid disease.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>ADVANTAGE OF THE MIDDLEMAN.</head>
        <p>ADVANTAGE OF THE MIDDLEMAN.</p>
        <p>When the middleman goes his regular rounds, and collects the eggs directly from the producer, it is mutually convenient for all concerned; the eggs are purchased" and paid for at once,—a pro- ceeding satisfactory to the poultry-keepers, especially to those , who depend upon their poultry to augment a slender income. Ready money in the direction of a rapid return for their, produce is always w?lcome. Thus the poultry-keeper is satisfied. and the much- maligneld middleman goes on his way rejoicing at the one egg, or so per dozen that he has secured as his share of the transaction. It, is often imagined that the middleman drives a hard bargain with poultry-keepers who are badly in need of ready money, and who have no convenient Mode of transport to market or customer. Undoubtedly the middleman does take /a certain amount of toll for the long dis- tance he may have to travel, but not to the extent that is usually supposed. We are not claiming for, him any great virtue, or suggest- ing that he has qualms about offering low prices, but competition for the eggs is very keen indeed.. Since during certain months of the year the demand is considerably in excess of the supply, the middleman does not have his own way entirely.—Middlesk, in "Farm and Home."</p>
        <p>Any one can make a window box at home with very little trouble. Buy planned half- inch boards of the right lerittfl,.;with pieees. for the ends about eight inches Wide. Nail the front boards over the edges of 'the side pieces, unless the boxes are :mitred at the corners, which would Make them more expensive by requiring the services of a carpenter: When the boards are neatly nailed together tack narrow moulding along the edges and over the corners to give them a finished appearance. Then Paint them the colour of the trimmings of the windows. Secure them with . iron brackets and fill them first with one inch of broken ash Coal and then well prepared earth. Bore three holes with an auger in each box for •drainage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>OUR INCREASING ARMY.</head>
        <p>OUR INCREASING ARMY.</p>
        <p>A large part_ of "Eye-witness's " dispatch is concerned with the increasing, strength of Sir John French's command. "It is a 'matter of common knowledge," says the writer, "that our Army in France has increased immensely both in numbers and perfection of equiPment and armament, and the fruits of this improvement are now beginning to be seen. .. . As regards the moral side, a consciousness of the general iticrease, in power and the feeling that we are growing stronger every day, while the Germans are becoming less strong, is the chief factor responsible for the undoubtedly excellent state of the spirits of our forees, which is even better than ,it has ever been."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>EARLY FLOWERINP CHRYSANTHEMUMS. For decoration in gardens, homes, churches, or anywhere, nothing can approach the chry- santhemum. This class will give you sheaves of flowers at no 'expense at all. , The culture is easy, no manure is needed, they will grow almost anywhere, and no stopping or pinching back being required, ne plant will give scores of flowers in large sprays. Rooted cuttings of this class should be ordered now from special- ists, and from 2d. each upwards very good varie- ties can be obtained to give every satisfaction this season. One thing must be remembered, ' and that is that chrysanthemums must he well staked up with canes about •IfL in length on account of the wood being very brittle. Insert these canes at the time of planting, so as not to injure the roots later on. These canes cost 3s. per 100, and with care will last many seasons, and can be bought front, local seedsmen at'about that price. The war has afiected. the price of almost everything, so the exact price quoted must not be taken for granted: Plants can be planted ,from now onwards, according. to the weather, and a happy medium • between each plant is 2ft. Varieties I liave found very- satigfactory • are the " Masse " family, these being ,very floriferous and Vigorous growers. Choicer varieties will give less Jowers, but larger blooms and grand in shape. For the beginner I recommend the " Masse " family, which are as follows; White Masse, white, aft.; Horace Martin, yellow, 3ft.; George Bowness, crushed strawberry, 3ft.; Mad. M. Masse, deep mauve, 3ft; Crimson Masse, bronze crimson 3ft.</p>
        <p>The 'above varieties do not need disbudding if only for garden decoration. -</p>
        <p>Choice Varieties.</p>
        <p>Frani&amp;ld Early White.—Very fine pure white blooms, broad fiat petals and large flowers; dis- bud; vigorous; height, 'Roi des Blanes.—Fine Pure"white-blooms, iial- row petals; large flowers, habit could be better; does very well disbudded or undisbudded; height, 2Aft.</p>
        <p>Golden.Glow.—A grand large incurved variety, pure yellow, and. a vigorous • upright grower; disbud; height 41t. Ebstob Yellow.—An improved .Horace Martin, buttercup yellow, good grower; does well either . way; height 3ft. Goachers Crimson.—A grand variety,, large crimson flowers; golden reverse, dwarf habit; disbud; height, 2ft. to 24ft.</p>
        <p>Mrs. W. S3-denhani,—Deep crimson, flowers on the Small 'side, very floriferous and easy to grow; good either way; height 21ft. Bronze Goacher.—A bronze sport from Goacher's- Crimson, identical in every way to its parent except colour. Abercorn Beauty.—A beautiful bronze: sport frOm the well-known yellow chrysanthemum " flatish petals, dwarf in habit; disbud; height, 2ft. . Hector.—ln my estimation the best of the pink varieties, large long drooping flowers, colour pale pink; disbud; height 3ft.; well worth growing. Improved Masse.—An improved Mad. M. Masse, as the name implies, deep mauve, growth good, flowers large, looks very well' disbudded; hikight aft. Alrairante.—A decorative, chrysanthemum, but does very well outside, a t-rifle later to bloom (October), very large flowers, chestnut crimson, lipped gold; disbud; vigorous groXver ; height, about 4ft. •</p>
        <p>Perle chattilonaise—A fine variety, colour pearly • white, tinted blush, large flowers, vigorous grower, is becoming a great favourite; height 41t. - ,</p>
        <p>There are others, but the above will be found to give every satisfaction Weak liquid manure can be given when the buds appear, and a word of warning—don't forget to tie them well up with raffia to the canes. and, if necessary, use two or three canes to a plant, it will be worth it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>WINDOW BOXES.</head>
        <p>WINDOW BOXES.</p>
        <p>PRUNING ROSES.</p>
        <p>• March is generally considered quite 'early enough for the hardier sorts, dnd. April for the teas. Owing to' a few warm days many, roses push out green leaves on the ends of the shoots, and amateurs on seeing them' hasten to prune' their plants. , The tops of the branches may be growing, but this holds in check, the buds further down the shoots. The loss of sap may be considerable when cutting the growing branches, but we have never found any -harm result. When -pruning always use a sharp knife, as the secateurs, we find, bruiSe the wood and the cut is not clean. See also that the inside of the tree is well thinned out, Other- wise a mass of growth that will hinder the in- gress of light and air will follow, and there will be no room for the proper development of wood. Always cut to it bud growing in an out- ward direction. The above remarks apply to bush roses and standards, as climbers only need the dead wood to be cut out, the very long branches being tipped to where the wood is well ripened, as well as the short, twiggy growths. If there. are any old and worn-out branches, remove them, and if a healthy young growth of last year is present, then let it take the place of the one removed.—Wick, in "Farm and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>SOWING PEAS.</head>
        <p>SOWING PEAS.</p>
        <p>The;iisual.way of sowing peas is to draw drill 3in. deep with a hoe,-the.base of the drill when got out in this way being quite narrow, thus causing all the seeds to run together quite thickly. There artiVo beth-,r,ways'. One ,is to make-the bottom of the drill as broad as is the top, thus allowing pf the peas being more widely distributed. The other is to draw two drills in the ordinary way side by side, placing the peas along each drill from 2in. to 3in. apart, this latter distance answering for the tallest varieties of peas. In the single wide row the distance between the seeds Should be as above. The result is an immense saving of seed, the plants are much stronger, branch more freely, and are far more productive, while the danger from drought and thrips is far le*. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>PLANTING IVY.</head>
        <p>PLANTING IVY.</p>
        <p>That there exists some prejudice against the big-leaved ivies cannot be disputed,- and the reason is that their aspect is rather sombre. This charge cannot, however, Iv made in rela- tion to the many small-leaved sorts,. and especially so in respect of those with gold and silver variegation. Indeed they are admirable for walls which are unsuitable for either flower- ing plants or fru,itS.. The present is an excel-. lent month for planting them in deep, fertile soil. In training, the bottom of the wall should have primary consideration, or it may be found later extremely difficult to furnish it hand- somely.—" Agricultural Economist and Horti- cultural Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>AMERiCAN REBUFF FOR</head>
        <p>AMERiCAN REBUFF FOR</p>
        <p>GERMANY.</p>
        <p>A n American reply to Germany's last -plote, which accused the United States of not observ- ing a strict neutrality, has. been delivered and published. The reply as;erts positively that the Washington Government has not and will, not abandon any neutral right to benefit any belligerent. It has admitted the right of blockade if actually exercised and effectually maintained. As for the export of war supplies, prohibition wmild mean a change in the law which would. affect differently the different belligerents. Such a change wduld then 'be "a direct 'violation, of the neutrality of the United States"</p>
        <p>The meeting was presided over by .Mrs. A. IL Crostield, who was supported by Mrs. Emma C. Osborne (the honorary secretary of the association), Miss Knowles (the speaker), Mrs. T. Pemberton, Miss Elliadi and other ladies, and by:Mr.'E,' A.-Sanders (the Liberal agent).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>WAR WORK FOR WOMEN.</head>
        <p>WAR WORK FOR WOMEN.</p>
        <p>Gail for Female Labour to Release Men for</p>
        <p>the Front..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>SE EAPLAINED TO WOMEN UtERALS.</head>
        <p>SE EAPLAINED TO WOMEN UtERALS.</p>
        <p>The -Warrington Women's Liberaj Association are earning quite a reputation as local pioneers. with works calculated to assist the country in the present strenuous" tithes of -War. No sooner was the national movement to supply comforts for our soldiers inaugurated than they forth- with established working parties, at which large numbers of their' members congregated and, by dint of assiduous sewing and knitting, managed to turn out bale after bale Of those articles of apparel which "Tommy Atkins" so sorely needed in order to help him to withstand the rigours •of wintry trench-warfare and which, in. Varying variety, he still stands urgently in need of, and for Which his' whole- hearted gratitude is more than 'ample reward for all the pains and trouble expended. Now the call has come for women .to register them- selves against their. being wanted for more strenuous- service; in short, for industrial ser- vice—for' Work in the munition factories, in the shopSund in the offices, in order-that such work as they may be capable of giving shall be the means, of releasing for the more •serious.,busi- ness in France and in Flanders, many a young man who would be able to take his place in the fighting line, but whose services have been so far required at home in order to provide the armaments, the uniforms and accoutrements and ,the other theusand and one things :that are required by the Army, as well as in coping with 'the daily needs and requirements of. the vast civilian population who Must perforce remain spectators of " the great game."</p>
        <p>'WARRINGTON WOMEN'S OPPORTUNITY. And the gall having come, the Warrington Women's. Liberal Aoociation have taken .some of the first steps to secure for it, locally, a response that shall be worthy of the women- folk of a town that has already given so lavishly of its men, and is still dedicating them in their tens and their scores to the ser- vice 'of ,King and country. On Monday evening a special and largely- attended meeting of the association was held in the John Crosfield Memorial Hall, for the prime purpose of hearing Miss Knowles, an organising officer of the Women's Pepartment of the •Government Labonr EXchanges- explain the proposals put forward by the Board. of Trade for making up. by employitig women, the serious 13hortage • that must occur in • the ranks of labour in the event of it being found necessary to .put the full fighting power of the nation into the field. against the Austro- German-Three enemy.</p>
        <p>providing the soldiers with arms and ammuni- tion to use in such arms, they must be provided with clothes, .and, in this connection, it was a well-known fact that many of the army clothing factories were badly handicapped by a shortage of female labour. Women were wanted, and wanted at once, to make the Uniforms for the soldiers- and also to take up the leather work •which was required in connection with the various kinds Of equipments for the troops. In addition to arming and clothing the men, the country had to see to it that the food supply was carried on, and ih this connection the question of labour for agriculture was becoming a very serious one.</p>
        <p>Work Women Can Co On The Farms.</p>
        <p>In the South of England great difficulty was experienced in getting sufficient farm labour for the growing:of crops for the forthcoming season, and in Scotland the shortage was equally acute, whilst in Lancashire and Cheshire the farmers were already beginning to sell their cows because the price of beef was very high and because the scarcity of men to milk them was abnormal. The consequence of all this was that women were urgently wanted for farm work; especially for milking and dairy work, and such like lighter occupations. Again, it was ,thought that women could do a large amount of the clerical work that was at present done by,men, and certain large 'employers had already taken steps to ;release .men from -their offices and to replace them by women; as for instance, railway companies, banks, muni- cipal authorities, insurance offices, &amp;c. There were many. men who could be released from shops of various descriptions if only the women came forward; indeed there was a variety of miscellaneous occupations in which women could temporarily displace men who were capable of serving their country. For instance, there Were cases in which women had been set to work as school attendance officers, whilst in their own town of Warrington she heard of women being engaged in wire work which was formerly done by men.</p>
        <p>Other Work That Will Release Men.</p>
        <p>Other directions in which women had found, or could find employment were in tin and copper works,.--in cleaning railway carriages and in doing light portering, to mention but a few of the many. Before the women could satisfac- torily replace the men it would, in a great many instances, be ircessary to train them in some way, and anybody who volunteered must be pre- pared to undergo some form of training unless she had previously been in industrial life of the same description. Various training schemes were already on foot. In connection with this women's work people of varying classes would be. thrown together and there was a clanger that some of the women might be squeamish on social grounds, and the way in which she .would sug- gest that that difficulty could be got over was by Putting women of the differtnt classes to work in separate detachments, each detachment under the supervision of one of the particular class composing it.</p>
        <p>Class Squeamishness That *Will Evaporate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>GOVERNMENT'S INVITATION TO</head>
        <p>THE GOVERNMENT'S INVITATION TO</p>
        <p>THE WOMEN.</p>
        <p>As we explained last week, already in certain important occupations there are not enough men and women to do the' work. This shortage will certainly spread to other occupations as more and more men join the fighting forces. In 'order to meet both the prewnt and the future needs of national industry during the war, the Government wish to obtain particulars of the, women available, with: or .without pre- vious training, for paid employment, Accord- ingly they invite all women who are prepared, if needed, to take paid employment of any kind, industrial, agricultural, clerical, &amp;c:, to enter themselves upon the register of women for war service which is being prepared by the Labour Exchanges. Women can register at the Labour Exchanges or on a form to be supplied by the local agency of the unemployment fund or women's societies. The Object of the Proposed registration is to discover what reserve force of women's labour can be made available, if reo _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>MISS TRUDA CROSFIELD KEPT AWAY BY</head>
        <p>MISS TRUDA CROSFIELD KEPT AWAY BY</p>
        <p>I LLNESS.</p>
        <p>At the outset</p>
        <p>. Mrs. CROSFIELD conveyed a message of regret from Miss Truda Crosfield, who was unable: to attend owing to illness. Miss Crosfield par- ticularly regretted her inability to be. present, as it. had been her intention to relate to the Women Liberals a few of the experiences she had. had in France during the last ,few months. • Mrs. Crosfield then introduced the speaker to the audience, remarking that the topic of how the women could work in the place of the men who had gone to the Front to fight for them was one that should interest each one of them. (Hear, hear.) , A letter regretting inability, to be present was received from Mrs... Ziba Armitage, who was staying at Barmouth, and who sent heir best wishes for a successful Meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>BIG TOVCVS MEETING TO BE</head>
        <p>BIG TOVCVS MEETING TO BE</p>
        <p>CALLED.</p>
        <p>Nia)or's Prompt Action.</p>
        <p>Miss KNOWLES began by explaining that that meeting was 'called as sort of preliminary, which, it was proposed, should lead up to a town's meeting, which the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) had kindly promised to 'call. The scheine of war 'service for -women had been started by the Board- of Trade with a view; to obtaining a census of women who would be willing to come forward and offer themselves for paid employment of, any description, with a view to releasing more men for the Front. and with a view to carrying on the work of those men who had already joined the Colours. (Hear, hear.) The 'Census would show how many more men could be released to join the .Army should the emergency arise. It did not follow, of course, thaV,Alie,,women who regis- tered themselves as willing to undertake certain branches of work would ever have to be called upon; indeed, they all hoped that the war would come to an end before it was necessary to use women . to any 'large extent, but the (iovernment wished to he in a position to see, in the event of more men being required for the Front, how many women would be available for carrying on some of the industries of the ,country; how it would be possible to allow those men to go with, easy minds, and' how it would be made possible for employers to carry on their businesses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Must Be Paid Employment.</head>
        <p>Must Be Paid Employment.</p>
        <p>The employment that was taken up by women in this connection must be paid employment, otherwise it would place the men in an extra- ordinarily difficult position when they returned from the Front and wanted to take up their employment' again. The classes of people whom the appeal should . reach were (a) the leisured class, who might be willing to come forward with the offer of their services, on patriotic ground; and (b) the women who had been in industry, but, who had retired owing to mar- riage, improvied circumstances, &amp;c. The census of women. available for war service was not meant to include the ordinary unemployed;; they Were already registered in the ordinary way at the Labour Exchanges, and would be given work in so far as they were suitable. Naturally labour recruited from .the leisured or retired classes would not be introduced until use had been made of those who were actually out of work at the .moment. - Two kinds of work in which women Could engage were most urgently needed, for they had to do with supply- ing the wants .of the naval and military forces. The first was armaments work, for it was user less sending an Army' to The Front unless they were adequately supplied with,the munitions. of war, and in the direction of supplying those munitions women could accomplish much use- ful work,. and 'there was a great Call for their services</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Women's Work in Ammunition Factories.</head>
        <p>Women's Work in Ammunition Factories.</p>
        <p>One firm alone, engaged in turning out ammunitions, at present required 1,500 women workers, and would want a thousand more in May, and another two thousand in August. The work to which women would be put in arma- anents factories was the filling of shells, the testing of the fuses of shells, the turning out of automdtic press work, &amp;c. Again, as well as</p>
        <p>'However, she thought that the squeamishness to which • she had alluded would speedily evaporate, when the women came to realise that - the work upon which they were engaged was national work, and when they remembered that men of all ranks and grades were fighting side by side in the trenches without .paying the slightest regard to class differences. Another problem that was found to creep in was con- cerned in what might be described as unprac- tical enthusiasm, for there would be a large number of registrations by those who 1, ould offer their services in a moment of patriotisM, and who would withdraw as soon as they found that real, hard work was meant. It was, there- 'fore, .as well to make it clear that 'the jobs which women were being asked to fill were not jobs that merely consisted in sitting on a chair so many hours a day and in smiling pleasantly, but that they were jobs -which wculd involve I downright work; in short, the work which their fathers and brothers had been I.6inif before they went to the Front. (Hear, hear.) A great many women would be vastly improved by a little hard work. (Laughter:) Awl even if, -as- well might •be the cage in some instances, the work involved a certain amount of aetral loss of health, she thought the women sho-lid respond to the call made Upon their services, 'realising how, much more the men were giving and how their efforts, supplementing those of the men, would play a part in speeding the end of this terrible war. A.notha. difficulty 'centred round the question.</p>
        <p>Will Not Displace Men When The War !s Over. What is going \to be el'one at the end of the war with the women who come forward; if they are taken into &amp;,labour market; how are they going to be got ota...Lan and make room for the men when they comeback? They would be doing no service to iticlustry if they clogged the labour market with women Who would displace men at the end of the war They must certainly guard against anything of the sort, and in order to do sá the Labour Exchanges, before sending any women to chi war work, were 'making ,it clear that the work was emergency work; that it was for • the time being only, and that it would auto- matically cease at the end of the war, provided there was man available to take up the par- ticular. work. 'Such- men as, came back must be at liberty, to take up their work again, and the women who had stepped into:. the breach and "carried on " in their absence Must be quite prepared to retire. That must be Plainly understood from the start; With regard to the housing of women who might have to leave their homes in order to take up sfiecial war work the. speaker said thht various suggestions were under consideration, one being that hostels should be. provided and another that hut accommodation might be ,ternporarily found. The scheme was an 'enormous one, and could not come into operation immediately. Con- sequently women who handed in their names should not feel discouraged or slighted if. after registering, • some time elapsed before their ser- vices were called upon</p>
        <p>Many Registrations—But " More and Yet More " Wanted.</p>
        <p>So far there had only been about 33,000 regis- trations for women's war service in the whole country, and in Lancashire and Cheshire only from 2,000 to 3,000 had been forthcoming. From those facts it. seemed to her that the women Of England had not 'yet quite realised what was expected of them; had not realised that the appeal was of the greatest, national importance; had not realised how much depended upon this country being able to Carry on its industries if more men had to go to the Front. This appeal afforded the women of the country a glorious opportunity ofi.showing their patriotism. (Ap- plause.). SO far the response from Warrington had not been what they might expect, for only about a hundred had so far come forward. She could not -believe that there were . only a hundred people in a town like theirs who were prepared to answer to the call of their country. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>The speaker then answered a number of ques- tions. To. one queStion she replied that, of course, some women would be able_ to give their whole time to the work that might be demanded; of them, whilst others would only be able 'to give two or three hours a day, and arrangements' might be made for the latter to work in short shifts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>EQUAL WAGES FOR EQUAL VALUE GIVEN.</head>
        <p>EQUAL WAGES FOR EQUAL VALUE GIVEN.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. Parker reminded the speaker that she had made no mention- of the matter of re- muneration; and she ,answered that, so far as possible, the women ought to claim equal wages with men for equal value of work done. (A pplause.) That Would. be perfectly fair to the men and also to the women.</p>
        <p>WAR WILL po THE_COUNTRY A DEAL OF</p>
        <p>GOOD.</p>
        <p>Mr. PARKER then proposed a vote of thanks to the speaker, and in doing so, ,4-aid: that he thought that in one Way and another the war: would do the people of the country- a gi?eat deal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <p>of good. It had lifted them- out of the ruts into which they had dropped, and it had given a variety to their lives and occupations such as they had never experienced. Many of the occupations which the war was opening up to women would be permanent in their character, for the production of wealth, by work, pro- duced more needs, and therefore Produced a call for more work. They-must not get into the way of thinking that the amount of work that could be done in Li country was of a limited quantity, for work in producing value produced mere work. His own opinion was that 'every woman who engaged in the various classes of work, in which, their services, were noNf so urgently re- quired, need not look forward necessarily to being displaced when the men returned from the Front. The country, by this war, was in- volved in a very heavy task, and it was united- as it had never before been united. Nothing else could have made the people Of one mind - like this war lia.d doe. 'These were indeed, in matters political, days in • which "none were for party but all were for State." (Applause.) All were united, in the great task to which the nation stood calmmitted. (Renewed applause.)</p>
        <p>The Bravery of the Women Left Behind. There was another thing that they had to bear in mind in these days,*and that was, although they recognised to 'the full the bravery of the - men. who had gone ,forth in answer to their country's call; that a heavy burilen was laid upon the women who had to remain at home-- the mother, the wife, the sister. Indeed, he sometimes thought that theirs was the heavier burden of the two. The excitement, the glory of the, battle, -sustained the men at the' Front, and enabled them to take up their burden joy- fully, but the woman at home had no such excitements to sustain her; no such sense of glorious achievement to buoy her up and on. Hers it was to weep at home, fearful lest any morning should come the message that some- one near and dear to her had been slain and now lies in a shallow \ grave "somewhere in France" or in Flanders. That burden of anxiety was a heavy one that the womenfolk had to bear, and he did not know that there was any better way in which the women could :lighten it than by throwing themselves heart and soul into the work which they were now being asked to do for their country and their King. (Hear, hear.) The call ought to come strongly to .those women who were so circum- stanced as to be at liberty to respond to it, and, hearing, they should respond, remembering that it was only by every unit doing its part that they could accomplish the great purpose which the country had set out to accomplish. (Hear, hear, and applause.)</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S CHANCE OF RECORD- MAKING. Mrs. OsnonNT, in seconding, said she would like to see a .good_response to the call to service from the members of the Women's Liberal Association, and also from the town of War- rington as a whole. "Let us have a record for Lancashire from the town of Warrington," she observed in conclusion.</p>
        <p>The proposition was then put and carried with acclamation.</p>
        <p>MRS. CROSFIELD'S CALL TO WOMEN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <p>• Mrs. CROSFIELD remarked that all must realise, how many works had been deprived of workmen who had gone to swell the ranks of the new Army. We had followed the growth of this Army (the greatest England had ever had) with pride. ,(Applause.) But the greater it grew, the:greater heeunie the need for women. to come foiqvard and fill the places left vacant by tflo-Ser who had volunteered their services where they were even more urgently needed *(viz., in the field). We had to do ;Our level best to keep .going the work of the country, and so the women were being asked, as far as possible, to come forward to assist with that end in view. That women . might lie induced to register them- selves, she asked those present to circulate amongst their friends and acquaintances all that they had heard from Miss Knowles that evening. By offering their services the women of the country would be really helping the growth of our • wonderful new Army, first by -releasing men and enabling them to do their, duty, and, secondly,, by assisting in increas- ing the output of the Munitions of war. As General Sir John French had recently,!' made clear, that was a. direction in which more - labour Was urgently required. More and still • more munitions were required, and the more the country was able to produce the sooner would the war end. (heer, hear) To her mind, the manufacture of ammunition offered to women . a direction in -.-hich they could accomplish work of the highest importance.</p>
        <p>Women's Unique Opportunity. To-day the womenfolk had a unique oppor- tunity of showing to the nation, and to the world at large, that they were really able and ready to render services to the nation. (Hear, hear) They were not going to -work on suffer- ance; they had been asked, to help and now was their opportunity to come forwUrd and show what they could do, and how useful their work could be: (Hear,. hear:) In France, where practically every mall able to bear arms had' been. requisi- tioned , for service, women had filled the vacant posts most successfully. They found them at the head of busiiness concerns, tilling the ,ground, driving taxi-cabs, conducting 'buses, managing hotels and working the tele- graph offices .and post offices everywhere. To her mind, women in France had always been far more actively concerned in work and business than had women. in England, and now that fact was standing the French nation in good stead in her hour .of great difficulty. When a woman in England was married—whether she had a • family or not—she generally liked to retire from business. French women, how'ever, rarely did that. Even the fact that they might have children did not, 45 4 general rule, deter therm - from doing a certain amount of work that brought in wages, with the result that it was generally to be found that the married.people in France lived very comfortably indeed, and were nearly always able to afford little holidays and certain luxuries that were not always within the reach of the people of the same class in life in England. To-day the women 9f Frivace had come forward wonderfully and theY had set their English sisters a splendid example.</p>
        <p>English Women as Good as French Women.</p>
        <p>There was no reason why the English women should lag behind; why they should not emulate the excellent example of the French women; en . example that was being set by all classes. ' English women were just as good as vVere French women; indeed, she supposed she Ought, to sal . they • were even better. (Laughter, ana ' applause.) The work which the English women were now being asked to assist in was work which would help to terminate the war; indeed, it was work which was second only in importance ; to the work Whial our gallant soldiers are dling' at the Front. She vas very sorry to hear that se far, only about a hundred won'ten of Warring' ton had responded to the call of work: in" deed she was quite astonished at the smallnes3 of the number. Still, the scheme was yet verY young and she was sure that, after that meet" ing, there would be a splendid response in the town. (Hear, hear.) In the matter of the 'use- , ful work that had been, So far accomplished 07 Warrington Women's. Liberal Association 1I3( practically led the way, • for at their sewing Meetings they had turned out thousands of ger" ments. They had shown that they could nurse, knit and sew, and now let- them show that thel could .discharge the fresh responsibilities whll devolved upon them whilst the men were ing for their country. (Applause.) Mrs. E. A.' Sanders' then sang "Are W° - Downhearted ?" after which the meeting enn eluded with a vote of thanks to Mrs. Crosfield, which was heartily accorded on the propogtion of the Liberal Agent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>WARRINGTON LIBERAL ASSOCIATION. The War Obscures Politics at Annual Meeting.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON LIBERAL ASSOCIATION. The War Obscures Politics at Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>MR. C4O,SFIELD AND CONSCRIPTION.</p>
        <p>Reply to Unseasonable but Legitimate Criticism of</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Liberal As,sociation was held in the John Crosfield Memorial Hall, Bewsey-street, on Wednesday _</p>
        <p>evening, when a good attendance was presided over by Mr. -C. E. Parker (the president of the Association), whilst those present included Mr. A. H. Crosfield (prospective Liberal candidate), -31-esdames E. Osborne (the secretary of the Woinen's Liberal Association), Dutton and J. T. Locker, Aldermen James Evans and Tinnion, Councillors Wilkinson, Skelland and Barton, and Messrs. W. Clegg, A. Cook, Peter Peacock, ink., F. Hammersley, T. Weston, S. H. Walker, A. Israel, E. J. Davies, W. Haywood, E. W. James, J. Mather, P. A. Brown, and E. A. Sanders (Liberal agent). Letters regretting inability to attend were received from Miss Bessie Dutton, Councillor Locker, and Messrs. Ziba Armitage, J. W. Meackin and P. O'Brien.</p>
        <p>Warrington would have the :,atisfac-tfon of feel- ing that they had played no small part in help- ing in the attainment of that victory. OFFICERS RE-ELECTED.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. CLEGG, adopting a suggestion thrown out by Councillor Wilkinson, proposed that "owing to the national crisis" the whole of last year's officers be re-elected en bloc, and that they be thanked for their past services. This was formally seconded and carried, the result being that the following gentlemen will continue to serve in the different offices named: President: Mr. Charles E. Parker.</p>
        <p>Vice-presidents: Mr. A. H. Crosfield, Mr. Ziba Armitage, Aldermen Pierpoint, Tinnion, Evans, and Burton, Councillor Roberts, Messrs. Wm. Madden, Wm. Bibby, Geo. Walker, Jos. Wilkin- son. E. Wynne-Jones, Wm. Russell, J. Jarman, J. G. Withinshaw, John Dodds, IL G. Roberts, Wm. Clegg, Councillor Halliwell, Messrs. M. Howarth, Thos. Dale, and Jos. Ainsworth. HOn. Secretary :,Councillor J. T. Locker.</p>
        <p>Hon. Treasurer: Mr. Chas. E. Parker. Co-optative members of Finance Committee: Alderman Tinnion, and Messrs. Jos. Wilkinson,</p>
        <p>Geo. Weaver, Councillor Halliwell, Messrs. Wm. Bibby, Geo. Walker, Councillor Lindley, Messrs. P. Delaney, E. W. Jones, J. Critchley, P. H. Brown, Councillor Hawthorn and Mr. T. Pemberton.</p>
        <p>Officers and ward representatives: Mr. C. E. Parker, Councillors Wilkinson and Locker; Town Hall, Mr. W. Patrick; Whitecross, Mr. A. Edwards; Bewsey, Councillor Gough ;. Orford, Mr. A. • Hoyle; St. John's, Mr. E. G. Hughes; Fairfield, Mr. M. Howarth; Howley.. Mr. Wm. . Clegg; St. Austin's, Mr:. A. Israel, Mr. 11. Roberts; Latchfoyd, Mr. G. Toulinin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>LETTERS FROM SOLDIER MEMBERS.</head>
        <p>LETTERS FROM SOLDIER MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>The Liberal Agent mentioned that he received ft letter from Captain H. Gordon Roberts about a fortnight ago, saying that he was progressing favourably, after being wounded, and that he hoped to be rejoining his comrades in the near future. (Applause.) Extracts from a letter written by Private A. 1- E. Hoyle, of the Motor Transport Section of the A.S.C., and .one of the secretaries for the Orford Ward, were read to the meeting</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>FINANCES.</head>
        <p>THE FINANCES.</p>
        <p>The PRESIDENT, who is also the treasurer of the Association, presented the financial, statement, 'Which showed the Association to be 0211 2s. 9d. to the good, when assets were set against liabilities. The statement was adopted, on the proposition 0,1 Alderman TINNION, seconded by Councillor WILKINSON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>ANNUAL REPORT. Pre-War and Ante-War Efforts</head>
        <p>THE ANNUAL REPORT. Pre-War and Ante-War Efforts</p>
        <p>11)etailed.</p>
        <p>..._The AGENT then presented the report of the •Uon. Secretary (Councillor J. T. Locker), who W 8 unable to be present. The report read as follows The half-year 'commencing January, 1914, 'was the busiest in the history of the party, rel 7 section working as it had never worked before, and doing its best to contribute its share to the political education and social success of the Party. Mass meetings were held at very short intervals, and to the following we owe our thanks: Mr. Hamar Greenwood, M.P. for Sun- derland; Mr. A. G. C. Harvey, M.P. for Roch- dale; Sir George. Teulmin, M.P., Mr. W. Bar- ton, M.P., Mr. P. M. Oliver and Mr. J. B. Alartindale. The wards had held successful socials, whist drives, hot-pot 'suppers, teas and Concerts. The stewards of the mass meetings had been entertained to supper by Mr. and Mrs. . H. Crosfield. An open-air land campaign /lad been successfully carried through, corn- , 'lrtenoing on May 25th, and finishing on Wednes- day, June 10th, fifteen meetings being held in various parts of the town, and ending in a final mass meeting at Bridge Foot. about 2,000 people .being present. The Junior Liberals had also a Conference in the Parr Hall, which was a pro- rre__need success, the following gentlemen ad- _essiag the meetings in the afternoon and even- inin% C. M.P., Si_ .r.r • Hayward. Mr. P. Raffau, gan. li.j m ,ederick Norman, Mr. G. Hay Mor- i'. M. Olive .P.. Mr. J. B. Martindale and Mr. Thy. D r. rogro • its open.it aslve Labour Leacrue had again held thro iugh inrucalnpaign, and ° had carried it ie usual efficient manner, many local speake Speakers se I's taking part, in addition to the it down from headquarters, includ- ing Our oh; , favourite, Mr Tom Wing,. M.P.</p>
        <p>Organisation Perfected The registration work had been thoroughly Carried out, every house in the town having ;een visited. The committee of the Peter "cock ChaHenke Cup Competition had brought to a successful conclusion the Cup Competition and :i_ main, a record entry having been secured. The wards had all heen perfectly organised. Were ready. Our subscribers had responded the usual healthy fashion. Every member of the party had been informed of the excellent Programme arranged for the winter. Finally, e half-year had been brought to a close by that memorable event, the opening of that mag- nificent building,_ the John Crosfield Memorial by Mrs. John Crosfield, a lady we are all Proud of.</p>
        <p>The Coming ,of the War Cloud. The war cloud, almost without warning. covered the country from Land's End to John o' ?,_rnate. In an instant all politics were dropped. '1473 country became united, and we were all !I:iends and prepared to meet the foe. A poli- Ileal truce was signed to 31st December, Oa ti,d has again been renewed to December 31st, This truce, speaking on behalf of the 'allY to' which I have the honour to belong, shall be faithfully and truly kept. Since the outbreak of war, only formal busi- t:ss meetings have been held, and purely regie- eticel work carried out, but the officials of the reSsociation are being continually called upon to or assistance in various ways, both locally from headquarters in London. Several to i. of thanks have been received to testify 'ne work done.</p>
        <p>hird of the Ward Workers with the Forces. are proud to record the fact that up to r ece "nle of making out this report, we have iv. tiew ed the names and addresses and full par- tTra of .310 ward workers .(a little over one- 7t), who are serving in the Army and Navy 11-a.vig.,,iB gigantic struggle, One ,ward—Orford-:- list7R its three secretaries at the Front. This not yet complete, and we appeal to all ha4-mmB to bring in the names of workers- who , tit:. enlisted, at the earliest possible opportu- eki., We are compiling a roll of honour for Ate' ward, to be hung up in the John Cropfield lueLl°rial Hall. Out of this 310 we shall be foie if we escape having to record sacrifices, uP to the time of making this report we vAlc'st Hugh Fogg, West-street, killed; John Dawl°,lllPson, Bostock-street, killed; and Edgar th, t,...e18, Forster-street, who went down with lette's°mnouth; whilst we have had several who ra and visits from wounded members, all of ttl are progressing favourably.</p>
        <p>Th• repo''haks to the, Women. A ills cannot be complete without Wei:- voce virort.., to the work of the Women Libei;als. Their lab 4. however, has been, so continual and preliTle, and has been carried through to the we —,.„,,t tinle with such excellent results, that seh;7lP.,...letely fail to find Nfords to express our thar-47' ,untier the circumstances we abandon tvaine‘aB,x, and . leave it to Mrs. Osborne (the rt 118 eeeretary) to give us a supplementary thrit en behalf of the ladies. There s one - 08;31gori i 08;31g. .hoer, howelr, which we eannot allow Mrs. e to ref...._ n t ke charge Of, and that is with thCr,Ce to the gift of the magnificent piano to Sehl•vv°lllen's Liberal Association by 111 . „less' lozezi:, and also with ieference to the kind- tha ' tee ladies in allowing this Associa_tiol.l kindlillse of the piano on all occasions. TI sl, 1 . gra,V4-e" We aCkllolViedge with the sincerest 4ude and thanks. Theil • we e.organisation of the Junior Organisation. the -; have pleasure in recording the fact that o.,_a:r9nior Liberals have been completely a re- and are in a fair way towards estai i- the ng thempelves as a very useful branch of IllaintitrtP Por this success our thanks are junia"ue to Mr. Crosfield. I arai sure the kr. 07 will feel the loss (killed in action) of Illan, adst°ne. the grandson of the great states- i,ecentrho addressed one of their meetings so • -Y in ' the Co-operative Hall. This feel-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>VOI LI ntaryism.</head>
        <p>VOI LI ntaryism.</p>
        <p>ing will be shared by the whole party and by our friends in Sankey-street. This is only one of many instances where a promising career has been cut short in this fight against military despotism. The whole country is proud of the men who are guiding our destinies, and the whole world, including our enemies, are struck by the spectacle of unity in the Empire—from India, Canada, Australia, New Zealand, and South Africa. We all feel loyal comrades and fellow citizens in this magnificent Empire: This war has swallowed up all issues which divided us. We intend to see it through tea finish, and back up to our utmost the men pho have stood by us in defence of right and freedom. (Applause.) • The PRESIDENT proposed the adoption of the report, which he described as a capital and in- spiring one. Councillor WILKINSON seconded, and it was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>WOMEN'S WORK.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S WORK.</p>
        <p>How Splendidly They Have Done H.</p>
        <p>Mrs. OsaonNk then presented her annual report as the secretary of the Women's Liberal Association. At the outset she mentioned that the report of the Men's Association had made reference to the fact that the truce which the Liberal party entered into with regard to party politics had been loyally kept. That applied to the Women's Association as well as the men's. Instead of political work, as in previous years, they had devoted .themselves since war was declared, to the making, of wearing apparel for the soldiers and sailors, and by so doing had added much to their material comfort, and, she might also add, judging by the numerous letters which they had received, shown that their sym- pathy and support were with them in their efforts. The women had been enabled to dis- tribute numerous gifts through the kind donations of friends, rich and poor, and also as a result of the money so generousl, sub- scribed by the members of the Men's Association and raised by a series of ward socials held in that hall.</p>
        <p>Sacrifice That Has Encouraged. The members of the women's committee had put in a considerable amount of time and hard work in the purchasing, cutting out and seeing to the making up of material, and in the knit- ting of socks, scarves, helmets, the making up of parcels, and the providing of teas, &amp;c., for which they deserved great credit. But they had been greatly encouraged by the splendid response and work of their women; by the way in which they had sacrificed not only their time in helping to make garments, but by contribut- ing in various ways to the fund. Not only purse strings, but- heart strings had been loosened. Never before in the history. of war- fare had so much good been bNought of evil.</p>
        <p>3,556 .Garments for " Tommies." It would probably be of interest for them to know that they had made and sent out 3,556 garments for the soldiers and sailors. Consign- ments had ..been bent to the 4th South Lanes. Territorials, 2/4th South Lancs. Territorials, the National Reservists, Town Hall Committee, the Eva McLaren Hospital (where Miss CroS- field had done yeoman service), General Moore (for the Veterinary Corps), the 6th Manchester •National _ Reserve,guarding, the Ship- Canal and railway bridges, the Belgian refugees at Alex- andra Palace, the Warrington Infirmary, and to local men stationed in all parts of Great Britain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>Even the Children had Helped.</head>
        <p>Even the Children had Helped.</p>
        <p>They had .also sent cigarettes, tobacco, pipes, chocolates, stationery and other useful necessities —all those things were kindly given by sympa= thetie' friends; even little children having spent their savings to purchase gifts for the country's brave men. They were concentrating their efforts at present more particularly on the men in the trenches. They, according to letters received from them, really needed atten- tion, and were frequently.. applying for parcels. To continue the work, and to defray the cost of postage, the Association needed funds. The committee had not only to work hard in getting the garments made, but had to provide ways And meals to raise money. She hoped those who had not yet contributed and were waiting to be asked, would help them for, no matter how small the donation, it would be thankfully received. She wished to specially thank the men's secretary, (Mr. E. A. Sanders) for the valuable assistance he had rendered in connec- tion with the ward socials and other functions. He was always courteous, willing and obliging, and the women had apprediated his services' very highly. Glove and Mitten Scheme. When the Grand Duke Michael made an appeal for gloves and mittens for the soldiers, Mr. and Mrs. Crosfield—to whom the Women's Association were indebted for cons,iderable help —very generously provided the wool, and a com- mittee was at once formed of representatives from various local work's; viz. Crosfield's, Alliance Box 'Works, Messrs. Rilands Messrs. Stubs' File Works., Messrs. Rigby and Armitage, the Richmond 'Gas Stove Works, Messrs. T. Locker's, 'Messrs. Hardy's; and the Women's Liberal Association to carry out the arrangements for the making of them in War- rington. When she consented to superintend the distribution of wool and the receiving, sort- ing and packing of the gloves and mittens, she felt that she had undertaken a large order. She was proud to say that the workers con- cerned had made 4,340 pairs of gloves and mittens. The ladies attached to the Bank Quay WOrking Men's Mission had also made 1,000 pairs, so. that Warrington had contribUtell alto- gether 5,340 pairs. She wished to take that opportunity of thanking all those women- who had so nobly responded ,to the call made upon them. At Mr. Crosfield's request the Women Liberals had also had the pleasure of sending a consignment- of mittens to his gallant •brother. Major Crosfield, in France, for distribution amongst his company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Statesmanship in the War.</head>
        <p>Statesmanship in the War.</p>
        <p>In conclusion, Mrs. Osborne said: "we may not .be considered worthy of the vote, but it is surPrising how much our services are appreci- ated—and how willingly women give them—in time of need. Incidentally whilst it is not our intention to enter into, party politics we must all, no matte'', *hat party we belong to, appreciate the almost superhuman efforts which have been put forth by our statesmen. We have always believed that in a great crisis as in normal times, their services would be given unselfishly for the country's cause, Nut, I ven- ture to think that not one of us ever dreamt for a single moment that they could have risen to the heights of true_ statesmanship such as have achieved during this lamentable war. W.O are proud of them, and also of the rank and file who have come .forward so willingly to risk and give their lives for their King and country.</p>
        <p>A TRIBUTE TO THE LADIES. Alderman JAMES BVANS, mi proposing the adop- tion of the report said, amid laughter, that Mrs. Osborne had taken the opportunity of "rubbing it in "—(laughter) —with regard to the franchise for women, but he did not know that he felt the one or two little 'sharp, points aimed at the men so very keenly. Ire must admit that dur- ing the war not only the women, of Warring- ton, but, also, the women Of Britain had none well, though he doubted whether any section of the women of the country had done better than the women of Warrington, who had given freely of their time in helping to make the lot of the, Soldiers more comfortable. (Hear, hear.) Mr. BROWN seconded, remarking that when the victory was won by the British forces and their Allies, as it would be won, the women of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>LOYALTY TO PARTI"MUCE. Mr. C. E. Parker's Presidential Address.</head>
        <p>LOYALTY TO PARTI"MUCE. Mr. C. E. Parker's Presidential Address.</p>
        <p>The newly re-elected PRESIDENT then gave an interesting address on the all-absorbing topic of the day, as it affects the political sphere. It was fitting; he said, at a time of national crisis like the present that party strife should be put aside and that all should unite in. the great national task of -defeating the common enemy, and of re-establishing the peace of Europe, which had been so ruthlessly 'disturbed. lie thought they could congratulate the political parties and political leaders on their loyalty to the political truce. (Hear, hear.) They might rest assured that those who spoke that night would not abuse the occasion and try to make party capital or stir up party strif% They wanted that night to view the situation from the national, not the party, standpoint. It was a disappointment to them; indeed, it was a loss to the nation, that the great scheme for land reform, including the question of better hous- ing, a more equitable distribution of the burden of rating and the like, had for the time being had to be put aside. International affairs now filled the whole field of their vision, and en- gaged all their thought and efforts. It was 'fitting therefore ,on an occasion like that that they should not only consider their duty in regard to the present war, but should also give their best thought to the whole question of national relationships, so that the peace, which would emerge out of that strife, would be settled on righteous principles, and the whole relationships between the nations of the world put on a.more satisfactory basis.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>Re-forming International Relationships.</head>
        <p>Re-forming International Relationships.</p>
        <p>In regard to, international relationships, they had during late decades been gradually emerg- ing from the conception that those should be based upon dynastic struggles, or have for their chief aim the increase of territory and the aggrandisement of the material resources of nations. In their conception of domestic legis- lation they were more advanced. They recog- nised in that sphere that the wellbeing Of the people and the embodiment of their will was the chief aim. But in foreign policy, power, influence and territory had been too much the dbjects, and material force, a powerful army or navy, had been required to attain and to main- tain those. The welfare and the wishes of their own people, and those of other countries, had occupied a very minor place in foreign policy. Only as the civilised nations became more democratised and recognised the principle of the greatest good for the greater number in all lands, would material force become a secondary Matter and exercise dm ever-diminishing influ- ence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>Armed Camp Not the Final Condition.</head>
        <p>The Armed Camp Not the Final Condition.</p>
        <p>As progressives, liberals. could not admit that the picture of an armed camp which Europe had presented during the laht two or three de- cades, was one that could be considered in any way the natural or final condition of civilisa- tion. The democracies of the different nations must be taught that the peaceful regulations of international affairs were in the interests of the whole people-. This could only be brought about by the recognition of the fact that right was the final arbiter and not Krupp's guns or England's Dreadnoughts. (Cheers.) If that conception could be grasped and made effective it would sweep away all secret negotiations,- all private arrangements between the rulers of the different countries—all conceived and arranged either to get some national advantage, or to prevent other countries from getting such ad- vantage, or maybe to establish that vague con- ception termed "balance, of power." Balance of right should take the place of the term bal- ance of power, because balance of power, as the words indicated, related alone to material force.</p>
        <p>Liberalism's Future Aim. That must be the aim of the Liberalism of the future, and he entertained the belief that. that disastrous war would do away for ever with the old feudal and dynastic conceptions based upon force. He had said that .secret diplomacy must for ever pass away. He did not entertain the idea, as held by some, that foreign negotia- tions could be conducted in their entirety and infinite detail by Parliament, but he did. hold that, just as no law •could become operative in their own national affairs, -so in• international affairs no arrangement binding upon this coun- try'should be entered into before it had been consiclered and approved W the representatives of the people in Parliament. They entertained the hope that one of the results of this war would be the creation of a more democratic, Liberalised Europe. (Applause.) • A further advantage they hoped would emerge from the conflict, and one which would be of lasting benefit to the democratic 'party and the nation as a wliole. In spite of the fables and falsehpods which contaminated the news they got of foreign cOuntries,• they ought, at home, to be able to appropriate what was true; and by doing so get a. wider knowledge of foreign coun- tries and their peoples. That should specially apply to the many now risking, or about to risk, their lives in Prance. They had a special opportunity of gettifig to know their brethren and fellow workers of other lands, and of be- coming conversant with their language, habits, industries, occupations and lives. Such per- sonal associations by the British soldiers, repre- senting, as they did, all classes, would- have a wonderful effect upon them, and through them upon the nation to which they belonged, in- creasing the people's knowledge and "widening their outlook. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>A Different England Arzer the War. England after this war would be . a very different one from whatirit. was before, and in the realm of knowledge and experience that change would be very marked. One significant effect"of closer assbciation might be already noted. The spirit of revengeful hate which was so prominent in Germany. and Which was not altogether absent in England was not found to any appreciable extent amongst the com- batants themselves. (Hear, hear.) He had</p>
        <p>found more expressions of that . insane hate amongst certain elderly ladies who knitted soldiers' socks in drawing-rooms at home, than he had found amongst the men who were in the death-struggle in the trenches, the men who had come face to face with the German foe And surely that was natural, for in spite of the excesses which had dishonoured Germany, combatants on both sides had acknowledged that their foes were brave men, and that the human virtues of honour, mercy and kindliness were, after all, more real and more enduring qualities in the human race than hatred,and revenge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>Sin Against Belgium.</head>
        <p>The Sin Against Belgium.</p>
        <p>"So much on general• grounds" said Mr. Parker, "now I want to get a little closer to some of the actual conditions in connection with the war. Perhaps of all the events which had occurred during the last nine months, the devastation of Belgium, and the barbarism of the Germans in that devastation, had .most startled the world. It was possible that with- out any violation of the Belgian treaty,---without that Special challenge to England's &amp;flour— that Great Britain would have been drawn into the 'European conflagration. But it was the violation of that treaty, and, perhaps more, the cruelty to an innocent:people, which made our participations inevitable—(applause)—and had supplied the moral enthusiasm and the spiritual sacrifices which this country had made, and would continue to make, until the great wrong to the people of Belgium, as far as circum- stances permitted, had been repaired. (Ap- plause.) Mr. Parker went on to read a number of engrossing extracts from a book entitled "A Surgeon in Belgium," which was written by a. friend of his, Dr. Souttar, and which he has promised shortly to review in our columns.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>HONOURING A BELGIAN.</head>
        <p>HONOURING A BELGIAN.</p>
        <p>Refugee Guest Elected To Honorary Membership.</p>
        <p>The President next called the attention of the meeting to the fact that it had in its midst two guests from gallant little Belgium—M. A. Puls, a schoolmaaer of Malines, and his wife, who</p>
        <p>had been driven to take refuge in England, and were now being sheltered at Hall Nook. The presence of Mons. and Madame Puls led him on to ask those present to put to themselves the question ,whether they felt that the people of England were doing all they could in the matter of helping the Belgians, who had lost so, much in this dire war. He did not think that War- rington had done all it could or ought to have done' in regard to these distressed people. With all respect, he did not feel that the central cinn- intttee, who were supposed to carry on the work of succouring the, refugees, had gone the .proper way about their task. He would like to put it to those present that, apart from the hostels which had been provided, they ought, in a town like Warrington, to find a number of people who were willing to receive one or two Belgians into their homes, if not as guests, free of charge, at least on terms that could be arranged. The committee would like people to come forward and say " would be willing to take—a woman and a child, for instance, if I could get enough to pay out-of-pocket expenses-7s. or Bs. a week, and I should be quite willing to do my part in regard to other things if the woman would be prepared to help in the housework."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>Will Anybody Offer Asylum?</head>
        <p>Will Anybody Offer Asylum?</p>
        <p>If there were any present who felt that they would like to assist, and they would communi- cate with him, he would put 'them in the way of doing something practical to heal the wounds of Belgium.</p>
        <p>Mr. -Parker then invited the meeting to pay M. Puls the compliment of making him an honorary member of the Liberal Association, remarking that he knew that M. Pills was deeply interested in all good and progressive work, as. well as in scholastic work, which he had, prior to the War, carried on in a school somewhat similar in size and character to that so ably presided over by Mr. J. Upson in War- rington. He proposed that M. Puls be elected an honorary member Of the Association. (Ap- plause.) Mr. A. H. CROSFIELD seconded. Alderman Tisxxox, in supporting, remarked that he was sure that M. Puls' asSociation with the Liberal Association would result in a broadening of their views with regard to those whom they ..used :to term foreigners, but, now, were very proud to call their friends. (Ap- plause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>BELGIAN SCHOOLMASTER'S THANKS.</head>
        <p>BELGIAN SCHOOLMASTER'S THANKS.</p>
        <p>The proposition having been put and carried with acclamation,</p>
        <p>M. Prms expressed his thanks in French, which was translated for the benefit of the audience by Mr. A. IL Crosfield. • Monsieur began by expressing the thanks of his wife and Willson for the invitation they had received to attend that meeting and for :the kindly welcome that had •been accorded them. He would not fail, when he. returned to his own country, to make the Belgian `Liberals acquainted with the honour that the Warring- ton Liberals had done for him. This courtesy which had been show him 'would render his stay in the Warrin n gton district the more profit- able and interesting to him, and his wife joined with him in his thanks to' the Association for their kindly reception. He had already had the honour of being allowed to visit the National Liberal Club in London, and he had been struck on the occasion of that visit by the interesting. collection of portraits and statues of past and present leaders of the party which graced the walls and halls of that building, to whose capacity he desired to pay a warm tribute.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>Eternal Principles of Liberalism.</head>
        <p>The Eternal Principles of Liberalism.</p>
        <p>The principles of Liberalism, M. Puls added, were not only universal in their application, they were not only principles postulating indi- vidual liberty and individual justice, hut they were principles that were eternal. (Applause. M. Pule also expressed his thanks for the tri, butes that had been paid to the courage, the energy, and the devotion of the men of Belgium, who, by resisting "the Barbarians" as they entered their country, had so materially helped. in fighting Europe's battle. (Applause.). Both Madame. Puls and himself greatly appreciated the feeling shown in England towards the King of the Belgians, who had worked just, as hard as had his soldiers; indeed, he had worked as if he had been a simple soldier himself, putting his shoulder to the wheel and taking a part alongside the humblest of his people in fight- ing the common foe. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>WOMEN'S INVITATION TO BELGIAN LADY.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S INVITATION TO BELGIAN LADY.</p>
        <p>Silaking on behalf of the Women's Liberal Association, Mrs. OSBORNE said that the Women Liberals of the town would feel honoured at welcoming Madame Pula at their gatherings at any time. -(Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>REPARATION. Mr. Crosfield Says Belgium Must</head>
        <p>REPARATION. Mr. Crosfield Says Belgium Must</p>
        <p>Be Restored.</p>
        <p>GERMANY'S GUILT.</p>
        <p>Mr/. Crosfield, who was received with applause. assured their guest that when the present. terrible Armageddon was concluded, when the Allies had conquered in the struggle, which be- yond comparison or parallel, was by far the most gigantic war ever waged in the history of the world, when that time came our first thought would be for Belgium. (Loud aw plause.) The Prime Minister said a true worli when he described- the war 'now raging as the nation's war, in which all men and women could, as happily the vast majority of them were doing, play a part, whether .at home or ,across the water. Just as this War, to quote the Prime Minister's . own language; was none of our seeking, so assuredly it was being -fought Out, and fought to the finish, by our countrymen in no grudging or selfish spirit, and therefore, when at last the mighty task was accomplished, and the ascend- ancy of Prussian ,Militarism and brutality crushed, and crushed for ever, our first duty, as it was our chief desire, would be sternly to insist on Germany making such restitution.and reparation as was possible and practicable to Belgium for the havoc, misery and horror. for which she was responsible in that country. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>The Haunted Kaiser.</p>
        <p>The Kaiser might well feel uneasy, even if he were not still capable of feeling some degree of shame at that awful spectacle. Next to his apprehension and his dread of the fearful retribution in store for his own eouptry, the dire records for which his soldiery had made themselves responsible in Belgium and in</p>
        <p>Fiance probably most haunted his waking moments. And so already they saw him begin- ning to twist and writhe in the efforts he was making to shift that crushing weight of responsibility; in the explanations which he got others to make on his behalf. Upon the whole, that struck- him (the speaker) as one of the most encouraging symptoms and signs of the times.</p>
        <p>Of course, tlie Kaiser still whistled to keep up his courage, and, of course, Herr Bethmann- Holweg and Herr Bailin did their best to help him, but how changed was their tune since the beginning of the war. Then it was a loud, triumphant major, but now already they were whistling in a minor key, and it was a very forced note indeed at that! And well it might be, because facts were sometimes hard nuts to crack, and the Kaiser had got hold of some of the hardest. When they had facts verified by such circumstantial evidence as was to be found in the Belgian Grey Book, in the Russian Orange Book, in the British Government's White Papers, and above all, in that memorable, intensely interesting and historic document, the. French Yellow Book, well, then he thought, they - need not be surprised if the nut proved . hard enough to break the Kaiser's teeth. (Laughter.) In the latest comnpnication made on his behalf by Herr Bailin, there was a wretchedly far-fetched attempt to saddle Sir Edward Grey with the responsibility for the war. As if the whole world were not well aware that, during the brief ten days at his disposal, Sir Edward Grey worked with might and main—with all the energy, tact, prudence and Moderation, as well as the personal influence, authority and per- suasive charm which he possessed—to save Eng- land, Europe and the world from that measure- less calamity. The reason • for his failure was to, be found in Germany—(applause)—and - no- where else, and the Prime Minister re-stated that terrible indictment in his speech the pre- vious night. Herr Bailin, it was true, at' the Kaiser's suggestion, would fain shift the responsibility to Austria, although it was one of the established facts of history that at the eleventh hour Austria did show her willingness to listen to reason and come to terms. The evidence of. the British Ambassador at Vienna and of ,Count Mensdorf, the Austrian Ambassador in London, was indisputable. But it was just then, when the international situation began to look more hopeful, that the Kaiser chose to force the pace and to issue ultimatums to Russia and to France of such a character as to admit of only one answer. ff in this world the consequences of his crime were the measure of the guilt of the criminal and 'of the punishment to be in- flicted upon him, what would be the prospect for the Kaiser at this moment; nay, what were his prospects when he came to he arraigned and tried at the bar of hilltory? The wretched make- believes of these days would not serve him then.</p>
        <p>Yellow Press Mud Throwers. While the Kaiser had been trying to shift the responsibility on to the shoulders of Austnia, and while he had been attributing shortcomings to Sir Edward Grey, others a little nearer home had been attacking, not so much Sir Edward Grey, as the Government, collectively, for lack of judgment and lack of foresight in the con- duct of the war. In these days party politics were supposed to be in abeyance. It was under- stood that, subject to fair and honest criticism, the Press, like the nation, would unite in snp- port of the existing Government, and would strengthen its hands in order to bring the war to a speedy and successful conclusion. All he could say was that if that was the general understanding, some of the language of the Yellow Press seemed strangely inconsistent with it. Therefore, reading that morning the admirable, statesmanlike and dignified address delivered the previous day by the Prime Minis- ter in Newcastle-on-Tyne, lie rejoiced to see the lit given to those wretched Yellow "rags" which sought to disgrace and debase English journalism, and which vainly, but none the less venomously, strove to injure the Government by their scurrilous attacks. If the efforts of those mud-throwers . had excited righteous in- dignation, lie thought they might, as loyalists 'end Liberals feel a good deal of legitimate satisfaction as they saw the false coin the mud- throwers had sought to put into circulation so effectually nailed to the counter. Passing by that inferior journalism, with the contempt which it deserved, be proposed to refer to. other criticism, .which came from more re- putable quarters.</p>
        <p>WOULD CONSCRIPTION HAVE BEEN _ BETTER? _</p>
        <p>, He thought the criticism to which he alluded was legitimate, in so far as it did not infringe upon party politics. He referred to what one saw widely discussed in the Press of the country, as well as by the man in the street and the young lady in the parlour, viz., the question as to whether this country could still save the situation and more especially whether she could not originally have saved the 'situation if she had adopted an all-round scheme or conscrip- tion. Since he had been in politics there was no subject concerning which more pressure had been put upon him—partly by political oppo- nents, and partly by those who had been his political friends and supporters.. He wanted to take that opportunity of putting a fe* salient considerations before people who were really misled by a great deal of tolerably super- fitial discussion of that .question; the question as to whether the country was or was not to be saved by. a measure of compulsory military ser- vice.</p>
        <p>A Dangerous Transition Stage. The first thing he would point' out was that if they had listened to the propaganda of recent years and had attempted to change the military system of the country from the volun- tary basis on which it rested at present, in order to adopt a compulsory system, they would im- mediately have been face to face with a most dangerous period of transition, for they could not suddenly jump from one system to another by a single stroke of the pen. It took time to effect a change like that, and, in his own mind, he had not the faintest doubt that, judging from what they knew of international polities, and what had been revealed to them of the frame of mind of Germany;the very first thing that would have happened during that period of transition would have been that Germany Wpuld have found a convenient pretext for starting the war when this country was a good deal weaker than it was found to be when the Teutonic enemy did launA her thunderbolts at the beginning of last Augnst. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>They knew what the supporters of compulsory service were constantly saving; they had heard it thousands of times. "Think," they said, " of the thousands and thousands of lives that would have been saved to-day if you had gone in for a compulsory system at the proper time; think of the fact, above all others, that this country would then have been able to prevent Germany from ever getting into Belgium !" There was not a word of truth in all that. Close as She was at hand, even France could not get her conscriptionist forces into Belgium in time to stop Germany coming in; whilst all rurope thought that Britain had accomplished an almost super- human task when she transferred her Expedi- tionary Force on to the Continent in so short a space of time as she did. No time was wasted in' getting that force across, hut, quick as the English soldiers were, they did not arrive in anything like time to prevent Germany from getting into Belgium. - When that fact was pointed out to the supporters of compulsory ser- vice, they immediately fell back on to a second proposition, and said: "Oh, then, think .of France, and what you could have saved her!" [fetter Men Than Compulsion Would Provide. "Personally, be doubted whether under a pulsory system they could ever have sent out men, man for man, of the calibre and capacity of the troops that had gone and were still going from this country to help to win this :War. of freedom. The Prime -Alinigter the previous evening referred . not only, to the number of troops that were voing, but also to the quality of the men. (Hear, hear.) , He (the speaker) was talking a few days ago to a Colonel who was training a battalion at a place they were all familiar with in the North of England. Included in that battalion were all sorts and conditions of men—archi- tects, chemists, lawyers' clerks, skilled artisans and labourers. He discussed with the Colonel the question of the probable duration of the war, and the Colonel said: "I am bound to say I take a long view of it," and then added, not in a 'spirit of levity, but in all sincerity, "but perhaps the wish is father to the thought. Iniu know, the men I am training would be so terribly disappointed if they thought that they were not going to get their turn and their chance to strike a blow for the Empire. He had talked to soldiers up and down the country, and it was his experience that the spirit shown by those who. had voluntarily come forward to offer their services to the country was a spirit</p>
        <p>that they would never have got under a com- pulsory sykeni. (Hear, hear, and applause.) The Colonel he had referred to was a man of great ability and capacity, and he -(the speaker) put to him the question, well knowing what the answer would be—for he had put the question to other officers before, and had only received one answer—" Do you find that your men are picking up their duties pretty fast, and are proving capable pupils?" His answer was the answer that one always met 'with . where English volunteers were concerned.. "The Tate at which they 'are learning and acquiring a complete command of the whole of their work," said the Colonel, "is simply marvellous." (Loud applause.) .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>Not a Party Question.</head>
        <p>Not a Party Question.</p>
        <p>Compulsory service had much to be said for it, but there was a great deal more to be said for the voluntary system, in favour of which the arguments were many, and concerned not only the Army, but also the Navy. He hoped at an early date to have the opportunity of dis- cussing in Warrington other aspects of that question, and he purposed, on some such future occasion, to quote from the speeches of eminent Conservatives, as well as eminent Liberals; and also from the speeches of illus- trious Generals, in order to show how divided was opinion on this question. It was a groat mistake to regard that as being practically party question; a question concerning which the whole mass of the Conservatives were of one way of thinking, and the whole mass of Liberals of another. That was not correct. It was quite true that the bulk of the Conservatives 'did believe in a compulsory system, and that the bulk of the Liberals did not believe in it. But when they looked into the question, they would he struck by the fact, that while on the ,one hand the number of Liberals who supported any schelne of conscription, Was but small, the Con- servatives who opposed it included some of the most distinguished members of the Opposition. (Hear, hear.) He did not refer to this question in order to start any controversy with anyone now serving at the Front, in fact that was the last ' thing he desired. What he said was in reference to the rather aggressive remarks on the platform and elsewhere in their neighbour- hood by those who were at home. It was for that reason he had felt it his duty to make these few remarks on a question of the highest im- portance. (Hear, hear.) In conclusion, Mr. Cresfield congratulated the association upon an admirable year's work in face of the great difficulties that had beset them. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>OVERSTAYED LEAVE.</head>
        <p>OVERSTAYED LEAVE.</p>
        <p>Absentee Soldiers Before Th 6</p>
        <p>Magistrates.</p>
        <p>Before the Borough Magistrates on Monday, Jno. Mitchell was charged with having been an absentee from the 6th Battalion of the South Lancashire Regiment since April 13th.—The evidence was that a constable saw the prisoner in a drunken condition in Bridge-street and asked hi&amp; if he had a pass. The soldier replied in the affirmative, and produced a pass, but the constable found that the date thereon had been tampered with and that the man was four days overdue; he should have been back on Tuesday of the previous week.—lle was ordered to handed over to an escort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>" FAKED " AN OLD PASS.</head>
        <p>" FAKED " AN OLD PASS.</p>
        <p>James Barnes was charged at the same Court with being an absentee from the 3rd Battalion of the South Lancashires since the 17th inst.—A 'police officer said that at 12.45 a.m. on Sunday he heard a noise at the back of an outhouse, and upon going to investigate, found the prisoner with a. quart bottle of beer in his hand. He was under the influence of drink, but when asked whether he was on leave he promptly re- plied "Yes," but was unable to produce a pass. When told that he must go to the Police Station he refused, and when the officer took him int( custody he aimed a ki'ck at him, but missed. With assistance he was got to the lock-up ail( it was ttere found that he had a pass in pocket, but the date had been altered from 16th to 18th.—Prisoner told the Court that he intended getting back for the Sunday morning. but his arrest prevented him doing so. Ho alleged the police officer pulled his belt off and "knocked him about" with it, the result being that he "had to act in self-defence."—Superin- tendent Williamson said there was no question of self-defence about it. The. prisoner had been there repeatedly for assaulting the police. It had been ascertained that he came to Warring- ton without a pass at all. He had, however, an old 'pass by him,- which Shad been granted him for the purpose of visiting Liverpool, and he altered the date cT this.—Prisoner was re- manded to the cells pending the arrival of an escoit.</p>
        <p>1. is</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>JUSTIFIED CHALLENGE.</head>
        <p>JUSTIFIED CHALLENGE.</p>
        <p>Private Thomas Farrell, of the 6th Br attalion South Lancashires (now training in this county), was brought before Thursday's Police Court for being absent from his regiment since April 15th.—Acting-Sergeant Hayes had chal- lenged him in Sankey-street the previous day and found he had no pass.—The defaulter was ordered to be handed over to an escort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>ABSENTEE BROTHERS.</head>
        <p>ABSENTEE BROTHERS.</p>
        <p>Privates Edward and George Bushell were separately charged at Thursday's Police Court with having been absent from the 3rd Reserve Cavalry (in the South) since the 17th and 20th April respectively.—P.C. E. Taylor described his visits to Pierpoint-street the previous night, where he found the prisoners, both of whose passes had expired.—The men were ordered to he handed over 0 an escort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <p>. 15,4.4 - - -,--'` "1"4.14%.'1'L---1...- f", Ze':"--,-- 'l'r , 2:: ..,ii ..:if Ql-...- , .1; ' -ipos=trt CV't OP f. ~</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL</p>
        <p>Cost Of Edueatioan In Cheshire.</p>
        <p>oNsT VVFLY INCREASING DEMANDS.</p>
        <p>At their annual meeting on Tuesday, the Runcorn Rural District Council elected a fresh chairman, in Mr. Thomas Antwiss, who was proposed by the retiring chairman, Mr. C. E. Linaker, sen. The latter, who has served for three years in succession, was nominated, and his name was seconded, but the Council were not afforded the chance of expressing the appreciation universally felt for the admirable rammer in which the meetings have been con- ducted by Mr. Linaker for the past three years by re-electing him for the fourth consecutive year, for he declined to stand, preferring to see the honour of holding office go round. The new chairman has had experience in the vice7chair. Mr. W. Harrison was elected viee-chairman. FROM FOURPENCE TO ONE-AND FOURPENCE. The Hale Urban District Council wrote stat- ing that They had recently had under considera- tion the cost of education in the County of Chester, and were alarmed at the constantly increasing demands of the County Council for the purposes of education. The cost of educa- tion was included and collected in the poor rate, and in that district alone the poor rate had more than doubled itself during the last ten or eleven years, and a prospective increase was threatened. The increase was due principally to the increase in the cost of education, which, in 1903, stood at fourpence in the pound, and was now one-and-fourpente. Every ratepayer was not an fait with the principles of rating. and some were inclined to cast reflection on the District Council when called upon to pay more rates than previously. The gradual increase in the poor rates hampered -Urban and Rural Coun- cils in the execution of their duties under the Public Health Acts, and important works had from time to time been deferred for the reason that the Councils did not care to increase the genesal district rate. The Council suggested some united action should be taken in making representations to the County Council in the matter.</p>
        <p>No action was taken in the matter. VOTE OF THANKS TO EX-CHAIRMAN. Mr. Wright and Mr. Mclntyre proposed .and seconded, respectively, a vote of tthanks to Mr. Linaker, sen., for "the• thoroughly business- like and impartial way" in which he had con- ducted th'e business during the past year. Mr. Linaker, in reply, said that he had felt that he had more public work on his hands than he ought, to have, in fairness to himself, and in relinquishing office as chairman of that Council Ile was relieving himself of a certain amount of work and responsibility. He was glad to see one in the chair who had carried out the duties of vice-chairman so well for three years.</p>
        <p>He Did His Best. Mother: "Gracious, Willie! Where did you get that black eye?" . Willie: ".Johnny Smith hit me with his fist." Mother: "Well, I hope you remembered what your Sunday school teacher said about 'leaping coals of fire on the heads of our enemies?" Willie: "Oh, I didn't have no coals, so I just stuck his, head in the ash can!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Indigestion</head>
        <p>Indigestion</p>
        <p>NerVeCollapse Trained Nurse's Striking Testimony to the All- ''sh Remedy, Dr. Cassell's Tablets.</p>
        <p>Here is a professional nurse recommending Dr. Cassell's Tablets. This lady is Mrs. Ranson, of Green-street Farm, Green- street, Ingatestone, Essex. She says: "Some three years ago I was suffering with a severe nervous at- tack. I was a certificated nurse at the time, un- married, and I have no ,sex. doubt that the attack was brought on by the strain of heavy work. I had to leave off. I was so ex- tremely nervous•that a sound Would make me Jump. *My digestion, too, was upset; I had no appetite, and suffered much from flatulence and headaches. Medical treatment did not seem to suit my case, but when I took Dr. Cassell's Tablets I got better daily. They enabled me to resume work, and; from that time I have had good health." Dr. Cassell's</p>
        <p>Briti • se, .3lrs Ranson, Rs.</p>
      </div>
