      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Vital Statistics.</head>
        <p>Vital Statistics.</p>
        <p>During the week ended April 17th, 53 births and 23 deaths were registered in Warrington, as against 47 and 17 respectively last year. There Were 83 scarlet fever patients under treatment at the Borough Hospital on Saturday of last week.</p>
        <p>Padgate Cottage Homes' Roll of Honour. No fewer than 25 ex-pupils of the Cottage Homes, Padgate, have joined the Army and Navy. One has been killed in France, one went down with the Monmouth, and another is 3 prisoner of war. Six of them are serving in Judie,</p>
        <p>BIRTHS, MARRIAGES. DEATHS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>OUR FRIENDS THE CRITICS.</head>
        <p>OUR FRIENDS THE CRITICS.</p>
        <p>The Liberal Government has received, since August, as it had a right to expect to receive, the support of every patriotic heart that beats in these islands or in any part of the great Empire of which these island§ are the centre. But there are some people who cannot sur- vive for any long period unless they have a go for the wicked Radicals. The disease is a national and a local one; fortunately, it is so easily detected by its special kind of venom that it does not stand much chance of doing , harm before it is rtzthlessly exposed and up- rooted. It is a scandal that ivhile such a body of men as Mr. Aseurru and those who are literally slaving to win this war and to maintain all that is dear to us in the way of tradition, and right and freedom, there should be found crews of wreckers whose only aim is to throw discredit upon the administration, not because it has failed, but apparently because it is normally of a different political complexion. We do not intrd for one moment to enter into recriniination; we wish to. submit ourselves whole-heartedly to the political truce which has been set up. We would rather leave these misguided and mis- chievous people to be dealt with by the saner and more patriotic men in their own party, and to await the criticism—which we shall be prepared to meet—at the proper moment of those whose broad-minded conduct now will have given them the right to speak with authority. When the war clouds have rolled away, there will be small room for those wife have been slandering either in the Press or the platform in the minds of decent, men of either party. But there are some points that have been raised that ought to be carefully considered now, for fear the evil things said should be taken for truth. The Government has been accused, for instance, of having been lax in its duty; the Government ought to have foreseen everything that has happened and made arrangements accordingly. Well, Liberals do not lay claim to be omniscient, but ASQUITH, CHURCHILL, HALDANE, LLOYD GEORGE, and GREY have, at least, done some- thing towards meeting the most tremendous contingencies that have arisen in the history of this country. GENERAL ZURLINDEN, in the Paris " Gaulois," discussing the armies of the Allies, referred in the following way to the British Army: "Whilst giving its splendid support in all the opening engagements of the war, on the Marne, on the Aisne, • and in Flanders, the British Army has increased ten- fold." ,The Army in France is now three- quarters of a million strong, and there are untold hundreds of thousands ready to follow. Loan i KrTCHENER is satisfied with what has taken place, and hopes to be satisfied with ihat has to come. As much ammunition was expended at Neuve Chapelle as during the whole Boor War, but we have been able to supply not only (nu- own wants, but help out</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY. APRIL 24, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY. APRIL 24, 1915.</p>
        <p>those of the Allies. Then, ask the critics, what is the use of saying we want more men, and that we have been checked for want nf ammunition? It sounds like an echo from a debating society room or a budding mutual improvement debating class. We are at war, and are striving for the best. We want more men, and although we have organised our resources so that ammunition has been suffi- cient, the needs of the future will grow, and we cannot afford to rest for a moment on our oars.</p>
        <p>A typical sample of the arguments that are being produced to regale a nation that is fighting for its life is that if we are doing so well, drink tannot have done the harm that has been charged against it. These gentry also allege that it was the teetotalers that have asked for restrictions on the Trade. As a matter of fact, the ship builders who asked for prohibition specifically said they were not abstainers, and the figures they placed before the CHANCELLOR showed that</p>
        <p>36 per cent. of the men worked, including overtime, less than 40 hours a week during March, and that the total working hours of practically one-half of the men were less than 45 hours a week. The ordinary working hours of peace times are 53 or 54. The aggregate amount of ordinary hours avoidably lost dur- ing four weeks of March was 668,000 hours, or 25 per cent. The employers attributed this loss, in great part, to drinking habits, directly or indirectly.</p>
        <p>If we are doing well, how much better ought we to do by getting rid of this criminal waste! Much of the petty cavilling that is being indulged in is capable of similar ex- posure, but we can all reserve our thoughts for the consideration of higher and infinitely more important purp6ses than looking for holes in a political opponent's coat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>MR. CROSFIELD AND GREECE.</head>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD AND GREECE.</p>
        <p>The letter which we published in "The Examiner" last week from the pen of Mr. A. H. CROSFIELD, called upon Greece to rally to Venizelos, her greatest citizen statesman and leader, and so "make it clear to a military and Court clique that what is a limited mon- archy and constitutional government in name shall be made so in fact." This letter also appeared in "The Westminster Gazette," and has been commented upon most favour- ably in very influential quarters. Mr. CROSFIELD has made a deep study of the war and its causes, as a perusal of his speeches recently delivered before the Junior Liberals would clearly show. The way he marshalled his facts and the obvious authority with whioh he spoke greatly pleased his audience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>LIVELIHOOD AND POVERTY.</head>
        <p>LIVELIHOOD AND POVERTY.</p>
        <p>We have made some inquiries on one or two points which were not quite clear in the book "Livelihood and Poverty,',' td which we have , preViously referred. Dr. BOWLEY informs us that in applying the New Standard to dis- cover what proportion of the population is above and what below the poverty line, the actual rent paid by each family has first been,deducted. This is because it is assumed that a poor man will pay no more in rent than is actually necessary. 'The deduction for fuel was is. 3d. per household. This will be seen to be a very moderate allowance. Our own estimate would be, for a- minimum of 2 cwt. per week per household, and at present prices this would mean about 2s. 2d. or 2s. 3d. At the time the inquiry ivas held, i.e., Septem- ber, 1914, possibly 2s. 2d. would have covered the cost. Perhaps some of our readers would give us their opinion as to the minimum re- quirements in coal per week all the year round. This does not mean what is the amount actually consumed each week in working class houses, for 2 cwt. would very often be quite insufficient. Would some of our, readers with first-hand experiente send us postcards saying what is the smallest amount of fuel that a family can manage with each week when a single fire is being used, 'assuming that no gas cooking takes the place of an open fire? Facts like, these are of great value to any serious social student. It would be interesting to know whether the consump- tion of fuel is much reduced in summer time. Unquestionably it is So reduced in middle class homes, where a second or thirefire is used. For the moment our concern is with the homes of the general labourer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>POOR CHILDREN'S HOLIDAY.</head>
        <p>POOR CHILDREN'S HOLIDAY.</p>
        <p>It' has been one of the most satisfactory features of the critical times through which we have been passing since the outbreak of the war that deserving charities have not been unduly depressed through the diversion of donations from them to the war funds. War funds have flourished, but not, to a large extent, at the expense of-other objects. True effort and true charity consists in giving in accordance with the needs of the situation, not in saying: "I will give. so much and not go beyond that point, however much the amount has to be divided and sub-divided." The real philanthropist and the real patriot— and it is a pleasure to feel that we have many genuine specimens in our midst—is the man or woman who realises that the present is an</p>
        <p>abnormal situation which must be met liber- ally and unreservedly—and even self-sacrific- ingly. The poor children's summer camp is one of those things which we are sure will not be allowed to languish even for one season, because of a mad Kaiser's war. At the annual meeting of subscribers held last Friday night and reported by us last Satur- day, it was stated that there was a small adverse balance at the bank, and an appeal was made for help to liquidate it and also to build up the funds for future needs. Between 500 and 600 poor, but deserving, boys and girls, doomed never to see more of the glory of Nature than can be viewed in the cramped and slummy portions of our town—unless some kindly agency comes to their rescue— are annually taken to Prestatyn in the sum- mer holidays to have their bodies strength- ened, and, what is of more value to the com- munity, to have their minds broadened. Last summer 402 children had visited the camp when the war intervened, ant the interrup- tions in the train service cut short a season that would have been a record. Mr. Edward WALLINGTON gave many reasons why every, effort should be made to keep the camp up to its high level of efficiency and useful- ness, reasons which, we hope, will be taken to heart.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>New Fire Chief.</head>
        <p>New Fire Chief.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. H. Turner's probationary period as; superintendent of the Fire Brigade having ex- pired, the Council have agreed, on the recom- mendation of the Fire Engine Ccmmittee, to appoint him superintendent of the brigade at an increased salary of £135 per annum, with house, coal, gas„c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>ATTENTION</head>
        <p>ATTENTION</p>
        <p>Answer To Your Name, Young</p>
        <p>Man I</p>
        <p>XING AND COUNTRY NEED YOU.</p>
        <p>Patriotic Scenes At Open-air Recruiting Meeting.</p>
        <p>"Why don't they let me go; I'm only fifty, and I had twenty-one years in the Army, and I'm good for plenty of service yet?" This was the question addressed to Colonel Moggridge by an erect, keen-eyed, but white- headed man at an open-air recruiting meeting held at the Park Gates on Thursday evening. The questioner was importunate and repeated his query whilst the crowd cheered 1 evident earneOness, especially when he backed it up by adding that if " they " wouldn't let him go to the Front he was at any rate just off to do a "bit" for his country, for he was engaged on a night shift helping in the construction of Colonel Moggridge paused in his address to congratulate the interrupter on his grit, "That's the kind of spirit we want to see in the younger men!" he said. "Aye, sir!" replied the white-haired one, bringing his heels together with a click and saluting, "I am as willing as the next young man, and if you'll take me you have only to say the word. I've done one 'bit '—in years gone— and I'm ready for another and, what's more, I know how to handle a gun, big or little! Why should men like me be turned away from the barracks like dogs?"- "No, no! not like dogs," protested the Colonel, "but you aTe in the same boat as sothe of the rest of us—getting a little bit too white on top for fighting. Besides, you are doing good and useful work as it is!"</p>
        <p>"Aye, sir," continued the man, "and if they won't let me help over there I'm going to put all I can into my job at home, but it is hard to be refused when one wants to do some real work for the old country." Captain Sparrow, of Birkenhead, himself a veteran, later on, took a hand in the dialogue. "You and I have the same grievance old friend" he said. "They told me I was too old</p>
        <p>for the trenches, too! but I have found some- thing I can do, and something that you can do, too; in addition to helping to provide the munitions of war, and that is, if they won't let you go yourself, to persuade younger men, who ought to do so, to go! There's a task you can take a hand in, old friend!" (Cheers.)</p>
        <p>"And, I will, sir!" the Captain's "old friend" responded, saluting with one hand and grasping with the other the hand held out to him t shake.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>SAME CRY-MORE MEN AND STILL</head>
        <p>THE SAME CRY-MORE MEN AND STILL</p>
        <p>MORE!</p>
        <p>The meeting was presided over by Alderman I Tinnion, in the absence of the Mayor, who, it was announced, was suffering ,from a cold. The ALDERMAN explained the object of the meeting—to get "more men and still more men!" It was the duty of every able-bodied young man to offer himself; it was the duty of every ,patriot, because this was no war of aggrandisement, but was a war of self-preserva- tion—a war to save the greatest, the most mag- nificent Empire the world had ever known. The married men of Warrington had done splen- didly—nobly! In their hundreds, they had given up their work, they had left their wives and families and they had gone forth in answer to the country's call. The married men had done all that, whilst there were yet plenty of eligible single men who evaded their duty, who shirked their responsibility in this matter, and whose services were required in order to bring the terrible struggle to the earliest poSsible and most glorious conclusion. There were any num- ber of men between the ages of 19 and 38 who ought to go, and if they did not come forward of 'themselves he appealed to their mothers, their sisters and their sweethearts to send them. If the world were not soon- rid of its greatest curse, of the most terrible military tyranny , that had ever been imposed on mankind, whose fault would it be? It would be the fault t.f those men who had not stiffened their backs and given themselves; it would be the fault of those who had not put their:shoulders to the wheel. If the Germans were allowed to win England would not be worth living in. He was too old to go and fight, but he would rather die than live for five minutes under Teutonic rule. (Applause.) Unless the young men were pre- pared to make sacrifices to-day, they might be compelled to make even greater sacrifices to- morrow. (Hear, hear.) "Young men," con- cluded the Alderman, "we don't want to lose you, but we think you ought to-go!" (Cheers.)</p>
        <p>WHAT ARE SOME YOUNG MEN DOING? In the course of his speech, Colonel MOGGRIDGE said he could not under- stand how it was that so many young men, who professed to love their country, and who had friends fighting at the Front, could be content to stay behind doing nothing. (Hear, hear.) The only reason he could conclude was that they thought that they were not wanted; but that was quite an erroneous idea. It was no use their shielding themselves behind the idea tlkat Germany was already beaten, for that was qdite an erroneous idea. A nation that had been pre- paring scientifically for war for twenty-five years and more was not so easily beaten as all that; indeed, the truth was that Germany was still very, very far from beaten. "Young men," said the Colonel, in ringing tones, "You have got a duty to do Why don't you go mid do it? Warrington may have done a bit," but :It has not done the whole of its " bit " so long as there remains behind one single young man who is fit to go. (Hear, hear.) Why are you wait- ing? Is it that ou are waiting until you are fetched? Come, young men; your friends at the Front can't understand your absence !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>"COME WHERE GLORY WAITS!"</head>
        <p>"COME WHERE GLORY WAITS!"</p>
        <p>Mr. dosnm ELWELI„ who was introduced to the audience as a Trade trnionist and as one who, had done as mu&amp; as anybody for the uplifting of labour in Warrington, next addressed the gathering, at length, recapitulating the history of Germany's military and naval aggression, and making a forcible appeal to the young men who yet wavered. They were asked to come and fight in a glorious cause; to fight for the freedom of which they sang, to fight in order that "Britons never, never shall be slaves!" (Cheers.) They were not only asked to fight for their own freedom, but also for the freedom of their children and of their children's children;</p>
        <p>to fight in order that they might be able to hand down untarnished and' unsullied, to posterity, the glorious heritage which they had themselves received. They were engaged in the greateSt war the world had ever known, and every man of fighting age must prepare himself for going into the field should his services be required. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>UNLESS WE LICK GERMANY-</head>
        <p>UNLESS WE LICK GERMANY-</p>
        <p>A breezy speech by Captain SPARROW, who was accorded a rous- ing reception followed. It was, he said, up to every single man to go and help to " lick " Germany, and it was not on English soil that the "licking" had got to be done, for if they wished to spaie English women and children the outrages that had been inflicted on the hapless Belgians it was on Continental soil that the hiding had to be administered. (Hear, hear.) The Germans used to say that the Belgians were their best friends. Well, all the world now knew how Germany treated her best friends. The English they had always regarded as any- thing but their best friends; therefore if the "best friends" were subjected to outrages too horrible to describe, what sort of treatment did they think would be meted out by the German to people whom they never pretended to regard as over-close friends?</p>
        <p>"Do you take women, sir?" cried a shrill female voice. "Because, if you do, I'll be the first!"</p>
        <p>_ "Good for you!" said the Captain, "but it's men we are looking for at present." "You've had some of mine," was the retort, whilst the crowd cheered "I've sent my hus- band and my two brothers." The audience, having joined in singing " Tipperary " and the National Anthem, gave cheeirs for the Chairman and speakers, and "the recruits who are coming along," and then separated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>SECOND MEETING.</head>
        <p>A SECOND MEETING.</p>
        <p>Another meeting was held at Bridge Foot last night, when a goodly-sized crowd assembled and listened to rousing speeches from, amongst others, Alderman Bennett, Captain Sparrow, Mr. James Ball and Mr. Hunter Watts, of London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>PEMBERTON 'CASE:</head>
        <p>PEMBERTON 'CASE:</p>
        <p>Councillor Seeks Opportunity To Rehabilitate Character.</p>
        <p>JUDGE REFUSES LEAVE TO APPEAL.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thurs- day, before his Honour Judge Gurner, Mr. Bertram Jones, solicitor, Liverpool, on behalf of Councillor John Pemberton, Stock- ton Heath, made an application for leave to appeal against his Honour's refusal to grant a new trial in the case of Weaver v. Pember- ton. The application was made under Sec- tion 120 of the County Courts Act, 1888. It will be remembered that a fortnight ago, Councillor Pemberton, through Mr. Lindon Riley, the well-known barrister, made appli- cation for a re-trial of the recent action brought against him by Messrs. G. and J. Weaver, cabinetmakers, of Bridge-street, to recover the balance of an account for £lO 15s. id. for work done, in respect of which account defendant had paid into Court the sum of £7 14s. 10d., leaving two items in dis- pute, the same relating to repairs to a book- case and the making of a cushion for an oak settle.</p>
        <p>With regard to the bookcase, it was stated at the trial that the defendant alleged that the bookcase, which he sent to be mended on December 26th, 1911, had not been returned, another having been sent in its place; whilst it was contended that the cushion supplied was too short. At the trial, judgment was given for the plaintiffs for the amount claimed, with costs, his Honour remarking that, in the absence of documentary evidence, he did not believe a single word of the evi- dence of Mr. Pemberton or his sister, Miss Florence Pemberton, who also went into the witness-box. It was, he said, a pity they did not consent to judgment instead of going into the witness-box and deliberately perjuring themselves. When Mr. Lindon Riley made his application, the plaintiffs, as at the trial, were represented by Mr. T. S. Steel. Mr. Lindon Riley was instructed by Mr. Bertram</p>
        <p>Jones, of Liverpool. The Judge refused to re-open the case,and concluded that he told the defendant and his. sister that without documentary evidence he did' not believe them. "Neither do I now," added his Honour. "I can only stand by everything I said on the former occasion, and a new trial is refused, with costs." Subsequently Councillor Pemberton made a personal explanation at the Warrington Town Council regarding the action, and declared that his defence was an honest and truthful one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>THURSDAY'S APPEAL.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY'S APPEAL.</p>
        <p>New Trial Wanted To "End The Matter."</p>
        <p>Mr Jones said he asked his Honour to exercise the discretion he had under the sec- tion named, so that the matters in dispute could be argued, if necessary, before a Divi- sional Court.</p>
        <p>His Honour: It is no use asking me. It is only a question of fact. I have decided upon that. I have decided twice against you, and no Divisional Court will upset that on any condition.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jones pointed out that Councillor Pem- berton had a statutory right of appeal if the amount involved had been over £2O, but this not being so, he contended that there was a principle involved. His Honour: There is no principle involved. It is a question of fact. Mr. Jones submitted that with all defer- ence to his Honour's views, Mr. Pemberton thought he had been charged with perjury. His Honour I told him twice; that is Mr.. Jones said it had injured Mr. Pember- ton very considerably, and this was the only way in which he could ask his Honour to exercise his discretion to see if a Divisional Court would order a new trial. If his client lost there was an end of the whole matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>WOULD FIND SECURITY.</head>
        <p>WOULD FIND SECURITY.</p>
        <p>He was instructed to say that Mr. Pem- berton would find security for all costs. He wished, if possible, to rehabilitate his good character i the town. His business had been more or leis damaged, and by an appeal to a Ihvisional Court there was a chance of him coming out more creditably than the public Pres.: appeared to have made him on the 18th March. The only loophole they had, the only thing left to Mr. Pemberton, was to secure his Honour's leave to this appeal on whatever terras his Honour thought fit. Men's minds were sometimes different at different times, and if this case was tried again his Honour might come to a different conclusion. Fresh evidence could be got- His Honour: Of the same character as the last?</p>
        <p>Mr. Jones said he was instructed to say, without any show of disrespect to his Honour's ppsition as the Judge in that Court, if it was a: case where there was to be hard swearing on each side, a second* trial might better enable the Judge t? say on which side the truth lay.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>GOOD CITIZEN.</head>
        <p>A GOOD CITIZEN.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pemberton was a .good citizen of .War- rington, had done well for his town, had a cPrtain amount of influence, and he, thought he was a well-respected man, but this stigma lay very heavily upon him, and he felt it very much. His friends thought he should do all he could to have it removed. He (Mr. Jones) appealed to his Honour to give Mr. Pemberton a Chance of rehabilitating his character in the town, which his Honour's observations had seriously affected. There was a great prin- ciple at stake for Mr. Pemberton. He had not been guilty of this crime, and in principle, at law, every man was presumably innocent until he was found guilty by a jury.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>LEAVE TO APPEAL REFUSED.</head>
        <p>LEAVE TO APPEAL REFUSED.</p>
        <p>His Honour: There is no question of prin- ciple involved. The question is one of fact. I have found the fact absolutely and definitely against Mr. Pemberton. If I were to grant leave in such a case as this, where the facts are found specifically against him by me, I should not only be stultifying myself, but the Divisional Court would laugh at an appeal of this character, as well as at counsel who ap- peared for Mr. Pemberton. Under these cir- cumstances, I do not feel inclined, nor will I, geve leave to appeal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>KEEPING AN EYE ON 'EM.</head>
        <p>KEEPING AN EYE ON 'EM.</p>
        <p>New Duty.for Hotel and Lodging- House Keepers.</p>
        <p>Under an Order in Council issued on April 13th, every keeper or manager of any hotel, boarding-house, lodging-house or apartments is required to keep a register of all aliens over the age of fourteen years staying in his, or her, house, and to obtain from every person staying in the house a signed statement showing his nationality and, in the case of an alien, a state- ment as to the land of his birth, trade, &amp;c., and also particulars as to whether he has served in any army, navy or police force, together with the nanie of the place from Which such person came, &amp;c. _</p>
        <p>The Order applies not only to aliens, for it also expressly states that "every person, whether an alien or a British subject, who stays in any hotel, inn, boarding-house, lodging- house or apartments" has a duty imposed upon him "to furnish to the keeper or manager of the house a signed statement" containing par- ticulars as to his name, age, trade, &amp;c. Failure, or refusal, to comply with this Order may in- volve a fine of £lOO or six months' -imprison- ment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>WEEK OF CLEVER PLAYS AT ROYAL COURT.</p>
        <p>A week of first-class plays—plays that have either won or enhanced great reputations for their authors—played by a capable little com- pany of painstaking artistes, is something rif a novelty in Warrington, and one has to confess to a feeling akin to disappointment that the houses which have, so far, been e.ttracted to the Royal Court, have neither been sa large nor appreciative as could be wished. Bernard Shaw is the most discussed, the most advertised and in some respects the most brilliant play- wright of the day, and one might have thought that a Shavian masterpiece would have drawn a crowded and curious, even if not enthusiastic house. But nothing of the sort! When "Arms and the Man "—a sparkling comedy, conceived in that most brilliant and caustic vein which G.B.S. has made peculiarly his own—was pro- duced on Tuesday night, the house was barely a ' third full and it was, in the parlance of " the profession". " painfully wooden." Bright epikram, racy dialogue and biting sarcasm all ' ran to waste, and one left the theatre feeling, as no doubt the company did also, that the Lancashire people may be " gradely "'business- folk and intensely hard-headed and practical, but that they have scant taste in the way a drama for that which is genuinely clever. Some- how the higher modern drama is not a paying line of goods in the industrial north At the customary matinee on Monday, and again on Monday evening, Herbert Henry Davies's clever if somewhat unconvincing "Captain Drew on Leave" was presented, and on Wednesday " Don " formed the attraction. Thursday evening the house was given over to the manager's benefit—which is duly reported elsewhere—and on Friday Mr. N. • Thorpe- Mayne and his company revived T. W. Robert- son's fine old comedy entitled "Ours," which set our fathers and their fathers a-laughing even in the darkest days of the Crimean War, with which subject it deals. In all the plays named the principal parts are played by Miss Beryl Hamilton and Mr. N. Thorpe-Mayne, two artistes of undoubted ability, who are equally effective in the lightest of light comedy, and in the more serious passages of domestic drama. The Shaw play is to be repeated to-night. "A BUMPER BENEFIT."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Complimentary Show At The Royal</head>
        <p>Complimentary Show At The Royal</p>
        <p>Court.</p>
        <p>In these days of the many thousand feet cinematograph films, one is so constantly coming across the two-part idea that no sur- prise is felt when the rage is found to have -spread into other provinces. Mr. H. L. War- burton, the genial and popular acting-manager of the Royal Court Theatre, Warrington, took his third annual benefit on Thursday evening in two parts. And the process of dissection was in no way overdone; in fact it was, underdone, for, judging by the fact that there were two crowded "houses," it seems not unfeasible that the " benefit " might with success have been spread over three performances. If •audiences that fill the house, if the best services of a long list of talented entertainers, if the good-will of everyone—staff, patrons, performers—make what is meant by the well-worn phrase "a bumper benefit," then Mr. Warburton's was "a bumper of bumpers." By his courtesy, judgment and consideration, Mr. Warburton has won the esteem and liking of the vast majority of those with whom he has come into contact as manager of the Royal Court. His popularity with the public—a public whose taste he has served so faithfully—was reflected in the attend- ances; the regard of the staff of the theatre for their chief was betokened by the presentation made towards the end of the second perform- ance; and the feelings of the contributing artistes (amateur and professional) needed no further exhibition than in the enthuSiasm with which they entered into their respective parts of the entertainment.</p>
        <p>Many Good Things in the Programmes.</p>
        <p>One could dilate on the many excellencies of the two programmes presented, but space for- bids. One of the tit-bits of the evening was the performance at the second " house " of a one-act farcical comedy "A Little ' Fowl ' Play," by members of the Warrington Dramatic Society. It was a delightfully clever play, splendidly acted. The author is Mr. Harold Owen. The parts were taken, by Mr. H. G. Mosley, Mrs. Olive Wheeler, Mr. Sidney Wall- head and Miss Jessie Forshaw. Another amateur " turn " of very high ' merit was "Flora's Holiday" (H. Lane Wilson), com- prising a cycle of old English solos and con- certed items. It was given by Miss E. Hewitt, Mrs. J. Smallshaw, Mr. W. Booth and Mr. Fred Fieldhouse. The part-singing, as well as the rendering of the songs, was of a high order, and was much enjoyed by the audience. Mr. H. M. Ashton (whom every Warringtonian—who is anybody—knows to be, an exceptionally gifted entertainer) enhanced his enviable reputation with his treatment of "Evings' Dorgs"Orspital." Others who contributed with distinction were: Mr. N. Thorpe-Mayne and Miss Beryl Hamilton and their company, Miss Winnie Williams, Mr. Cyril Cooke (violin), Wee Georgie Beck, Jack Sheridan and David Poole. There was a specially-augmented orchestra, under Mr. James Roy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Presentation.</head>
        <p>The Presentation.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Slater (stage manager), in presenting Mr. Warburton, on behalf of the staff (includ- ing the orchestra), with a silver epergne and flower stands, to commemorate his third annual benefit and also his marriage, wished him and Mrs. Warburton long life and happiness. Replying, Mr. Warburton thanked everybody— the proprietor (Sir Joseph Beecham, Bart.), the gener9,l =Auger (Mr. A. M. Loader), Mr. J. Walters and Miss- Lowe (the secretary for the benefit). In view of the exceptional times, he proposed to give a percentage of the proceeds of the " benefit " to the Mayor's fund for the relief of war distress.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Popular Drama Next Week.</head>
        <p>Popular Drama Next Week.</p>
        <p>For next week Herbert Shelley's Company in a new and original play of absorbing in- tere,st, entitled "A Mill Girl's Wedding," has been booked. The play has proved a big hit wherever it has been presented, and this will be its first visit to Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>FASHIONABLE FANCIES AT THE HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>FASHIONABLE FANCIES AT THE HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Tatteu Hall, in his latest comedy creation, "Fanciful Whims" (in which Miss Donna Haydon also appears) scores a success' at the Hippodrome this week. He also gives a beau- tiful love-scene, ," In a Chinese Garden."</p>
        <p>Wee Georgia Beck, the baby star comedian and "everybody's favourite," appears first as a girl in captivating sentimental song, and afterwards, in a complete change, as a toreador crossed in love. Miss Bessie Butt, as "the fashion Nate in male characters," contributes up-to-date topical ditties in 1 polished style. Cromo and Hamilton "bring down the house" with their capital burlesque scena, "Fashions" —which is one long laugh and is full of origin- ality. David Poole is equally successful in his delightful ventriloquial sketch on school- life—a cleverly contrived humoresque featur- ing many novelties. Will B. Wilby is a "horsey" comedian with a highly-entertaining repertoire of eccentric oddities. Rich Hayes is a sort of futurist-freak who "juggles as an excuse." Incidentally he performs some of the cleverest feats of his art ever seen at the Hippodrome—and all in his own excruciatingly funny style. Jack Sheridan (son of Mark of that ilk) introduces a nover comedy element, in which traces of his famous father are most happily evident. "The Gaumont Graphic" supplies, on the screen, the usual " war-news" in picture.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Belgian Trick Cyclist Next Week.</head>
        <p>Belgian Trick Cyclist Next Week.</p>
        <p>Romani Noiset, who is described as Belgium's moat daring trick-cyclist, will provide the star turn at this popular place of entertainment next week. His feats will include a thrilling ride on "the Devil's table" whilst it is revolving at the rate of eighty miles an hour. The Sisters Sprightly will appear in their latest topical and patriotic sketch "Business as Usual," and John Paley, formerly a military bandmaster, will present a versatile and artistic musical act. Tom Drew and Co. premise a riot or laughter with a Sketch entitled</p>
        <p>" the'Registry," and a number of other excel- lent turnsivill go to the making up of a tip-top</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>REALISTIC WAR-FILMS AT THE</head>
        <p>REALISTIC WAR-FILMS AT THE</p>
        <p>STAR KINEMA.</p>
        <p>The powerful programmes shovring,this week at the cosy and comfortable-Kinema incinde plenty of the latest war—and other—films. During the first three days a beat...fife scenic film, "The Wonderful Grotto," was followed by "Pathe's Animated Journal," giving pic- torial details of many of the most recent hap- penings in all quarters. "Dandy Donovan, the Gentleman -Cracksman," was a beautifully- filmed "thriller," featuring the world-renowned Gladys Cooper. The wonderful horsemanship of the Portuguese Cavalry was exhibited fully, and "The Gaumont Topical" included a graphic series of actual scenes from the bril- liant French victory in the Vosges. "The Child-Killers" was a most moving and realistic drama portraying the proved brutality of the Huns in their mad advance into France. "Cupid Backs the Winners" proved an abso- lutely delightful serio-comedy of love and mis- understanding. To-day's highly attractive bills of fare in- clude a further grand selection of war-films, topped by "The Fighting Strain of Old Eng- land."</p>
        <p>The chief violinist of the se. Falaba—which, two weeks ago, was sunk by a German sub- marine—has been cordially received whilst entering this week upon his season's engage- ment at the Kinema. For Next Week.</p>
        <p>A film that should "get right there" has been booked as the leading feature in the pro- gramme for the first three nights of next week. It is entitled "A Lancashire Lass," and is highly spoken of by all who have had the privilege of seeing it. At the Week-end the " star " film will be "The Unwelcome Mrs. Hatch."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>GRAND PICTURES AT THE GRAND.</head>
        <p>GRAND PICTURES AT THE GRAND.</p>
        <p>The programme submitted at the Grand this week have been most enjoyable. On the first three days, in addition to " Pathe's Animated Gazette," depicting various scenes from the seat of war, there were shown numerous other ex- ceedingly attractive films, including a scream- ingly funny comedy entitled "The Face on the Bar-room Floor," and " Polidor Declares War." The " star " picture, however, was a stirring four-part drama entitled "A Four- million Dowry." Another fine programme is being submitted during the Nyeek-end. This includes "The Life of Lord Roberts, V.C.," a fine film, depicting the chief events in the life of the great Field-Marshal and one that should not be missed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>WHY BRfID HAS RISEN.</head>
        <p>WHY BRfID HAS RISEN.</p>
        <p>The Difficulty Of Obtaining Wheat From Russia.</p>
        <p>An " Examiner " man the other day inter- viewed Mr Toft (secretary of the Warrington Master Bakers' Association) and Mr. Wm. Atkinson (manager of the. Warrington E.I. Co-operative Society) on the matter of the most recent increase in the price of bread. From Monday of this week, ninepence and eightpence have been charged for large loaves, and four pence halfpenny and fourpence for two pound- ' loaves. The price for best bread prior to the commencement of the war was sixpence half- penny. Subsequently it rose to sevenpence half- „ penny and then to eightpence before reaching the present figure. This, as was pointed out by Mr. Toft, is by no means a high price for war time. Speaking from memory, the Secretary of the Master Bakers' Association said that bread cost as much as one and eightpence per large , loaf in the Crimean War. The reason for the present advance, said Mr. Toft, was the diffi- culty in obtaining wheat, none at all being forthcoming from Russia. Asked whether he thought there was any likelihood of a further rise before very long, Mr. Toft said he thought the next month or two would be the worst ex- perienced so far since the outbreak of the war. LIMIT PRACTICALLY REACHED, Mr. Atkinson expressed the opinion, in reply to a query, that the price would not ativaneu much further than it has already gone. He added, however, that in the event of one or two liners carrying large consignments of wheat being sunk, the market would be upset. As an illustration of his remark, he cited the increase in the price of tea, which was due to the fact that the Emden, in the course of her exploits, sent four million pounds of this commodity to the bottom of the sea. "But for the fact that we had large stocks of flour in hand, and were thus able to give our customers the benefit of our purchases, the price of bread in Warrington would have been up to ninepence two months ago," Mr. Atkinson said, and added that the society was now at the end of its tether, so far as old stocks were concerned, and the result was that they had had to raise the price of their bread.</p>
        <p>UP THE GOVERNMENT'S SLEEVE. Alluding to the Government's position in the matter, Mr. Atkinson said there was no doubt they (the Government) had large wheat re- sources to draw upon that they were reserving for, possible emergencies. They would not, say what they contemplated doing with all that wheat and with the flour that had come over from Canada. They were keeping dark their intentions. He thought it would be• better if they stated what they were going to do.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>CLARA BUTT.</head>
        <p>CLARA BUTT.</p>
        <p>Next Monday's Visit To Warrington. Warrington people will have pleasnrabel memories of the visit to the town years ago of Madame Clara Butt and her husband, Mr. Kennerley Rumford. It will be gratifying news that this distinguished lady singer will be the chief attraction at a patriotic concert •to be given on Monday next, at the Parr Hall. We may say here as a point of local interest that the Warrington Musical Society have consented to sing in the choruses, "Land of hope and glory" (Elgar), "The home flag" (Craxton), and "It comes from the misty ages" (Elgar). The concerts given by Madame Butt and her husband have enabled the "Clara Butt-Rum- ford Fund to do splendidly in the way of help- ing others during these trying times. They decided to give the entire proceeds of all their concerts during the past autumn to various war relief funds; and to this end they gave a concert at the Royal Albert Hall in October, another at the Queen's Hall in November, and 25 concerts in the principal provincial cities before Christmas, with the result that they col- lected a sum of nearly £7,000 for distribution. The Queen's "Work for Women" Fund received £1,700, the Arts Fund .2350, Blackpool War Funds £l5O, East End Christmas Dinner Fund £lOO, National Sunday League (for employment of artistes) .21" and various dona- tions to other deserving objects, panounting to £2lO. Madame Butt then devoted her efforts to helping as many as possible of her less fortunate colleagues in the musical profession, and with the express approval of the Queen, has formed a fund entitled the "Clara 13utt-Rumford Fund," for providing engagements for artiste/ who have been so hardly hit by the disastrous conditions prevailing in the musical world. Madame Butt is still adding to this fund 139. the proceeds of the sale of flags and her auto- graphs at the concerts she is now giving alone; in the absence of Mr. Kennerley Rumford, who has been actively engaged with Red Cross work at the Front since November last. In connection with the Warrington concert, Mr. R. H. Pearson, 75, Bridge-street, ttas charge of the plan and tickets. During the interval the ladies of the chorag have undertaken to sell small Union Jacks at the Warrington concert for the benefit of the Clara Butt-Rumford Fund, which aims at pr°' viding engagements for artistes who have beell so severely affected by the war. Madame Butt is announced to sins "Go shall wipe away all tears" (Sullivan), "0 res, in the Lord" (Mendelssohn), My So, (Teresa del Riego), "The Home Flag ("-,; Craxton), two Irish airs "The Lover's Curse, and "I know my love" (ar. by H. Huglies,!' and "Land of hope and glory ' (Elgar). , other artistes are Mr. W. Hyde, the Misses 31e. Blower and E. Faulkner, and Mr. W. H. &amp;Oil:, ('cellist), with Mr. H. Craxton at the piano Mr. A. E. Godfrey at the organ. Seats sof, be guaranteed to all tickets if purchased' befi" concert day at the shop.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</head>
        <p>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>Street Lighting "As Usual." Colonel J. A. Moggridge, commanding the 40th Regimental District, gives notice that the Order issued with regard to the reduction of Public lighting in the Warrington district is ;low withdrawn.</p>
        <p>Safeguarding the Public. ,The Local Government Board have notified me Town Council that they have issued an Order prohibiting the sale of certain shellfish 'which was hitherto obtainable, the said shell- fish being likely to cause danger to public health.</p>
        <p>No Overalls for Policemen. At a recent meeting of the Warrington Town klouncil it was suggested that the policemen on Point duty should be provided with overalls, as ',protection against rainy weather. The Watch ik:"unittee, having considered the suggestion, 'aye decided that no action be talfen in the /natter.</p>
        <p>School Sermons at St. Mary's. The annual school sermons will be preached by the Rev. Dr. Downey at St. Mary's R.C. Church, Buttermarket-street, to-morrow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Street Accident.</head>
        <p>Street Accident.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday a horse and trap was being driven ,41°11g Church-street, when, just opposite the liso-operative Stores, the horse fell and threw the 1111;iver into the roadway. Fortunately, however, li man was not seriously hurt and, after the e had been got to its feet, was able to pro- ceedoers on his journey.. •</p>
        <p>Fire Brigade Changes. The Council have accepted the resignations of ,l'ergt. W. Monks and Fireman E. A. Bradbury members of the Fire Brigade, and have ap- Pointed Firemen A. Monks and F. Clegg ser- geants. Mr. Thomas Palmer has been ap- pointed a new member of the brigade, and an- other fireman is being advertised for.</p>
        <p>Mr. Benjamin Wadsworth. , Mr. Benjamin Wadsworth, formerly of San- rY-street, Warrington, and now of the Music Ins. 5, Queen-street, Rhyl, was on Tuesday acted vice-chairman of the Rhyl Urban Conn- At Rhyl the year's vice-chairman becomes 'ext Year's chairman automatically.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Death of Mrs. Madelev.</head>
        <p>Death of Mrs. Madelev.</p>
        <p>With regret we announce the death of Mrs. -rJlna liadeley, wife of the Borough Vbrarian—which sad event took place at her ...4t°11113 on Tuesday. Thp deceased lady was 70 -Leiars of age. At noon to-day the funeral will L'ute place at the Cemetery.</p>
        <p>'Complaint re Insured Soldiers. ao the War Office, the Insurance Commis- ort,e,rs have forwarded copy of the resolution e Warrington Medical Benefit Sub-com- -13:11it he of the Insurance Committee, complain- that persons suffering from minor ailments rebeing temporarily discharged from the 4._11317 as medically unfit—apparently in order ;'! get treatment from their panel doctors (and ;I_lleidentally sickness benefit from their ap- Proved societies).</p>
        <p>No Horse Parade This Year. p4t a meeting of the committee of the Horse arade Society, held on Tuesday, the following s°llltion was passed: That on account of the a'r_tile parade be postponed for this year." G..Lun behalf of the committee," writes Sir til'uert Greenall, the president, "I desire to auk the subscribers for their support in the Past, and to say that the abandonment of the tintcle this year is only temporary, and express tl e hope that\ they will give the committee ueir assistance when the present crisis is over. The receipts last year were £74 is. 10d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Thehiall Canal Mystery.</head>
        <p>Thehiall Canal Mystery.</p>
        <p>_ At the Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday Luorning, Mr. Albert Hampton (assistant over- applied for the payment of expenses, Aounting to £2 16s. 6d., incurred by the find- and burying of the body of an unknown woman—recovered from the canal at Thelwall ?,11 the 7th (as reported in the " Examiner " 'lte following Saturday). The application was Ranted. We understand that the police have 4°17 a clue to the woman's identity.</p>
        <p>Slitirlhand. ,_ At the ' last meeting of the Shorthand Writers' Association, Mr. T. W. Durant (pre- sident) lectured on Pil°ll°PaPhY''. The merits of the "Gregg" "A Bird's-Bye View of sYstera of shorthand compared with Pitman's sYstem were touched upon by the lecturer. The diFicussi°n wee opened by the Secretary, who took the view it tri that as a progressive association would be that their while to give Gregg vote of thanks, proposed by Mr. T. We, seconded by Miss M. L. Buckley, brought lthe raeeting to a close. The dictation matter or the speed practice was taken from the ectures of Andrew Carnegie.</p>
        <p>Congregational Appointment. *The Rev. Fred Hibbert, of Southport, has :rePted•a call to the pulpit Of the Victoria- Ctireet Congregational Church, Blackpool—in ecession to the Rev. S. Gamble Walker, now of Cavell' dish-street Church, Manchester. In -uls letter of acceptance, Mr. Hibbert says he 11111 come as no stranger to Victoria-street, as b, worshipped there as a boy. He was also at ackburn for nineteen years. Mr. Hibbert, Woo is ex-president of the Lancashire Congre- onal Union, has frequently preached at yvYcliffe, Warrington—especially at young tople's services. He is most highly esteemed and a great favourite of, Warrington C,on- l'`tTationalists.</p>
        <p>Warrington Scotsmen. At the annual meeting of the Warrington lialedonian Association, held in the Lion b.°tel, 011 Thursday evening, a letter was read Ir. Fulton-Smith tendering his resig- Itet.:_ 4 4un of the presidency :—Mr. DaVenportc! .ppl'erY high tribute to the work la the at t'o—hsiderrt, emphasising the fact that the posi- I ti—:f the society was largely due to his 1111;4.4.,„11 efforts to maintain it on its very high as a social institution and in the of their fellow-townsmen who were not beautifully illuniinated "Roll of 414-2,;;31_11r" containing the names of those of the trr "ere who have responded to their coun- qe4B call %vas exhibited. It was much admired it 3 the work of Mr. J. Tennant, the hon. re+n s'r3r of the association. Nit's! election of officers resulted in the ap- -1.4 jj'Dk ent of the following: President, Mr. J nee:''eliport; vice-president, Mr. W. Murray; Ilev.iee-presidents, Messrs. W. Skinner, B t4ri—er and J. Fulton-Smith; hon. secre- tary' J. Tennant; assistant secre- k L4l r. E. Williamson; committee, Dr. J. cro ',eon, and Messrs. J. Bennett, D. R. Cam- % 3141,• Finlayson, J. Hunter, J. McDermid, ir.tuar, P. H. Brown, E. Clayton, M. Grant, auevekerbie, G. Marshall and W. Mitchell; l'rs Messrs. 11. E. Robertson and R. B. , 11111 '44 Chile Girls' Guild at Colborne- . street. ottinTjatile Golborne-street Baptist Schoolroom . nrsd, aY evening, an interesting and de the ,„4-14 concert was given to the children by Geitrithers of the Hill Cliffe Baptist Girls' the The. proceeds were devoted to reducing Ite.v. debt on the new school buildings. The fe„,,,tf• Ogden presided, and in the course of, 611re"ei.(/Pe, fling remarks expressed great plea- he indication of denominational unity, one Z„lseu by the fact that the children from or ,101 Should assist in reducing the debt ,zoi:iteilicrinsoSun:aoy school. A most enjoyable thrall:1111e Was afterwards submitted. Miss; opened with a recitation en- Itritannia," after which Miss Florence 431:::8"1 Sang "'The Death of Nelson." A Atisseforte solo was given by Miss Holt, whilst tmilY Williams contributed a recitation ``Tha,,ed. "War News!, Another recitation, 'flgele Whisper," was given' by PopPY yetteZ. 'while an action-song entitled "Won't Lit% InY pretty .flowers?" was given by eited kiss Annie Lonsdale re- l'endel.:4''ut3'3" while Miss Bertha Nicholls song entitled "Little Leaves." d" recitations--i' The Shadow and the given band "The French at Ratisbon "—were I"efien,st,.3' MISS Phillys Moss, and Miss Eva reePectively. The Hill Cliffe Sketch a'ti'tiburc, right the programme to a close oreat —*lng sketch entitled "Four After One." with eon credit is due to Mrs. Roughsedge and her s.vine for the very able manner in which had'beert trained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Christianity and War.</head>
        <p>Christianity and War.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. Ernest Elcock, of Manchester, will give a public address on "Christianity and the War" at the Friends' Meeting House, Butter- market-street, to-morrow evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Wycliffe Anniversary.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Anniversary.</p>
        <p>At the Wycliffe Sunday school anniversary to be held to-morrow—the Rev. W. Evans will conduct the services, whilst in the afternoon special music is to be rendered by the scholars.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>For Comaleseent Soldiers.</head>
        <p>For Comaleseent Soldiers.</p>
        <p>The new Military Convalescent some at Thelwall Heys will be open for public inspection on Thursday next, from 10 a.m. to 6 p.m. Gifts of groceries, stationery and money will be welcomed by the commitee, of which Mr. F. Aylmer Frost is the hon. secretary. No Politics.</p>
        <p>Mrs. A. H. Crosfield and Miss Truda Cros- field have been appointed delegates from the Warrington Women's Liberal Associati9n to attend the annual Council meetings of the Women's Liberal Federation in London, May 19 and 20. No politics will be discussed this year. Another Advance In Wheat.</p>
        <p>Wheat prices again advanced at Wednesday's Warrington market, and there is every likeli- hood of still further increases. English wheat is getting more scarce, prices ranging from Bs. to Bs. 3d. per bushel of 701 b. Oats were firm at late rates, viz., 4s. to 4s. 3d. per 451 b. Wheat and oat straw fetched 345. per ton, XX. flour 48s. per 2801 b., best bran is. std. per 201 b., and eggs a shilling for ten. Brother John Holden.</p>
        <p>The "Duke of Lancaster" Lodge, R.A.0.8., was favoured on Tuesday by a visit from the Grand Primo of England, the Right Hon. Sir Francis Early, who took the opportunity of pre- senting the Knight John Holden, K. 0.31., K.G.}.1., with a gold lion jewel from the Grand Lodge of England, "as a small tribute to his loyalty and devotion to the Order," Bro. Holden said he would look upon the jewel as one of his greatest treasures.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Concerts for the Wounded at the Infirmary. •</head>
        <p>Concerts for the Wounded at the Infirmary. •</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon another enjoyable con- cert arranged by Mr. W. B. Randall, was given to the wounded soldiers in the Infirmary, when the following artistes took part: Miss M. Blackburne, Miss C. Rigby, Mr. Walker and Mr. Caldwell (the blind musician). Mr. Randall made an excellent accomlaanist. On Thursday evening Miss Doris Welsby, Miss Welsby, Mr. Delaney and Mr. Harrison (humorist) entertained the soldiers. Mr. Randall again accompanied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>One of Grappenhall's Oldest Parishioners.</head>
        <p>One of Grappenhall's Oldest Parishioners.</p>
        <p>The death of Mrs. Sarah Shakeshaft removes a well-known and much-esteemed figure who will be happily and gratefully remembered. Mrs. Shakeshaft was a native of Grappenhall, and had almost completed her 89th year. She re- mained cheerful and active to the last, and passed peacefully away on Sunday without pain or apparent disease. • It is worthy of mention that fifty years of her life were spent in one house as tenant of the Smith family. The funeral took place on Wednesday after- noon at Grappenhall Church, and the service was conducted by the Rector (Rev. Harry V. Pigot). There were present: Mr. Samuel Shake- shaft, Mr. Peter Shakeshaft, Mr. George Shake- shaft (sons), Mrs. Lloyd, Mrs. Longshaw, Mrs. Swatridge (daughters), Miss Gertrude Miller (grand-daughter), Mr. James Shakeshaft, of Norwich; Mr. and Mrs. Albert Wright and Mr. Albert Wright, jun. The funeral was arranged by Messrs. J. Mounfield and Sons.</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. Caldwell, a-Latchford. We regret to report the death of Mrs. Cald- well, of "Thelma," Latchford Without, who passed away at her home on Monday, at the age of 65. Her early life was spent in Leigh, but she has resided in recent years in the neighbourhood of Warrington, and has become well-known and greatly esteemed among a wide circle of friends, by whom her loss will be keenly felt. The funeral took place on Thursday. After a service conducted at the house by Mr. R. B. Woods, the cortege left for Leigh Cemetery. Among those present were: Mr. Josiah Caldwell (husband), Mr. Arthur Caldwell', Mr. Fred And- rews, Mr. David Caldwell, Captain W. Savage, Mr. F. Andrews, Mr. Arthur Caldwell, Miss Maggie Bellamy, Mr. and' Mrs. Albert Boydell, Mr. and Mrs. Wm. Hilton, Mrs. John Fearn- hea,d, Mr. Albert .Fearn.head, Mr. T. Dootson, Mr. R. Henshall, Mr. R. B. Woods, Mr. NV: Bellamy, Mr. A. Mounfield and Nurse Calender. The service at the cemetery was conducted by Mr. R. Henshall, and the committal service was read by Mr. R. B. Woods. Messrs. J. Mounfield and Sons carried out the funeral arrangements.</p>
        <p>Ma mum Output of Leather. There was no particular change, in either the demand or output last week, the latter being kept to maximum figures, writes the Warring- ton correspondent of the "Leather Trades Review." The demand seemed hardly so strong as usual, except for the heavy-weights, which, as usual, were in persistent iriquirY both for early and late deliveries. Supplies continue to be small, and it is likely that all the inquiry adds very little to the bulk already booked. Information from manufacturing centres is that much of the large Government order which has been so freely commented upon has not yet been placed; hence the leather being sold to-day is mostly wanted for completion of contracts which were insufficiently covered. There seems to be some expectation that the new orders will call for a somewhat lighter type of boot, as the present style, whilst well adapted for winter use, might be found rather cumbersome for a summer campaign on the Continent, where normally the season is much drier than with us. The position as to tanning materials is obscure. It is not so much a question of price as supply, regarding which expert opinion differs widely. The only point on which they agree is that, although stocks are actually lying on the quay sides, both in France and Italy, it is unlikely the British tanner will handle any of them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Photographers Active.</head>
        <p>Photographers Active.</p>
        <p>Two important meetings have been held during the week under the auspices of the Warrington Photographic Society. For the first time in its history the Lancashire and Cheshire PhotograplAic Union was entertained at Warrington on Saturday. I The secretaries' and lecturers' annual meeting was held at the Warrington Photographic Society's Rooms at the Old Academy. Mr. Greenall, of Chorley, president of the union, was in the chair, and the meeting was unexpectedly large. Several important discussions were raised on the sub- jects of the portfolios of prints, and the boxes of lantern slides which circulate amongst the , affiliated societies, on the treatment of lecturers by both secretaries and societies, and the attitude of .the union towards the new colour work in photography. The members were heartily welcomed by Mr. A. Davis, president of ,the Warrington Society, and were after- wards entertained to tea at Atkinson's Cafe, Bridge-street.- The newly-finished Warring- ton Bridge incidentally came in for a good 'deal of admiration. On Tuesday the annual meeting of the War- rington Photographic Society was held at the same historic rooms. Mr. Davis occupied the chair, and for a 'fourth term, was elected president. The Secretary (Mr. J. W. Row- land) resigned, and his place was filled for the first time by a lady—Miss Jackson, Lon- don-road, Stockton Heath. All the other offi- cers were re-elected, except the delegates to the Lancashire and Cheshire Photographic Union. This year's delegates are Mr. W. H. Hartley and Mr. G. H. Walker. It was decided to appoint all members who are serv- ing under the colours as honorary members for the war-time and to have a Roll of Honour drawn up. The syllabus of summer meetings and outings was completed, and these promise to be full of interest and pleasure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>EXAUINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 24, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAUINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 24, 1915.</p>
        <p>TERRITORIAL ROLL OF HONOU R.</p>
        <p>Revised List of Casualties to April 18th.</p>
        <p>KILLED, '9; WOUNDED, 61.</p>
        <p>This week another official list of the casualties that have occurred to the 4th South Lancashires on the Continent has been pub- lished. Some of the names have already been published, and there is, consequently, some overlapping, but the following figures may be taken to be approximately accurate : Killed Wounded Sick</p>
        <p>The additions that have come to hand this week are 2; wounded, 12. They are :</p>
        <p>KILLED. Private W. Reeves (Newton).</p>
        <p>DIED OF WOUNDS. Private J. Jones (Norwich).</p>
        <p>WOUNDED. Sergeant C. H. Walker. Sergeant H. S. Goodwin. Lance-Corporal A. Woods. Lance-Corporal Slinn (Newton). Private J. Holmes (Colborne). Private W. G. Baskerville. Private G. Plant (Newton). Private F. H. Parkinson. Private J. Lloyd. Private R. Woods (not on official roll). Private T. Dalton.</p>
        <p>WOUNDED. (Not Admitted to Hospital.) Private A. Macdonald (Crewe).</p>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>SERGEANT C. 11.</p>
        <p>WALKER.</p>
        <p>Hit Above Ankle When Entering</p>
        <p>The Trenches.</p>
        <p>In the following letter to his wife at 2, Marsh- street, Sergeant Charles H. Walker last week-</p>
        <p>SERGEANT C. }I._WALICER.</p>
        <p>Wounded.</p>
        <p>which I was so closely connected with the lodge."</p>
        <p>Describing the work the Battalion has done since arrivai In France, Sergeant Walker says the journey to and from the trenches is anything but a pleasure trip, for the bullets from the German lines are flying around in all directions, and by these stray shots as they are termed, hundreds of casualties are caused. Once in the trench the strictest discipline, accompanied by a reasonable amount of common sense is necessary. . . . "We grad- ually adopted ourselves to the conditions," he says, "and in a very short time we were return- ing the enemy's fire with energy." All went about, their work with a zeal that would have done credit to the most hardened veteran; but our spirits were lowered to zero by the death of poor Tom Watson—he died like a soldier, doing his duty. He was one of my,own platoon, and our heartfelt sympathy goes out to his father, whom we all know so well. We have since had numerous losses, but it is only what can be expected in such a great conflict. Personally, I never felt better in my life." Upon leaving the National School, Sergeant Charles Walker seri•ed an apprenticeship in Messrs. Rylands Bros.' works—remaining as a wiredrawer in service there until called up in August. He has also been a member of the Parish Church choir, and has three cousins in the Army, two cousins in the Navy, and a brother-in-law training in the South as a Sergeant in Kitchener's Army.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <p>end first conveyed to Warrington the news that he had been wounded. April 14th,--You will no doubt be surprised at the news I am sending you this time, but there is no need for you to worry about it. We were going into the trenches on Sunday night, the 11th, when I was hit by a bullet just above the left ankle. I am now in hospital, where I shall no doubt be confined to bed for a few days. Apart from the wound, 1 am keeping fairly well. Writing again the following day, the Sergeant</p>
        <p>says I am going on as well as can be expected. The hospital I am in at present is a field hospital, being entirely under canvas, and is situated on the top of a cliff, and quite close to the sea. It is a very peaceful spot, and a great contrast, to the trenches. s Everything here is cleanliness itself. Although I have only been here practically two days, I cannot speak too highly of the kindness which is shown to our wounded soldiers by those whose work takes ;them into the wards of this large institution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Fine Doctors and Nurses.</head>
        <p>Fine Doctors and Nurses.</p>
        <p>"The first thing which struck me on my arival here was the nurses—or sisters, as they are called. You cannot imagine what it felt like, after the rough life in the firing line, to hear these ladies speaking one's own language and see them moving about amongst the patients with a cheery word for every one. Then there are the doctors—all officers of the Royal Army Medical Corps. They, too, seem to lay themselves out to do everything possible to relieve and cheer up the unfortunate men under their care. "The food we get is excellent, again in con- trast to the trenches. To use the words of a. famous song, "It's just like being at home" to a great extent. The weather is lovely here to- day, and as I lie in my bed I can see the sea in the distance. Although lam far away from the scene of operations, I often think of the boys at the Front who are still fighting on, and I will continue to do so until either the war is over or they are wounded—perhaps. Something worse." On Tuesday morning further word arrived from the wounded Sergeant j who is quartered at the 16th General Hospital attached to the British Expeditionary Force, saying that he made favourable progress; whilst yesterday Mrs. Walker received from him two letters, saying he is quite comfortable, and continues to progress very nicely—although he is not yet out of bed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>AN ODDFELLOW.</head>
        <p>AN ODDFELLOW.</p>
        <p>Born in Warrington 35 years ago, Sergeant Walker is the son of Mr. and Mrs. Henry Walker, 21, Marsh-street. An old Volunteer, he served in the South African War as a private in the active service company com- manded by Captain F. M. Appleton, and holds a medal with bars for Laing's Nek, Transvaal, Orange Free State and Cape Colony. On his return, he in time attained the rank of Colour- Sergeant: Also he gained the Territorial Efficiency Medal. Whilst in training at Tun- bridge Wells, immediately after the outbreak of war, however, he suffered a nervous breakdown, and on his recovery reverted to the rank of 'Sergeant. Happily he was able to realise his desire to reach the Front. A P.G. in the 31st "Loyal Constitution" Lodge of the Independent Order of Oddfellows (Manchester Unity) Sergeant Walker has passed through the various offices of the lodge, and some three weeks ago his father—who is alsn an Oddfellow—received on his behalf the illumin- ated certificate showing that he had fulfilled the office of Noble Grand. In this connection Mr. Henry Taylor has just received from the Sergeant the following letter—written before the Sergeant was wounded : _</p>
        <p>_ "April sth, 1915,-1 must thank the members of the lodge for the honour which they have bestowed upon me by the presentation of the Past Officers' Certificate. I deeply regret that I was unable to be preseAt to thank the mem- bers personally for their kindness and assistance during my term of office, but I can assure you I am full of gratitude to all for the splendid manner in which I was supported by the officers and brethren alike. Wherever I may go I shall always look back with the greatest pleasure to those two and a hall years during</p>
        <p>was at the time, and of the attention received there. .</p>
        <p>Since penning the letter to his father. Sergeant Goodwin has been transferred to the First Eastern General Hospital at Cambriage. Writing to his brother from there he says :-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>SERGEANT H. S. GOODV% IN.</head>
        <p>SERGEANT H. S. GOODV% IN.</p>
        <p>"Not A Bull's-Eye."</p>
        <p>"I have had the honour" says Sergeant H. S. Goodwin, of tho Warrington Territorials, writing to his father at 9, Froghaa-lano, War- rington, "of being a .target foi the Germans, but I am glad to say they have nut made a bull's eye of me yet." This letter was written on April lath, in a hospital in France, where Sergeant Goodwin ,was an inmate along with a large number of Canadians, whom he describes as " a line lot." Tie speaks highly of the hospital in which he</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Twice Wounded.</head>
        <p>Twice Wounded.</p>
        <p>"I had a painful day when I got hit. You see a chap can't be taken from the trenches in the daytime. If you get hit in the morning, as I did, you have to wait until night to be re- moved. . . . I am going on fine. I am in a nice open-air hospital, and am being well looked after. . . . This is the second time I have been hit. I was hit the first time in the knee by a piece of shrapnel. I doctored it my- self. . . . The other day I was on a general court-martial over a chap who had shot his finger off, . . I was writing home when they came and told me r was for England, and you can imagine my delight. .. . Last night I managed to, walk to a concert on a pair of crutches."</p>
        <p>Sergeant-Major of Warrington C.L.B.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Goodwin was actively engaged with the Warrington Battalion of the Church Lads' Brigade, of which he is the Sergeant-Major. He is twenty-one years of age. At the age of thirteen he joined the St. Paull; Company of the Warrington Battalion of the Church Lads' Brigade. Later he•became band-instructor and band-sergeant. The excellence 6r) the bugle band of the company is largely( due to his efforts. He holds a commemorative medal for being one of the two officers who represented the battalion in London on the occasion of the Coronation of King George V. Educated at Hamilton-street school, he subsequently served an apprenticeship to Mr. Ralph Johnson, cabinetmaker, Bridge-street, Warrington, re- maining in his employ until the outbreak of the war. He is in C Company of the Warring- ton Territorials. He accomplished excellent work at the Front before being temporarily incapacitated by a bullet wound in the right thigh. He was sitting in a dug-out when a bullet came through the roof.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>PRIVATE JOSEPH LLOYD.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JOSEPH LLOYD.</p>
        <p>Reassuring Letter To Ills Parents.</p>
        <p>During the week-end Mr. and Mrs. F. Lloyd, of 1, Eustace-street, Warrington, received the following notification of the fact that their son, Private Joseph Lloyd, a. Warrington Terri- torial, had been wounded : "7th Field Ambulance, British Expeditionary Force.—l saw your boy in hospital this morn- ing. He was slightly wounded in the head yesterday (April 13th), but appears very cheer- ful considering. There is no reason for special anxiety. No doubt you will hear from him directly. He sends his love.—(Rev.) J. H. BAYNHAM, Chaplain to the Forces." Hopes to be Sent to England. A day or two later came a letter from Private Lloyd to his mother. He says : " I am just sending a few lines to say that I am much better. My wound is healing up nicely, and I hope to be sent over to England very soon. I am very comfortable here and am getting everything I want. They are very kind to me. I got a letter from you just be- fore I was wounded. I am glad to hear that you are all well. Don't worry a bit about me, because I ant quite all right." At the foot of the letter is a note :—" I have written this letter at your son's dictation. He looks well and is on the way to recovery. I am in charge of the Y.M.C.A. in this district. JAS. A. LITTLE." -</p>
        <p>This letter was sent from the Itawal Pindi B.G. Hospital, Boulogne, and two more fol- lowed from the same address, one from the senior nursing sister at to hospital, and the other from the Church of England Chaplain. The former says:— "He is not so well to-day-. I am writing to tell you, everything is being done for him that can be done, and we hope he will improve. If there is any change in his condition I shall write and let you know at once." The signa- ture is that of )(lies C. L. Warrack.</p>
        <p>The Church of England Chaplain (the Rev. Arthur Longden). writing for Private Lloyd,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>PRTVATB J. LLOYD. Wounded.</head>
        <p>PRTVATB J. LLOYD. Wounded.</p>
        <p>street; Jim Lloyd, of Stockton Heath; and A. and W. Hodson, of Liverpool-road. Private Lloyd has been wounded in the left side of the head.</p>
        <p>,' ' • , . ' - ........ .. , ';'''',;::::,:c5,•.t.i;:...%.0?*W-; .',.ki,......'.; . . 2 ~.;:,,,,,-:*,,..- -. . ...-.'• -'..5.b,,,,,,,.: ' .., . , , * . . ; I ; *, 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>PRIVATE THOMAS DALTON.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE THOMAS DALTON.</p>
        <p>Tried Three Times Before He Was Accepted.</p>
        <p>Despite the fact that thirty-eight years had passed over his head and that he had served a term as a Regular in the Royal Lancaster Regiment, when the present war broke out Private Thomas Dalton offered himself for active service. At first he asked to be allowd to re-join his old regiment, but he was refused re-admission because of defective teeth. Noth- ing daunted, he again offered himself for active service, but again met with refusal. A third time he tried, and this time—he offered his services to the 4th (Territorial) Battalion of the South Lancashire Aegiment—he was passed by the doctor, and he duly underwent, war training with the first detachment to leave Warrington. Fought Through the Boer War. Private Dalton is an old campaigner as com- pared• with most of his comrades-in-arms, for,- with the Royal Lancasters, he served through- out pkactically the whole of the Boer War, obtaining the medal struck in commemoration of that wai. and several bars. When his Army service expired he returned to his native town and for some two years worked at the Old Foundry, in Foundry-street. He is extremely popular amongst his wollmates, who describe him as "a real good sort," and, having musi- cal tastes, he devoted a considerable portion of his leisure to the Catholic Philharmonic Society. Private Dalton is unmarried and he made his home with relatives at 112, For- ster-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Shot Whilst Cooking His Dinner.</head>
        <p>Shot Whilst Cooking His Dinner.</p>
        <p>He was wounded in the left 'shoulder and fore-arm on April 13th, on vhich day two fellow "Terriers" were also hit. At the earliest possible opportunity he was sent across to Eng- land for treatment and he is now making satisfactory recovery from his hurts at Run- corn Vicarage, which has been kindly plktced at the disposal of the Red Cross authorities as a temporary hospital. Writing to a relative re his injuries, he says:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <p>PRIVATE T. DALTON. Wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL_ ALBERT WOODS.</p>
        <p>As a Volunteer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <p>with his rifle—and is, indeed, considered one of the best shots of his company.</p>
        <p>Son of the late Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Woods, the Lance-Corporal was born in Napier-street; and under Mr. Dewhurst's headmastership received his education at the National Schools —at which his son now attends. His daughter has just left Fairfield Schools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>PRI% ATE F. H. PARKINSON.</head>
        <p>PRI% ATE F. H. PARKINSON.</p>
        <p>Modestly Omitted To Write Home</p>
        <p>About Ws Wound.</p>
        <p>Curiously enough, Private Frank Hodson Parkinson—who has lived for two years with his sister, Mrs. Joseph Miller, at Smith's Cottages, Knntsford-road, Grappenhall, and is the son of Mr. John Parkinson, of China-lane, and the Borough Surveyor's Department, Town Hall— has not written to his Warrington relations of his hurt. The news locally was first received on Thursday morning at the Drill Hall, when the second official list of the 4th South Lanca- shire Territorial casualties included: Private F. H. Parkinson, A Company; bullet wound in left side of the neck; received April 11th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Shot After Church Parade.</head>
        <p>Shot After Church Parade.</p>
        <p>An officer writing this week says : I believe our casualties are not so heavy this time, although several have unfortunately lost their lives, one rather a sad case, happening as it did. On Sunday evening, after a glorious hot day, with a lovely sunset, our Major suggested a small church service in the trench, which he conducted. We sang "Fight the good fight" at the conclusion of what I felt was the most im- pressive and heartfelt service I ever attended. No sooner had the service finished and the men on the return to their posts than we were startled to see a man named Parkinson shot, the bullet passing first right through the sandbag wall. He fell within a few yards of where I had been seated. STORY OF HIS LIFE.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday Private Parkinson's age will be 25. In peace times he is employed in Messrs. Crosfield's export department office—to which he has belonged since leaving the Wycliffe Schoolst where he was taught under Mr. Alfred Corney'S headmastership, at the age of four- teen. He formerly attended Friars' Green Independent Methodist Chapel, but more recently he has worshipped at the Grappen- hall Parish Church. Recently he has devoted his spare. time 'to the study of commerce—in which he has already passed several examina- tions "en route" for a diploma. Born at Egremont, the Private has from in- fancy lived in Warrington, and is a nephew of Mr. John Parkinson, photographer. His mother passed away over seven years ago. . To the Territorial Army Service Corps he had belonged for five years, during which period he attended each annual camp. In September he joined the South Lancashire Regiment, and went out to the Front immediately after four</p>
        <p>[ place, and it is dangerous—for you cannot see the Germans, but they can see you. "He was a nice fellow, always joking. But I suppose it had to be; and if anybody &amp;sues home safe he is a lucky fellow. For going in and out of the trenches is very dangerous; last night the bullets were coming past us—but we all got out safe, thank God. .. ." Fell at Comrade's Feet.</p>
        <p>Pritate S. Miller's word says: "You will have heard about one of Leslie's sons (from Mersey- street) being killed. He was talking to Ide ittSt as he was getting on the parapet to fire; and he fell at my feet, shot through the head. I felt sorry for _him. Ile was such a nice chap, and had only been promoted to Lance-Corporal about three weeks: All of us liked him. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <p>PRIVATE F. 'H. PARKINSON.</p>
        <p>Wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>"ONE OF OUR BEST MEN."</head>
        <p>"ONE OF OUR BEST MEN."</p>
        <p>Captains. Tribute to Late Lance- Corporal Leslie.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Leslie, of 50, Mersey-street, War- rington, the parents of Lance-Corporal Wallace Leslie, of the Warrington Territorials—who, as was reported in our last issue, was killed in action on April I2th—have received many letters expressing sympathy with them in the loss of so gallant a son. One of the letters comes from the deceased Lance-Corporal's Company Com- mander—Captain Reginald Smith—who writing on the day that young Leslie was killed, says:— I very much regret to have to inform you that your son, No. 1895, Lance-Corporal Wallace Leslie, was killed in the trenches this morning. Just Fired at the Foe When Fatal Bullet Sped. At six o'clock he had just fired a shot at the enemy, when he was struck in the head by a bullet. lat once went to him. He was quite unconscious and I saw that his case was hopeless. He died in about half an hoar, having suffered no pain. Your son was one of, our beat men, and his loss will be greatly felt. . . The offieers, N.C.Cr.'s and men of " D " Company desire to express to you their deepest sympathy. ALWAYS BEHAVED AS A GENTLEMAN. A second letter was received from Private C. E. Preece, of the Signal Section of the 4th (Territorial) Battalion of the South Lancashire Regiment, who is at present quartered in the South of fl.ngland, and who writes:—" I am taking the liberty of writing to you offering You my heartfelt sympathy in your irretrievable loss. was Wal's chum Auring our stay, in Scotland, and continued to be such down here until the battalion left for Prance. I was lift behind in hospital here; otherwise I should have been,with him at the front. . . . I feel that 1 Aare lost my best friend and chum. . . . and I don't think I shall ever meet one to equal him in my estimation. He always behaved as a gentleman to every one, and was well liked in "D" Company, to which we both belonged. Whilst in Scotland we both made some excellent friends, whom, I have no doubt, Wal. told you about. I am sure that they will be upset too, for we all liked him.. . . I sincerely hope that you will be consoled a little by the thought that he died one of the finest deaths a man can die; laying down his life for his country. . . . My association with Wal. give S me the impres- sion that he was a worthy son." COUSIN VISITS DEAD SOLDIER'S GRAVE. A cousin of the deceased Lance-Corporal's, Driver Jno Stockton, of the Army Service Corps, attached to the Liverpool Scottish, writing to Mr. and Mrs. Leslie, 85s:— "Our poor Wallace has met his fate, having been killed in the trenches between 5.30 and six o'clock on the morning of April 12th. They came down to tell me and I can assure you that it was a big blow to hear that I had -lost the best friend / had out here. He was buried at 11 o'clock on Wednesday morning. I went down to see him for the last time, but I was just too late, for he had been laid to rest. He sleeps in a peaceful churchyard in the village where we are at present. I learn that a nice service was conducted at the graveside by the Battalion's own chaplain. . . • He was laid to rest by Guest (of Napier-street) and Walker (the postman) and they have made 'a fitting grave for him. I met his Company-Sergeant- Major and he asked me to go down and see him, promising if I did so to tell me something more abont Wallace's death."</p>
        <p>"A GOOD CHUM, AND, WITHAL, A MAN." Another glowing tribute to the memory of the deceased is paid by Company-Quartermaster- Sergeant W. Tynan, of the 4th South Lancs. who in the course of a letter to mutual friend; in Dunfermline, says:—" It was during a smart rally-up by the enemy that Wallace got an unlucky one in the head. I understand he was a particular friend of yours. The respect you had for him was shared by each man of the company, for he was always genial—a good chum and; withal, a man." _ _</p>
        <p>Private Guest, of Napier-street, Warrington, who helped to fashion the Lance-Corporal's grave and to lay him at rest in it, has written home speaking in most appreciative manner of his old friend, the deceased, and expressing warm regret at his demise. The parents have also received a letter from Mr. Arthur Down, son of Mr. T. J. Down, in whose band the deceased used to play. Mr. Down expressed his sympathy and also pays's. tribute to the deceased's many excellent quali- ties, and to his popularity in the band whilst a member of it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>NATIONAL RESERVE.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL RESERVE.</p>
        <p>Over 300 Men Already Drafted From</p>
        <p>Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>ANCIFIIEft COMPANY BEING RAISED.</head>
        <p>ANCIFIIEft COMPANY BEING RAISED.</p>
        <p>Over three hundred National Reservists have left Warrington already for duty in various parts of the country, and another company is now being raised at the Drill Hall. This is to be the 3rd Supernumerary. Company of the 2nclk4th South Lancashire Regiment. WELL-KNOWN LOCAL MEN AS OFFICERS. Two welt-known local gentlemen have been appointed as officers, viz., Captain E. F. Will- cocks, second son of the late headmaster of the Warrington Grammar School, and Lieutenant W. H. Bellian. Captain Willcocks joined the Ist Volunteer Battalion of the South Lancashire Regiment in 1897, being gazetted captain in 1902. During the South African War he went out in command of the fourth draft of War-. rington ;Volunteers. Captain Willcocks also saw service throughout the Zulu Rebellion in 1906; being appointed captain in the Inanda Divi- sion of the Colonial Forces, whiah position he held for four years. Two years in the Natal Militia Artillery followed; and he finally re- tired in 1912.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant W. H. Bellian is well know* in Volunteering circles, having done twelve years' service with the Ist Volunteer Battalion of the South Lancashire Regiment. Since the out- break of the present war Lieutenant BeWan has been closely associated with several loon/ war charity organisations, for which he has per- formed valuable services, and his help will be greatly missed now that he has entered on a more active sphere in his country's defence. The third officer of the company is Seeonc/ Lieutenant Hatfield, who is well known in New- ton-le-Willows and among the members of the Lancashire Hussars Yeomanry. Among the non-commissioned officers ap- pointed are Colour-Sergeant McNamara, Hon. Quartermaster and Sergeant J. R. Jones, and Sergeant T. 11. Ashworth, all with long and meritorious service. _</p>
        <p>Amongst the other non-commissioned officers of the company will be Sergeant D. Griffiths (late of the R.G.A.), Sergeant J. Murray and Sergeant Allan (both of the Ist Battalion of the South Lancashire Regiment). All three saw active service during the South African War. PAY BEGINS ON ENROLMENT.</p>
        <p>The orderly room at the Drill Hall is open for recruiting each dtv from 9 a.m. to 9 p.m., and men can there be enrolled on pay immediately. It is expected that the new company will be completed and equipped in a few weeks, when it will be dispatched to its post of duty. During the last few weeks drafts to the extent of 34 men have been supplied to the coin- panies.which are already on duty.</p>
        <p>A THEATRICAL SECRET. HOW ,TO POLISH YOUR TEETH. Actresses obtain that brilliancy and snowy white- ness of their teeth with AURA DENTAL PASTE.</p>
        <p>All diseolonrations and stains will disappear from the teeth after using.. Prom all Chemists, or send P.O. 9d. for Large Tube, or Two Penny Stamps for Sample Tube, to AURA, 3, DENMAN-ST., LONDON, S.E.</p>
      </div>
