      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>DI',.:::TRICT MEWS</head>
        <p>DI',.:::TRICT MEWS</p>
        <p>LIYMM</p>
        <p>• Justices in Attendance.—On the Bench at Wednesday morning's Petty Sessions there were: Messrs. A. Watkin (in thern chair), A. W. Callender, T. J. Ridgway and W. P. Rylands.</p>
        <p>Forthcoming Anniversary.—To-morrow at the Independent Methodist Church, Cherry-lane, will be celebrated the Sunday School anniver- sary. The preacher is to be Mr. Thomas Worthington, of Wigan.</p>
        <p>Concert For Red Cross: Repetition.—The concert given in .the Statham Schoolroom on Tuesday, April 6th, will he repeated on Mon- day evening in the Congregational School- room to aid the Lymm Red. Cross Hospital.</p>
        <p>Surely a Ffecordl--Since the outbreak of the' war, Mrs. Mary E. Clare, of Booth's Hill, has ; received no less than a hundred and three letters and postcards from her three soldier-sons--Ser- geant Harold, Corporal Ernest and Private: Joseph.</p>
        <p>Further Tribute to Private P. Leigh.—Private Wallace McHugh, of C Company of the sth Cheshire, writes - from the firing line to a friend: "I expect you heard about Peter Leigh being killed. Wasn't it sad? But I suppose it was God's will. .. ." -</p>
        <p>Councillor's Mother Dies.—We would express our sincere sympathy with our greatly respected townsman, Councillor R. H. Gotobed, in the loss he has sustained by the death of his mother —which sad event took place on Tuesday after- noon at her home in Bishop's Castle, Shropshire.</p>
        <p>sth Cheshires Gallant Attack.—'There has been an attack on the German trenches here," writes Private Wallace Mcflugh from the Front, to Mr. Frank Leach, of Grappenhall, on April 18th, "and they captured some. The Ger- mans must have lost a lot; the big guns have been going all the time. We are having nice weather at present, 'and are under canvas."</p>
        <p>Successful Display.—Complete suocess ,at- tended the displays given at the Drill Hall on Wednesday evening—when the Lads' Club gave a grand exhibition of gymnastics, the Scouts contributing a fire-lighting parade. A good at- tendance marked the function.—On Sunday morning the Scouts attended the Parish Church. --lAA night they met together and partook of supper.</p>
        <p>More About Easter Services.—Sergeant Clare's latest budget of news from the Front says: " I was glad to hear of the services at our .church going off so well at Easter, and was very pleased to know you had Mr. Dempster. We all remember that memorable service before we left Lymm. We have just returned from our church service—which was very impressive.. . . Please give kindest regards to all our Lymm friends... "</p>
        <p>It's a Small World!—" Please remember me to the East Surrey Sergeant." writes Sergeant Clare last week to his wife. He refers to Sergeant Prentice (who—as stated in our last issue—left "Brookfield," the Lymm Red Cross Hospital, last Friday). Sergeants Prentice and Clare were in the trenches together when the fornier had to leave, suffering from frost-bite; and it is a curious coincidence that he should have been sent to recuperate in Sergeant Clare's native village. Former Fustian-Cutter Dies.—At 8 o'clock or. Thursday morning, the death took place at Newfields of Mr. William Jennings, from heart failure, at the age of 56. Mr. Jennings had Wen under the care of Dr. De Jong, and until the failure of his health two years ago worked at fustian-cutting for Mr. Adams.—He leaves a widow, and six sons and daughters—two of whom are married. The funeral is to take place on Monday afternoon at the Parish Church.</p>
        <p>Temporary Authority Granted.—Samuel Ridgway, of 104, London-road, Hazel-grove, Stockport, applied at Wednesday's Petty Sessions for temporary authority at ,The Jolly Thresher. He said he had no previous ex- perience in the work, haying been for ten yeitrs general manager of the Co-operative Society.— Superintendent Sutherland informed the Bench that the man was perfectly satisfactory.—The Chairman (to applicant): The house is a little difficult to manage sometimes, and you will have to be careful. The application is granted.</p>
        <p>Late Mr. Stephen Ravenscroft.-Itt the Parish Church on Wednesday aftern/ron, the Rector conducted the funeral of the late Mr. Stephen Ravenscroft—whose death took place on Thursday of last week at his home, 4, Newfields. Dr. de Jong was his medical adviser, and at the end he was also attended by Dr. L. Thorpe. Mr. Ravenscroft, whose age was about 45, had suffered for several years, but declined rapidly since last year—when (following influenza) ,his eye was extracted at the MaricheSter Institute. Ile worked as a farm labourer for Mr. Henry Gibbon.—The funeral arrangements were carried out by Mr. W. G. Groves, and on the grave relatives laid beautiful wreaths.—The deceased leaves five brothers, all of whom attended the funeral.</p>
        <p>" Tommy's " Doggie.—Sam Stanbank, teams- man, of Reddish-lane, Heatley, was summoned at Wednesday's Petty Sessions for having this month kept at Lymm a dog without a licenc4.— His wife appeared on his behalf, and the Chair- man said the Bench would make an exception this time as he was working and labour was so scarce.—P.C. Robinson said he found the dog in Reddish-lane, and found it came from Stan- banks. Next day he 'saw the man, who admitted he had not got a licence.—Mrs. Stan- bank stated that the dog belonged to a friend who had gone to the Front with the Welsh Fusiliers, saying he would come for it when on furlough. He had been away since August sth, and it never occurred to them about the licence. She now had one.—The fine was five shillings, including costs.</p>
        <p>Bible Work at Home and Abroad.—The annual meeting of the Lymm Branch of the British and Foreign Bible Society was held on Tuesday in the Primitive Methodist School- room. Mrs. E. Ridgway, the president, occupied the chair. The secretary and treasurer having presented their reports, a highly interesting and instructive lecture was given by the- Rev. T. A. Wolfendale on "Bible Work In and Around Constantinople." Latitern illustrations de- picted the society's work in connection With the war. The Secretary (Mr. D. Mort) was re- elected, as also was Mr. R. Hewitt (treasurer). At the close of the meeting (which was very well attended) a. hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the Chairman and to the Lecturer, on the motion of Mr. Henry Brown, seconded by Mr. T. Nash. A collection was taken in aid of the society's funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>MORE BELGIANS ARRIVE.</head>
        <p>MORE BELGIANS ARRIVE.</p>
        <p>/ Twenty-nine Refugees Now At "Beeehwood."</p>
        <p>'At " Beechwood," the LylllM home of the Belgian refugees, have this week arrived sixteen more of those unfortunates—eleven of them being children. There are now twenty-nine in the home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <p>1 i 4 :',. i...„., -,:_-,......,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>PLENTY OF FIGHTING.</head>
        <p>PLENTY OF FIGHTING.</p>
        <p>Of Which Germans Got Worst.</p>
        <p>Corporal Jack H. Moston writes home to his aunt, Mrs. G. Davies, of Booth's Hill: "Just a few lines to thank you for the cakes, which we received last night. I am very glad to say that all of us are keeping • well, and are having lovely weather. We are having a rest now, but go in the trenches to-night for four days. "We are at a.place where there is plenty of ighting, and where some of the men of our hest regiments have fallen. There has been a big battle hero this week-end; I shall never forget hearing so many guns going off. I think the War has only just begun. There must have been thousands of Germans put out, and it must have been .a big blow to them. I think if they have many ~more good doings like this one they will be glad to 'give in. "You will see that I have been promoted. . . Allis well with us. . .</p>
        <p>EASTER SATURDAY SCENE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Elderly Couple's Separation NA ill Make</head>
        <p>Elderly Couple's Separation NA ill Make</p>
        <p>Husband Return To NVork.</p>
        <p>At the Petty Sessions on Wednesday, Jane Rowles, of Booth's Hill, Lymm, applied for a private summons against her husband, John, for an alleged aggravated assault on her at Lymm.—Defendant pleaded not guilty, and stated they had been married about 41 years.</p>
        <p>" Sent Her Flying." Mrs. Rowles said she had eleven children, a: over sixteen.</p>
        <p>Her husband, she continued, had behaved very badly to her all his life. On Easter Saturday at ten to eleven at night he came home not sober. She-told him he ought to be ashamed of using such language as, he had come out w.ith, and he then struck her on the shoulder, and ' sent her flying against the fire- grate." Her arm was black from elbow to wrist. On many previous occasions he had pushed her about. James Rowles, son of complainant, Corro- borated. His father was half drunk. / - P.C. Robinson said that at half-past eleven he was brought to the house. Complainant wos injured as described, and he had great difficulty in preventing the man from assaulting his son and daughter also. Mrs. Rowles was a good, hard-working woman, and "kept the house going." Defendant: I didn't dci, it wilfully. Her son might have done it in tile squabble;.he did as much as I did, and my daughter had a poker about 2ft. flin. long. "Why Don't You Work." _.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. A. Watkin) : Why don't you work? Defendant: Nobody will employ me at my age —64.</p>
        <p>The Chairman Yes, 'they will. Any man who wants to get work now can do so. You could work on the fields, couldn't you? This is a time when 'any man can find work. lam sorry your wife—a hard-working. woman—should have a husband like foul (To Mrs. Rowles): If 40 fine him it comes out of your pocket. (To de- fendant): You could find lots of work, and keep your family in comfort if you liked. To fine you would be a punishment upon your wife. She has been a good wife to you,— Defendant: Yes, she has.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: But I am afraid you have not been a good husband to her; she has had to bring you here after forty years of married life! You must go to prison for seven days' hard 'labour; and after you come out you can find work. We must put a stop to these lazy men!</p>
        <p>Separation Granted.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Bowles applied, for a separation. She did not think he would work; she had kept him for twelve years, and he had Rot done a day's work all that time. He had never complained to her of a wasting disease. Her husband had worked as a bricksetters' labourer. Defendant: It is only about seven years since I left. I was asking Mr. John Holt about it the other day. The Chairman (to Mrs..,Rowles): What has he ever "brought you in?" Mrs. Rowles : Never more than fifteen shil- lings. 141st Saturday I had 3s. 10d. from him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>What He _Must Pay.</head>
        <p>What He _Must Pay.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (to defendant): You will have to pay her four shillings a week when you come out of .prison, and will have to keep yourself. There is plenty of work to he found.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>KIND LI-MAI "TERRIERS"</head>
        <p>KIND LI-MAI "TERRIERS"</p>
        <p>Help Those In Firing-Line During</p>
        <p>Their Rest.</p>
        <p>Private Percy Clare, wrote home two days ago: "I was pleased to hear that you had sent to Mrs. Hammond a photo, for she was very kind to us when we were at Stonemarket; and she is very good to us yet—she has sent us word that we can have anything, no matter what it is.</p>
        <p>"Well, we are out of the trenches for a few days; ,but by the time you get this we shall be going in again. The time seems to fly when we are out of the trenches, and when out we have to go up to the trenches on what they call fatigue. We go digging sometimes, and other times go to the trenches for the boys—we take them things. such as footboards and sandbags to repair the trenches.</p>
        <p>Germans Can't Stop "Tommy." "Please thank Annie Percival for the cake; it was grand. And the cocoa and milk will come in when we are in the trenches, as we can get a drink now and then—even, if th‘Germans are near to us; they will never stop' the " Tommies " from having a drink of tea! . . ."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>"A NEAR SHAVE."</head>
        <p>"A NEAR SHAVE."</p>
        <p>Rut Private Guest Came Through All</p>
        <p>Private Jack Guest, writing on April 19th, to a friend, says he is in the best of health, and continues: "We are having very nice weather just at present; I only hope it will keep so. "The guns are going at it again. They have been going at it since Saturday night without a stop—we can hardly tell if they are our guns going off or the German shells bursting.. It is one continual buzz of shells, one road or the other. We had a Sunday yesterday; I hope we never have another the same. We could see the shells bursting on the trenches—it looked awful. . . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Tiring Work.</head>
        <p>Tiring Work.</p>
        <p>"I am feeling, very tired now. We have not had much rest these four days; we haven't to go to sleep in the day, and we can't sleep at night because of the guns. . . . We are going to the trenohes again to-night, but it is not so bad for us this time—we are only in the sup. norts.</p>
        <p>"I had a near shave yesterday; a piece of shell dropped a few inches from me. I have got the piece, and am going to bring it home with me just to show you what, it is like. . . I shan't be able to write again for four days after this, owing to the fact that we are going into the trenches. I pray to God that He will bring us all back. . . .</p>
        <p>"Four German shells have just burst, one after the other. Our guns are replying."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>LYIVIM TERRITORIALS' ROLL OF HONOUR.</head>
        <p>LYIVIM TERRITORIALS' ROLL OF HONOUR.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER CASUALTY.</p>
        <p>Particulars of another casualty amongst the local " Terriers " have this week reached Lymm, the latest unfortunate who was wounded, being Private Henry Cadman. Corporal Henry Barber has 'bad a " miracu ions" escape from being wounded.. The other three Lymm wounded warriors are progressing very satisfactorily at their respec- tive hospitals, Private Richard Shaw having had a bullet extracted from his leg. On Monday night the Lymm men---after four days' respite "in the valley "---re-entered the firing line and remained there till Thursday night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>LATEST SERVICE IN TRENCHES.</head>
        <p>LATEST SERVICE IN TRENCHES.</p>
        <p>In word to his wife,- received on Wednesday, Sergeant H. Clare said: "1 am still enjoying the iciest of health, and so are all our boys, who pre also in fine spirits. The weather continues to be beautiful, as I have told- you before. It is marvellous how cheerful and contented the boys all are. . ." Writing from the firing line on Tuesday, the 13th, the Sergeant remarked: "This is our second day in the fresh firing line. I am very pleased to tell you that all goes on well with us. We shall he relieved on Thursday night for .another four days' rest, and I trust during the next two days we shall have the good luck to have no casualties. . .</p>
        <p>"Whilst I am writing the Belgian artillery are shelling the German trenches about sixty yards from us. Shells are whistling over us every second. These are very good trenches we are in. . . . Since writing .my last letter I am glad to .say that the weather has changed, and it is now bgautiful and warm. The trenches are nice and dry—which is quite a treat, for the mud really has been dreadful. . . ." Sending home word on Thursday week, the Sergeant declared: "I thought I was going. to send clean sheet as regards casualties, but am sorry to say Harry Cadman is slightly injured. . Harry Barber had a 'miraculous escape. . . . Otherwise we are all quite well, and came out of the trenchesiunhurt. . . . The weather was lovely the whole four days. ,</p>
        <p>"We felt very tired when we readied head- quarters, but after a rest and a good breakfast we are ourselves again. We are resting now till Monday night—when we go in again. . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>PRIVATE HENRY CADMAN.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE HENRY CADMAN.</p>
        <p>Arm Grand By Bullet Which</p>
        <p>Shattered Ms Rifle.</p>
        <p>The news of Private Cadman's misfortune on Thursday week first arrived at Lymin on Tues- day night, when a field postcard from him</p>
        <p>PRIVATE HENRY CADMAN.</p>
        <p>reached his sister (Mrs. S. Owen, of Brookfield Cottages, Booth's Hill). It said :</p>
        <p>I have been admitted into hospital wounded, and am going on well. Letter follows at first</p>
        <p>opportunity.- April 17th.</p>
        <p>A week before his last-received communica- tion had said he was quite well, and acknow- ledged the receipt of a welcome parcel. Sergeant H. Clare writes to Mr. F. E. Evans: I am sorry to. tell ,you of Private Harry Cadman being wounded.. A bullet struck his rifle and shattered it, then crazed his arm. The hurt. is only of a slight nature, and I believe we shall have him back in the course</p>
        <p>of a few days. In word sent to his wife on Thursday of last week, Sergeant Clare told her: "I am sorry to say Harry Cadman is slightly injured. It happened about 10 o'clock last night. We were cutting a drain when the bullet struck his rifle and shattered it. Then it grazed his arm. I don't think he will go in hospital [the wounded Private has, however, been conveyed there] but he will be with us in a day or two—at least,' I hope so! But we never know how a simple bruise turns oul." , •</p>
        <p>Private Percy Clare's latest word home says: "We all came out of the trenches all right; but we had to go digging behind them, and Cadman got caught in .the arm. It is not so bad—it burned him more than anything. lie is gone away; I .hope he gets home with it. ."</p>
        <p>.. d ~,.•,.,:i-.:•,..,.....!.•:,-,,,•••••• •,:',.•:'-'.:!!..•:7''',..71:'....:1.:7,1-,.M.b7llit'ZiAlligt 1;;;;,......,.'''.:;.::i-',.• • ' .. .......: '''''........,;''''.771:::#;110,.),1,•;.„....,:::, .• - ''''' '':'•:ig.V.-':"1. 1 . ...' .7. '''''. : . -..i -• . ',.,....i.V. !,' , , ~... . .. .:4 .... . ~ f,:5?....:.:*.,.4A,4 ' ... ' ' •••'.',"0' '-‘ - • -,, .•• „v.. ~..,..: , - •:• . a.,, ~..:,,,ge•-'4:"•..17!;;;•••..--. :1'.'r1.4.1 •-,:..--„,,,..„-::::-_,;_,,,,•,,.-: ,::_.. .;:„:..,-.,,,,y0, .:-.., '••••:::...,•-.;,:a,,,..-... ..,..„„.. f.i.:,.:lir:-.7\ •••••••• ',,,';:z.--1.,c::::••• ..::.,. -.,.' -:::::1%:k.,.... I.4o'.el.'*'°frill''':;,;"'.'.'''''.'7.l.•.:::.:::.,;.2l!t:l'lli''-Z,i,:::;',',:t.:i. ~..,,;..ir,..;.,....;:;,:;,::w..i.,4::::.....,..0..,,:...!:„,,,:..,,,,,!::,.:.:..-:::,.,..::,:.5.‘,..i..,....,..,.t,::;i:.,:i,,i,,•,,..:::11r: : ~..i.....:•;.;..,.•,::•,;•:?,.i..::;,ti:,...,:;.:,,,,-:,,..,..,:i:!.&lt;,:.::,i.,,,••;:,ik;:;:,...i,;::,.,:.!,,,,!::,:-i,,..:til.:;;:i::(.::::::;,,.:::;;!..:,:o.,.•,:?;....., , .44,r, .....„....„.„,,,,,•...,..,:•-:-5...0.,,,,:,--.--,..„11:4,,;:_i_,.?„. ,: . •:•:•.......;:„.....4,::,,,%:.,.,..,:.:i,....-.-...-:-..,,:::w5,,,0,.----..--.,- .•.„. ..,."....44,4,.'-', ......-1..a.,:-,.:......i.,...:::.,,;:ii,,,;----...,,,, . ii#:,il,-10e..,:.--,:-.•-',..,..:.:54),,,,.:4-.-jiiiki.'..':',rii...,-.:-..');,?&gt;,'.,fr?,.:::-....,-..-- .-, , ~. Tr..-.,....,:.::,:::',. :3';‘:.:.!.!:lri,.;,l:i::!•:•:kilittillZ.iititil!"..:li4:--I'..'''' .V.;:t:.;!:' ' •,..,..„,,_. ~,:,,,,,,,•:...,..:-:?,,,:,,...„,,,4,e,m.,„,,,,;:..,:....•• _ „..-„,,, •• _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>ALL ABOUT HIM.</head>
        <p>ALL ABOUT HIM.</p>
        <p>Private•Cadman's age is. 21 years, and prior to offering for active service he worked as a bricksetter for Mr. John Holt, of. Cherry-lane— which employment he has had since leaving the village school seven years ago. The sth Che- shires he joined as a Territorial five or six years ago, and had attended three ,of their annual camps at Aberystwyth and other Welsh towns. Unfortunately, at his fourth camp last year he was taken ill, and had to spend three week § in the Chester Military Hospital. He is the orphan son of Mr. and Mrs. Henry Cadman, his father having passed away so recently as November last. Private Cadman was born in Lymm, and was a regular attender at/ the Congregational. Sun- day school—as well as at the church. Until recently he was also an enthusiastic member of the Lads' Club. In peace times he hues with his step-mother on Booth's Hill; It is interesting to note that Private Cad- man's father also worked as a bricklayer under Mr. Holt—for over 45 years!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>)1t tl. HENRY BARBER. Ills Pocket-Book Saved Ills Life.</head>
        <p>)1t tl. HENRY BARBER. Ills Pocket-Book Saved Ills Life.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday morning word came from Cor- poral Henry Barber—now at the • Fron.t with the C Compank, sth Cheshires—to his mother at Rush Green and to his sisters, per Mrs. Wilfred Phillips, at Brookfield Cottages, Booth's Hill. To the latter he say, under date April 16th : "My dear sisters,—Thank you for the parcel. Sorry I have not answered before now, but the day I got it we went to the troches for four days and only came out last night. And we can't get any letters through when in the trenches.</p>
        <p>I may tell you I have had a narrow escape. A bullet hit me on the right side, but my pocket-book (in which I have 'a letter and some postcards) turned it off. \I will send you an envelope it has gone through. It upset me a little, but I am quite all right again. The bullet just scratched my side, but no damage was done. So don't let it upset you whatever.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Clare wiites to a Lymm gentleman: "Harry Barber had a marvellous-- escape. A bullet went through his coat pocket and barely grazed, his chest. It was the fact of him liftvinc, his pocket-book and letter in his pocket which saved him, for they turned the bullet off. He is quite all right—just suffered a little from shock at the time. . . ."</p>
        <p>On Tuesday of last • week the Sergeant also tells Mrs. Clare, who resides at the Drill hail: "Harry Barber had a miraculous escape. A</p>
        <p>bullet went clean through some letters he had in his coat pocket."</p>
        <p>CORPORAL BARBER'S LIFE STORY.</p>
        <p>Born at Lymm about 23 years ago, Corporal Henry Barber 'attended the village school and the Parish Church and unday school through his boyhood. At the age of fourteen he Went to work at farming under Mr. S. W. Gould—with whom he remained for a considerable period. In peace times he now works, and has for many, years worked, at Lymm for the Ship Canal Company.</p>
        <p>'Formerly belonging to the Lads' Club Corporal Barber took a prominent place in its football team. At that time he had also joined the Cheshire Territorials, .and before the outbreak of war had attained the rank of lance-corporal. At Cambridge in September he was again. pri) moted to his present rank. Two days later the Corporal again wrote—to his nephew, Wilfred Phillips—saying that lui was quite all right.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>PRIVATE RICHARD SII.IAV.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE RICHARD SII.IAV.</p>
        <p>Keeps Bullet Taken From His Leg As</p>
        <p>Souvenir.</p>
        <p>IN, his mother at Booth's Hill, Private Richard Shaw again wrote from No. 4 General Hospital (British Expeditionary Force) on Thursday of -last week: I have had the bullet taken /out of my leg," he informed her. "and the (lector has given it to me as a souvenir. My leg is going on fine now. "‘ We are having very .nice weather at present, ,and it is very hot to-day; there are two or three aeroplanes up owing to its being so fine." On the fallowing day Private Shaw wrote: "I am in a very nice hospital, and it is a lovely place round here. We get plenty of visitors; they bring us chocolate, 4gafettes and plenty of nice books to read. But the chocolate is not as nice as ours, so don't forget .to send me some. . . It is lovely to-day. The sun is quite hot. You know it is warmer here than in England. . . . My leg is doing very well mow. The doctor got the bullet nut last-Satur- day. I shall. be able to show it to you. . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>PRIVATE JAMES TAYLOR.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JAMES TAYLOR.</p>
        <p>Use Of Left Arm Not Vet Reeo‘ered</p>
        <p>Private James Taylor, of 1, West Hyde, New- field—who was the first Lymm " Terrier " to be wounded, is, still in Lincoln General Hos- pital, where he is progressing satisfactorily, although he has no use in his left arm, through the muscles of which the bullet passed. On Tuesday and Wednesday the gallant Private was visited by his father, who found him very cheery and contented. It transpires that had the fatal bullet struck him an inch or two lower than it did it would have pierced his heart. Last week Private Taylor unfortun- ately caught a chill, which has caused for a. time his 'return to bed, and somewhat delayed his recovery. On Sunday morning the l'rivate sent home word that his mother had better not come to see 'him yet. He also paid a grateful tribute to the kindness of the Lymm people—many of whom had sent him very welcome gifts. The Private has also been visited upon two occasions by a neighbour—the daughter of Mr. Charles Blease, of "Newfield."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>CAPTAIN HARTLEY.</head>
        <p>CAPTAIN HARTLEY.</p>
        <p>Gloving Tribute,' From Sergeant am</p>
        <p>Privates.</p>
        <p>In word written to Mr., F. E:- Evans on the 18th, Sergeant Harold Clare—having referred to the wounding of Private. Cadman—eontinues : "Otherwise all our boys are quite sound and in perfect. health and spirits. It is a great pleasure for .me to tell you of their splendid work out here. Their bravery is 'magnificent, and we have performed some very daring actions— which only good, brave and steady men Could do. I feel sure you are very glad to know this. . .</p>
        <p>Teacher of Best Type. `.` In Captain Hartley we have a teacher of the best type—fearless! This accounts for the steadiness of our boys; he encourages us, no matter what duty we are called on to do, and his behaviour under fire is splendid. Our boys will follow him anywhere. No wonder they are a credit to Lymm ! Captain Hartley is worthy of the praise we give him; we will at all times support him tooth and nail. He will have a lot to tell if he has the luck to pull through. He has supporting him a very good officer in Lieut. Walker, who is our platoon commander, and a brave officer. He thinks the world of us Lymm boys. . . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>Tremendous Responsibility.</head>
        <p>Tremendous Responsibility.</p>
        <p>"Captain Hartley has a tremendous respon- sibility, and we all realise it is our bounden duty at all times to back him up. . . ." Private Joseph 'Clare writes: "There is no truth at all in the rumour that Captain Raft- ley has been killed. We don't want to lose him, for we think a lot of him V'</p>
        <p>In ninny 'other letters from the Front, Lymm "Tommies" pay glowing tributes to their cap- tain when they have occasion to mention him. With characteristic modesty, however, Captain Hartley declines the publication of his own letters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>UTTER DESOLATION.</head>
        <p>UTTER DESOLATION.</p>
        <p>I,3mm Lads' Respite Amid Scenes 01</p>
        <p>Destruction.</p>
        <p>In a letter from the trenches, dated April 14th, Private Percy Clare tells his mother (Mrs. G. Davies, of Booth's hill): "We are still alive and kicking. We are doing four days in the, trenches; we do two days in and two days in reserve. We have done two days in the trenches and now we are in the. dug-outs. These are lath), .huts dug in the ground, and we are thankful to get in them when we come out of the trenches; they are nice and warm. . . We are having better weather now; it is raining a bit at present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>Where Shells Do Not Drop.</head>
        <p>Where Shells Do Not Drop.</p>
        <p>"The dug-out are in the yalley. Shells and bullets are flying over the top of us, but they never drop in the valley—so we are all right. "You would be surprised to know where we are, but lam sorry we cannot tell you. But never mind! I think we shall be able to tell you all very soon now, as ,we have hopes of it being all over before long—the Way things are going on now. I think Kaiser ' Bill ' is getting 'fed up.' We are all 'fed up' with it, but you know it is our duty to be here.</p>
        <p>Everywhere Shelled.</p>
        <p>"It would have been awful if the Germans had got in England, and treated it as they have done this fine place . . . there is not a house or building standing that has not been shelled. So •we can thank God they are not in our dear old England. . . . Buck up; things will come right in the end ! . . ."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>" EXAMINER " AT THE FRONT.</head>
        <p>" EXAMINER " AT THE FRONT.</p>
        <p>mm " Tommy " Discusses ' Publica-</p>
        <p>lion of Casualties.</p>
        <p>Private JoKph Clare writes to his mother: "Thanks for the paper rlNhe_ ExaMiner" which I_got while in the trenches. . . . i</p>
        <p>suppose it has made some of the Lymm people realise what we have to go through—with seeing the photos of our killed and wounded comrades in the paper. It's no use saying much about our experiences, for it is just those that talk most who are most frightened of doing their share. . . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>What Upsets a Soldier Most.</head>
        <p>What Upsets a Soldier Most.</p>
        <p>"I hear you are ill again.. . . Try and be brave! It upsets .me more than anything when I hear of you being ill with worrying. I know it is hard, but cheer up! Look on the bright side of things, and take no notice of rumours !"</p>
        <p>Like r Many other Lymm "lads," Private Clare reads with delight "The Examiner" every week, being quick to appreciate the authen- ticity and completeness of its local war ser- vice.</p>
        <p>NO MAY QUEEN.</p>
        <p>But Children Will Probably Have Field Day Later On.</p>
        <p>EFFECT OF THE 'WAR.</p>
        <p>The village has done remarkably well in. collecting for the war funds, and I don't see how we could expect subscriptions for the May Queen.</p>
        <p>After all, everything depends upon the sub- scriptions.</p>
        <p>It is a great pity to have to "throw cold water" on the festival, but there can be no doubt that the thoughts of the people are not on this sort of thing.</p>
        <p>The above remarks were passed by different speakers at the annual general meeting in con- nection with the Lymm May Queen Festival— held on Monday evening in Mr. Daniels' Pavilion, Booth's Hill. They are representa- tive of .the general sentiments of the meeting, over which Mr. W. G. Jones (treasurer) pre- sided.</p>
        <p>The Treasurer presented the balance sheet of the • 1914 festival. Whilst not showing • the a mount to be carried forward to be so great as they.. could have wished, he said, the committee had been enabled to liquidate the debt of £l6 4s. 10d. standing against the old committee. The balance, remaining was £3 2s. 7d., thus showing a clear profit on the year's festival of £l9 12s. In addition they had been able to give the children a, field day--at which they had tea and sports. That went to show that the committee were moving along the right track, and with the assistance of friends they would soon •be able tO place the festival on a sound financial basis. (Hear, hear.) INCOME: EXPENDITURE.</p>
        <p>The income Of £lO4 18s. 51d. included balance, 9s.i 10d.; donations (collected by Mrs. Hinde, Misses M. L. Hazlehurst, W. Mairs, Jessie Pear- son and E. Wright, and Messrs. C. Bradley, A. 11. Hudson, S. Inglefield, jun., W. G. Jones, IT. -Reed, Wm. Richards, ,A. .Sutton, and by post), £33 16s. 3d.; street collecting boxes, £1 10s. Md.; sale of 372 sixpenny tickets, £9 6s. afternoon gate receipts £22 17s. (six- penny) and £2 65.. (threepenny); even- ing gate 'receipts.. £7 ;Is 6d. (sixpenny) and 13s. 61. (threepenny); stand receipts £,9 6s. 9d.; sale of _handbooks, £2 Bs. 6d.; advertisements in handbooks, £8 55.; committee's subscription to Queen's crown, 10s.; and sale of children's frocks (per ladies' committee), £1 11s. Amongst the items of expenditure were: Bill- posting. and advertising, £5 16s. 6d.; printing, £9 25.; erections on ground, £22 12s. 6d.; Lymm Subscription Band, £.7 10s.; hire of conveyances, £2 13s. 6d.; costumes £9 4s. id.; crown for Queen, 10s. 6d.; lurry decorations, balloons and. rosettes, £l. 2s. 4-Id.; hire of field, £5; teas, El Os. 6d.; police and ,expenses, £l. 16s. 6d.; Employers' . Liability 'lnsurance, £1 195.; chil- dren's teas, £6 2s. 6d.; and prizes, children's tea, 13s. 10d. To Mr. A. H. Hudson had been handed over on aeCount of the old debit balance, the sum of £l6 4s._ 10d. The totals, added Mr. Jones, were somewhat smaller .than, those of previous yeat4, but the main. object was the balance. (Applause.) . The balance sheet was unanimously adopted on'the motion of Messrs. A. Sutton and H. Reed -the Secretary (Mr. Jas. Webb) putting the motion to the meeting. TO BE OR NOT TO BE?</p>
        <p>In turning., the assembly's attention to the election of officers, The Chairman said he thought it would be advisable if they •first considered whether the festival should be held or not this year—because if it were not, there would be no object in elect- ing new officers. -.Mr. Jas. Webb said his own opinion was that the festival _would .have to be abandoned, but that a provisional committee might be elected. Then if .national conditions improved the chil- (ken might be given a field day later on. As the Knutsford festival had been abandoned, he hardly thought they could hold theirs. Mr. Sutton: Would it not be possible to give something of the kind just to keep the thing going--and to give the profits to the Red Cross fund?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>Subscriptions All-Important.</head>
        <p>Subscriptions All-Important.</p>
        <p>The Secretary; We cannot begin anything before we have subscriptions. Most Of our subscriptions come from Manchester, where the people will probably be working during Whit- week. Personally I don't see how we can hold the festival at the present time. The Chairman: You will notice, in connec- tion with the subscriptions, that most of the publicans are represented. Of course my opinion is that the publicans do not support us sufficiently; but when I took the balance sheet round to the subscribers their general remark was that we must not look to them for any • support should we hold the festival, be- cause their trade was "in a bad way and likely to be worse." Besides, there are only about two of the former landlords left;,they are all new ones! • • -</p>
        <p>Miss J. Pearson thought people were giving all they could to the war funds. The Chairman: We are all as deep in the pocket as we can be: (Laughter.) Mr. Sutton: Well, it would be rather a pity to run into debt again!</p>
        <p>The Chairman remarked that half of the £3 had yet to be paid away.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Hinde agreed with Miss Pearson. Mr. Reed declared thaf the majority of those with whom he had come in contact seemed to want the festival.</p>
        <p>The Secretary: I think we are all very sorry not to have one.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said the trouble was that al expenses had to be paid before the festival thus, if they lost, they lost heavily.</p>
        <p>Unseconded Motion.</p>
        <p>Mr Reed, "just to test the meeting," then proposed that the festival be held. . There was no seconder, and the motion fell through.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>" Fcr Men Must Work -"</head>
        <p>" Fcr Men Must Work -"</p>
        <p>Mr. Sutton: There is no doubt the same num- ber of 'people would not visit Lymm this year. Mr. Reed.: The men might be working, but the women would not.</p>
        <p>Miss Pearson: Well, the girls will be working; only the married women would be able to come —and the men would then be paying. The Chairman: There can be little doubt as to the general opinion of the meeting. Mr.' A. Halliday did not think the opinions of the small attendance might represent the general feeling in the district. Could. they not first test the general feeling? The Chairman r pointed out that every week now more clouds would be hanging•over them; it was quite possible that.the gloom which was everywhere else would become accentuated in Lymm. It did not seem proper to carry on the festival' this year.—Mr. Jones added that should they decide to hold it Mr. Hudson (chairman) said he could not give them any support.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>Qualified Proposition.</head>
        <p>Qualified Proposition.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Margets proposed that they abandon the May Queen for this year—with the provision that they give the Children a field day later on, when things became mere settled—and that a meeting be called later on—say at the beginning of August—to consider the question. The Chairman: If it had not been proposed, I would most certainly have proposed it. It was Pointed out that if the meeting fixed a definite date for the question to be considered and hostilities had by then not ceased, the chil- dren's disappointment would be greater. , Mr. Margets ,then altered his original phrase to: "At the discretion of the secretary." The proposition was carried nem con, Mr. Wm. Richards seconding. •N ONE OFFICER ELECTED.</p>
        <p>The election of officers was proceeded with. The Chairman pointed out that the meeting's decision did awzfy with' the election 'of new offiCers. •</p>
        <p>The Secretary: The rule of the festival is thai all offices are vacat6d at the end of the year. iThe Chairman: Well, do we need a corn. mittee ?</p>
        <p>Mr. Webb: You will have to elect a secre- tary—having already passed a resolution regard- ing him. I hardly think there is any need of other -officers yet; they Could be elected at the next meeting, which is a public one. Mr. Sutton: If a committee is elected to deal with the children's treat, will they be in order to use the May Queen balance. _</p>
        <p>The Secretary: No. The assembly agreed that, only the festival having been abandoned, the old committee— excepting six who had not attended—would be- come mechanically members of the new com- mittee.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: I expect the Children's Treat Committee will formulate itself into the May Queen Committee; then the question of the balance can be considered. The Secretary: Unless Lymm opinion change.s very considerably the same committee will be re-elected!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>SERVICES APPRECIATED.</head>
        <p>SERVICES APPRECIATED.</p>
        <p>• Mr. Halliday proposed a hearty vote of thanks to the Chairman, Treasurer, Secretary and other members of the committee for their hard work last year, and for so nobly getting the festival out of debt.</p>
        <p>On behalf of his colleagues, the Chairman gratefully acknowledged the appreciation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>CHURCH'S RED-LETTER DAY.</head>
        <p>CHURCH'S RED-LETTER DAY.</p>
        <p>Nev Font Dedicated and Rector's Son</p>
        <p>Baptised.</p>
        <p>For Lymm Churchpeople, Tuesday was a red- letter day, by virtue of the fact that the new font in the church of St. Mary the Virgin was then dedicated by the Venerable the Arch- deacon. of . Macclesfield; the Rector's son christened thereat: and the day school children of Lymm and Heatley entertained by him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>New Font Described.</head>
        <p>New Font Described.</p>
        <p>The dedication service took the form of shortened evensong (from Lord's Prayer to end of Creed), and was conducted by the Rev. Mait- land 'Wood, archdeacon of Macclesfield and rector of Bowdon--the church being ' crowded. Many of the school children were in attendance, as were also Mrs. Thurston and Mr. F. E. Evans (vicar's warden). The font—which was beautifully- decorated with ferns, ivy, prim- roses, daffodils 'and other flowers—is of alabaster' from Derbyshire It is supported by five pillars (the central one being thicker than the others), and these: stand upon three thick circular slabs, the bottom one being of grey stone. The whole gift is from an anonymous donor</p>
        <p>The Rector (the Rev.' G. D. Thurston) christened his son by the names: John Gran- ville. The sponsors are: Mr. E. C. Ransome (of Ipswich), the -Rev. Max .Binney • (vicar of Richmond). and Miss Luck (of London). Among the hymns sung were: "Little Ones are Dear- to Thee" and " Bless the Children," whilst "Look on this Babe" followed the baptism. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>CHILDREN'S GIFT.</head>
        <p>THE CHILDREN'S GIFT.</p>
        <p>Afterwards the children assembled at their respective schools at Lymm and Heaney, and enjoyed tea. (provided by the Rector) followed by games. To the Lymm school the Rector presently paid .a visit, when Sidney Dawson (top •boy), presented him (for his son), on behalf of the Lymm and Statham school children, with a handsome silver porridge-bowl and spoon (sup- plied by Parker, Altrincham). Master Dawson said he had great pleasure in handing to the Rector the present on behalf of the teachers and children. •</p>
        <p>Oddie (headmaster) briefly alluded to the fact that the birth of Master Thtfrston was the first which had taken place at the .Rectoryi for over sixty Yeiirs.. He wished him every. joy and good health, hoping that when he used their gift he would think, of the donor. For the Rector and Mrs. Thurston he Always desired good luck.</p>
        <p>The Rector expressed his grateful surprise, and thanked thAm all very much on behalf of his little son; and was only sorry that Mrs. Thurston also had not been able to be with them.</p>
        <p>The evening was pleasantly passed by games in the Hall field,----kindly lent by Mr. H. Gibbon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>PADOATE.</head>
        <p>PADOATE.</p>
        <p>Whist Drive and Dance.—A whist drive and dance organised by the Padgate branch of the Primrose League, was held in the C.E. Schools on Thursday evening. The whist prize-winners were :-,-,Ladies: 1, Mrs. Blackwell; 2, Mrs.- Con- ron. Gentlemen: 1, Mr. Cruikshanks; 2, Mr. Blackwell. The M.C.'s were: Whist, Messrs. P. Royston and 11. Houghton; (lancing, Messrs. W. Brimelow, T. Antrobus and R. Simpson. Mr. W Wainwright, who very generously) gave his services. was pianist. There was a good attend- ance, the proceeds being in aid of the local relief societi,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>STOCKTON H EATH</head>
        <p>STOCKTON H EATH</p>
        <p>Hill Cliffe Baptist Sunday School.--Anniver- sary services were held in connection with the Hill Chile Baptist Sunday school on Sunday,_ _ _ _</p>
        <p>when the preacher for the day was the Rev. A. _Harvey, of, Widnes, whose addresses were of most appropriate character. At the morning service the reverend gentleman used a watch to illustrate the importance of watching our words, our actions, our thoughts, our com- panions, and, lastly, our habits. A 'song, enti- tled "The Gift," was very tastefully rendered by Miss Capel. Miss Byfield presided at the piano. Another item, entitled "In Time of War," was given by the Girls' Guild, after which Mr. Harvey di,,-oursed upon Matthesv xvii. 2. A young people's service was held in the afternoon, and was presided over by Mr. Ben Few, whose remarks were highly appre- ciated. Mr. Thomas Dale was the speaker, and he spoke words of encouragement and advice to those engaged in Sunday school work. Mr. S. Turton sang a solo entitled "The Watchman,'' and "Light and Darkness." Mrs. Massey was the accompanist. The Girls' Guild sang veil' sweetly "Children of Jerusalem" and "Samuel." At the evening service Mr. Harvey preached from Acts iii. 6. Special music wag - again rendered by the children, and Mrs. J.-- Kenworthy sang "0 Divine Redeemer" (Miss' Parkinson at the piano), and the choir contri- buted the anthem, "Sun of my soul." Mr. Peter Harrison was the organist throughout the day. The Boys' Brigade were in evidence with bugle and drum, and thus called attention ifl• their own way to the claims .of their work at Hill Cliffo. The collections totalled .C 9 14s. The congregations were large, and easily constituted a record.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>ELECTRIFYING STOCKTON HEIM</head>
        <p>ELECTRIFYING STOCKTON HEIM</p>
        <p>Vidal The AVarrington Corporation</p>
        <p>Will Do.</p>
        <p>. At the annual meeting of the Runcorn,Rural District Council on Tuesday, the Acting' Clerk (Mr. Hough) reported on the visit of a depute- Hon to the Board of Trade in the matter of the application by the 'Warrington Corporation for an Electric Lighting Extension Order, which will enable them to run their mains to Stockton Heath, and supply the township with electricity. The Council's representatives went to the in- quiry bent on opposing the application of the., Corporation, whose proposals in the matter were unfavourable to Stockton-Heath's interests, but fresh proposals were put forth, and these met ' with the approval of the representatives of the ' rural authorities, and their opposition was accordingly withdrawn. The result of the con- - ference was that the Board of Trade official who conducted the inquiry (Mr. Roper) indi- cated that the extension must be made towards the 'parish of Latchford Without. It a-as de- cided that plans should be drawn uP by the Warrington Corporation and forwarded to Of Board of Trade, showing'what had been agree upon. It was further decided that the tertaS should be the same as those on which the con- sumers in the borough were supplied. lie (the clerk) made an appointment with the Town Clerk of Warrington, and it was agreed between them that the extension should take in' Acker' road, Grappenhall-road, Victoria-square, Wal- ton-road. London-road, and a portion of White- field-road, and that the work should be carried out within a limited period of time. Mr. Linaker paid a tribute to the " verY clever way in which the Council's case was handled" by the assistant clerk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
