      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <p>at their own charge.: The House hereupon voted the -number of the said fleet amounting to ,73, and the number of men to man the seine, which they encluded to be 6,000 and odde (the greatest navy that bath. been set forth of late Scares) they gave order for victualling thereof and money to be raised for this purpose. The _com- mittee appointed to draw up an answer to the Ambassy of the Dutch Ambassadors made reporte thereof to the House, who after reading ordered the same to be committed and to be re- ported- againe with all speed. The •.committee formerly appointed to consider of bringing in an Act for constituting a high court of, justice for •tryall of Duke Hamilton and the other capital offenders before mentioned -made reporte to the House, they approved thereof • and com- missioners' names to be inserted therein which amount to about 60, and appointed any 15 hereof to be of the Quorunt being members of Parliament, army, city, and other private gentlemen, but none that were of the last.com-. mission.</p>
        <p>The Dutch Ambassadors this day desired, That what was deliveredoy them to the House in relation to their Ambassy, may not be com- mitted to. the presse. but 'kept private, the House joyning with theni in this ordered the same accordingly. They further ordered to consider of the manner of (4m-eminent to be established within this kingdome, and whether the kingly office, or the louse of .Peers, should be any longer continued. but the latter of these were to be first debated to-morrow morning.</p>
        <p>Saturday, February 3,</p>
        <p>The debate, according to former order con- cerning the present flouse of Peers, whether it shall he continued or no an essent all parte of the suprcatne authority of the Nutiiio. was this day to have begun, but the two f.,rirter busi- nesses being.of so great concernment and taking up so inn; h flow, it was ordered to he deferred till Munday morning next, and thee it is to he the first businesse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>WORLD'S GREATEST MEN.</head>
        <p>WORLD'S GREATEST MEN.</p>
        <p>Who Gained Their Honours When Youth Was But a Memory.</p>
        <p>The truth of Disraeli's famous remark, " Old age is unknown to genius," is certainly shown in the following paragraphs : • Dante was almost 70 when be composed his famous epic.</p>
        <p>Robert Browning!s greatest poem, " lling and Boyk," was composed when he was over 60.</p>
        <p>Tennyson was nearly 80 when be wrote " Crossing the Bar."</p>
        <p>Oliver Wendell Holmes was a professor at Harvard at 80.</p>
        <p>Michael Angelo completed the great cupalo of St. Peter's at 87.</p>
        <p>Titian, .finished his Last Supper at 77, and. at 79 West completed .his best picture, "Death on the Pale Horse." Haydn produced his sublime " Creation " at GO, while Verdi was past 70 when ho wrote the score of " Falstaff." .</p>
        <p>• Sir Isaac Newton wrote a preface to his " Principia " at 83, While Sir William Herschel!, when over 80, swept the heavens with a true range of vision. ~ Gladstone and BiSmarek were.Nthe control- ling powers in European polit•ics when both were past 75 years of age. Von \i 0! at 70 won the battle of Sedan, while Thiers. President of France, was 8() when, he achieved his diplomatic victory' over 13ismarck.</p>
        <p>Nelson, Blake, Fail-Agin. and Dell ts.‘" were beyond three score when they achieved their fame.</p>
        <p>Oyama, the. Japanese military leader, won his greatest victories over powerful Russia when past 60, and all his corps commanders were over 50.</p>
        <p>lto, Japan's greatest statesman, who did. more than any other man to make Japan what it is to-day, was busy-planning still greater achievements for his..c.ountry when assassin- ated at,69. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>Bargain in Ducks.</head>
        <p>A Bargain in Ducks.</p>
        <p>A story is tom of the famous Richard Brim- ley Sheridan, that one day When coming back from shooting with an empty bag and seeing a number of ducks in a pond, while nearby a man was leaning on a fence watching them, Sheridan asked: " What will you take for a shot at the ducks?" " Well," said the man thoughtfully, " take half a sovereign." " Done," said Sheridan and he fired into the middle of the floek, killing a dozen or more.• "I 'in afraid you made a inid bargain,." said Sheridan, laughing.</p>
        <p>"I don't know about that," the man replied " They're not my ducks."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>POLLUTED EUROPE. Disease Spread By Flies, WORK FOR THE SANITARY AUTHOR IT I ES.</head>
        <p>A POLLUTED EUROPE. Disease Spread By Flies, WORK FOR THE SANITARY AUTHOR IT I ES.</p>
        <p>With the approach of warmer weather, says. "The Times" medical correspondent, the ques- tion of methods of dealing with the fly pest once more comes into prominence. This year that question is much more important than formerly. Europe has been from a sanitary point of view, polluted. The danger of epidemic disease is very actual, and the precautions which can be taken against outbreaks are necessarily restricted by the exigencies of war. We shall do well, thus early in the year to consider the possible reduction of the fly visita- tion of the, late summer and autumn months. And it may be said at once that reduction will not be achieved by any policy of killing adult</p>
        <p>Flies, as is generally .well known, are developed from eggs and larvae. The female fly deposits her eggs as a rule in manure heap,. or heaps of rotting vegetable matter or garbage. When the eggs hatch out. lie fly larva., live in the manure or garbage until their wings are strong enough to permit of flight. To attempt to destroy • the eggs or kill the larvte is, generally speaking, a mistaken policy. From time to time one hears of preparations designed to be sprinkled upon or mixed with manure heaps; the advocates of these preparations contend that by means of them eggs are prevented from hatching and lervie Ato 'killed. But all such measures par- take a-the nature of trifling. The sound polity is to remove the breeding grounds themselves or to subject them to strict inspection and super- vision.</p>
        <p>The Removal of Refuse,</p>
        <p>our sanitary authorities can achieve a splen- did work in this matter. In all great cities the keeping of any sort of manure heap should be absolutely prohibited. Stables should be in- spected with the greatest care and must be cleansed with disinfectants at regular and fre- quent intervals. Stable refuse must be removed at once. either to an incinerator or to some tightly-closed receptacle which will be emptied without tfelay. In the country the problem is much more difficult; that it is solvable, how- ever, scarcely admits of doubt. Here, again, the manure heap or " midden " is the great danger. It must be dealt with as if it was a danger. Strict regulations should be enforced as regards its locality in relation to dwelling- houses, the -means to be adopted for its empty- ing, and for its disinfection when emptied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>THREAT OF EPIDEMIC DISEASE.</head>
        <p>THREAT OF EPIDEMIC DISEASE.</p>
        <p>That epidemic diseases, especially diseases like scurvy, dysentery, and typhus fever, have not so far manifested themselves is a remark- able tribute to the efficiency of the sanitary and public. health work being done for the armies in the field. But it would be unwise to leave these efforts unsupported. In a very careful paper on "War and Cholera," an abstract of which appears in this week's issue of the "Lancet," the writer, Pro- fessor Simpson, of , King's College, London, points out that the armies of Germany and A ustri a will be in a very different physical, mental and, .material condition in -August and the autumn of 1915 or 1916 from what they were in 1914, when victory seemed to them assured. Should epidemics then arise Ailey will not be confined to the particular armies that are first stricken. nor will the civil population in the devastated areas escape. Scarcity of food, it is further indicated, will play its part in reducing the resisting power of the population to disease. The vombination of war, flood and' famine must favour the spread of epidemic disease. Indeed, the terrible taint in flue air which already characterises the in- undated area on the Yser tells it own tale. (ruder this flood, it is said, lie a hundred I hmisa ml corpses. The Yser is not much further from London than is Newcastle., London, therefore, must take strict measures to protect herself; at a monient,'when health is of such vital import- ance to her she cannot afford to overlook any precaution. The effect upon hours of labour alone would be immense were al/ epidemic to arise; the effect of alcoholic excess would-be quite insignificant by comparison. Various measures of protection are now in .force. Every one who has crossed the Channel recently knoniA,,, that a medical- inspection is carried out at Folkestone before passengers are permitted to land.. This is a wise measure, and we may look for an even more vigorous applica- tion of it as the summer advances. Inoculation for Civilians.</p>
        <p>A suggestion has been made recently that inoculation against typhoid fever should be carried out among the civil population in Eng- land. There is much to be said in favour of thiS idea. England at present is full of camps in which large numbers of people are crowded together. These camps are models of good management; but, nevertheless, there are cer- tain (hangers. connected with life in what May be described as " primitive " communities which are:unavoidable. Some of these dangers have already been encountered. The " typhoid carrier " is one of them; be is nob, himself ill, but he carries the germ of the disease and in- fects other people with it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>FUEL CONSUMPTION.</head>
        <p>FUEL CONSUMPTION.</p>
        <p>(Queries and correspondence should be addressed to " The Hub," c/o the Editor. Replies cannot be sent by post, and all queries will be answered in our columns.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>METROPOLITAN TOUR.</head>
        <p>A METROPOLITAN TOUR.</p>
        <p>I have lately received a, good many requests from my readers for some particulars of easy and interesting cycle runs around London, in view of the fact that they are anticipating a holiday in the Metropolis, and are anxious to bringttheir mounts with them. By far the best plan under those circumstances is to take the train to a good centre rather than experience all the drudgery of a ride through the crowded London streets. Five Miles of such riding is far more fatiguing and, nerve-racking than twenty lades along an open. road. Within an easy distance of London there is no more.charm- ing spot than that served I,y the Metropolitan Railway. besides which special kicilities are offered lo cyclists. Apart from Ibe fact that reduced fares are obtainable, each tour allows of return from a station other than the s!carting noint, which gives the cyclist a very much greater latitude. For the tour lam going to suggest a start should be made from Amarsham, a quaint little Buckinghamshire village. The ravages of time seem to have passed it by 'll7l- scathed, despite the coming of the railway \and the never ceasing expansion of the Metropolis. One long .street—part of the road to Wendover and Aylesbury still forms its single, thorough- fare of importance, and even here . the picturesque houses, many of considerable antiquity, give an appearance of peace and repose. 'Leaving the town at right angles to the main street in the opposite direction to the station, a few minutes' ride brings the cyclist into some typical Buckinghlimshire country. The little hamlet of Penn Street. with its picturesque modern church, is soon left behind; on the left the landscape comprehends Penn Wood and King's Wood'- while on the right Wycombe heath affords an appropriate contrast.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>DISRAELI'S HOME</head>
        <p>DISRAELI'S HOME</p>
        <p>Very soon the busy little town of High Wycombe is in sight. As 'everybody knows, it owes its present importance to the mallufacture of chairs, unromantic perhaps, but distinctly profitable. From small beginnings the in- dustry has steadily increased to its present magnitude, until 5,000 men now find employ- ment in it. But it is the old part of the town which Is -the more interesting to the tourist. Here are some quaint old houses; the little Market House, built in 1601, and the eighteenth- century town hall; also a fourteenth-century house near the church and almshouses dating from 1562. High Wycombe has the largest church in the country, and although the pre- sent exterior is rather unprepossessing the in- terior well repays a visit. Leaving High Wycombe. follow the road alongside the rail- to West Wycombe, another picturesque village and a good church. Dore, the road forks. hut keep to the right. About a nith' anti a half further on is the village of Bradenham, celebrated for its connection with Benjamin Disraeli. Earl of Beaconsfield. Here his father, lsaae Disraeli, lived from 1829 to 18113. and it was hero that the future Prime Minister spent sonic of the happiest years of his life. Braden- ham has a fine old (+limb, with a characteristie Norman doorway. believed to have beeu. built by Swartley, the Saxon owner of the manor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>CHILTERN HILLS.</head>
        <p>THE CHILTERN HILLS.</p>
        <p>Continuing along the same. road, Princes Ris- borough, a good-sized village, is soon reached. It is chiefly interesting for the long connection of the manor with the reigning Sovereign or iis family. Turning to the right, nearly opposite the church, a good road runs to Monk's His- borough, one of the prettiest villages in the country, and beautifully situated on the margin of the Chiltern Hills. It has a number of old cottages, a picturesque rectory, and a fine old church, containing many interesting features. At the time of the Domesday survey this place was .part of the domain of Arch- bishop Lanfranc. Villages are close together in this particular district. About a mile and a half beyond Monk's Risborough arc the Kimbles," Great and Little, two picturesque villages, with their churches only a quarter of a mile apart. -Great Kimble will always be associated with John Hampden, who owned the manor, and his refusal in the Parish Church to pay his share of the Ship Money levied by the King. At Little Kimble the cyclist .has his choice of direction—he can eit her keep straight on as far as the •road from .kylf,:lmry to Amer- sham, then turn to the. right to Wendover, the nearer way of the two, or turn to the left at Turttwick, and to the right at Stoke 3laudeville, thence 'to Aylesbury. Both are charming roads amidst typical Buckinghamshire scenery. At the present moment one of the largest camps in the 'country, is near Aylesbury, in Lord Rothschild's celebrated ipark. The innumerable rows of wooden and corrugated iron sheds do not perhaps improve the appearance of the country, but they ,are a, reminder • of the strenuous days through which we are passing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>OLD MOTOR CYCLE ENGINES.</head>
        <p>OLD MOTOR CYCLE ENGINES.</p>
        <p>Bonte iteople are born ingenious. Others make use of the ingenuity of other. Here is an example of the former. In many country dis- tricts the household water supply has to be pumped up daily into a cistern under the roof, and this entails a considerable amount of labour. I came across a. man the other day who has made use of a very ancient motor- cycle engine to work his pump. The engine is some eleven years old, bordering as far as motor-cycles are concerned on prehistoric times, but it answers the purpose splendidly. The engine is of 2 b.p.; and is geared nine to one, which is ample in power f9l' the ordinary hand, pump. A counter shaft, with a. fast and loose pulley, was fixed, but this is really in the nature of a, luxury. It is not at. all »eees- sary. Some ingenuity is required to make the uttachuieut tu the pump shalt, but this is nut</p>
        <p>ZMIZMZA</p>
        <p>such a difficult matter as might be at first supposed.</p>
        <p>TON MILES TO THE GALLON. If the question were asked of twenty motorists whether the number of ton miles to a gallon of fuel is greater in the case of a car or a motor- Cycle, nineteen would in all probability reply, the motor-cycle. This, however, is not the case. It appears as if the heavier the vehicle the more efficient is the engine. In one instance a car weighing well over two tons succeeded in getting 38 ton miles to the gallon. Even a light car may have '3O ton miles for each gallon of spirit. In the case of a motor-cycle weighing perhaps 3501 b. and running at 100 m.p,g. the ton ;miles work out at less than 17 to the gallon. The laws of mechanics demand a. loss. of power in the case of a light vehicle irrese pective of whether it has two, three or four wheels. But there are certain directions in which loss might be avoided. The difference. in efficiency is very great' when 17 is compared with 30 and 38. There is no doubt. that a con- siderable amount of spirit never teaches the engine at all. This may be due to the car- burettor.</p>
        <p>SOME CAUSES OF PETROL LOSS. It: is quitt• a common occurrenee tor the'car- burettor to become very hot, which causes rapid vapourisation., It is true that a certain degree of heat in this part of the machine i).O useful, ,but in many cases the float chamber becomes hot, in which c;l,-., the spirit.rapidly turns into VI) pour when it escapes into the air-. We also tind slopping and creeping from the carburettor more or less common in cycles. This produces a certain amount of waste of fuel. Mostriders- appreciate the fact that some spirit, is lost itt this way, but few understtind to what ,an ex- tent this happens. Other causes may -be the irregular surface presented by the cycle, since this produces eddies which affect the running. The quality of the springing also pla3-s its part. A more or less solid blow is received by the cycle on a bad bit of road, whereas in a car the jolts are less noticeable. There are many other factors to be taken into account, dealing principally with the engine, but 'whatever the cause, there is sufficient evidence to show that a motor-cycle comes out worst.</p>
        <p>THE HUB.</p>
      </div>
