      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>CON SULT</head>
        <p>CON SULT</p>
        <p>"THE EXAMINER" OFFICE,</p>
        <p>BEWSEY STREET.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APR</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APR</p>
        <p>.:":":":"41*,++.:. 4":**).)..)4+4":":. 4,44.:"),4)++++ ++++++++++ ++++++++++++++++++++ +++++ ++++++++++ ++++ ++++++++++++++++ ++++++++++ ++++++++++ ++++++++++ +++4..3</p>
        <p>the mass with 'a knife into three or four parts; lay these on a hot hearth or griddle; when they are half baked la them edgewise on a toaster before the fire until they are quite dry. Butter mixed with the meal will greatly improve the cake.</p>
        <p>"441 ++ • + + + ++ ++ A very charming model had a jupe of black taffetas, very soft and supple. The fullness of the skirt was gathered into a yoke at the hips, and flared thence to the hem. The coat defined the figure, and had a tiny gored basque. It was closed to the neck, and had a high open collar, below which a. black silk cravat was worn. This is a typical this-season's coat.•</p>
        <p>Simple Suits for Children. • Children's clothing, and more especially their outside garments, is scarcely less important than the toilettes of grown-ups. Just now mothers, are selecting school and other outfits for the summer term, and for the warmn weather that will perhaps soon be here. For younger girls, the sailor snit, consisting of a kilted skirt, a long loose • jumper, with smart little breast pocket, is a very useful 4nd pretty style for everyday Wear. It is equally suited to serge in. navy, cream, .or red, or to cottons and linen. Narrow braid in some contrasting colour is the usual trimming for these little suits. When of serge, 110 petticoats are worn, knickers to match the dresses taking their place. The pinafore frock is as frequently seen now- for girls iv their early teens as the coat and skirt and blouse, without which. no Outfit has hitherto been considered complete. The daintiest dresses, but simpler and less ornate in character than in other years, are shown for party and best wear, and these are provided with charming petticoats of silk, and lace. Japanese silk of a good quality is tohO preferred to cambric in this connection. It washes better and is quite as durable.</p>
        <p>This Season's Millinery. The Millinery of the moment is decidedly in- teresting, for a touch of genius is evident in many models. The war probably accounts for 9, shortage, in some materials, but this seems to have put milliners on their mettle, and clever manipulation has made up for any inadequacy in fabrics, and resulted in a greater 'variety of really charming styles. As hats are to suit the costumes with which they . are worn, a greater number, and a wider choice will be required. Colour combinations. are in more delicate shades than last summer, and contrasts are much less startling. Despite the intimation_ that eccentricities were to be taboo, a number of bizarre shapes are shown. Scotland, Belgium, Russia, India and Japan have all fur- nished ideas for this summer's millinery. The Scotch turban is worn at the precise angle adopted by men of. the Scottish regiments, and is more becoming to young and pretty faces than to older women.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>. t 4 3 s t.:. .41 ." .RL /' 23, , •</head>
        <p>. t 4 3 s t.:. .41 ." .RL /' 23, , •</p>
        <p>Drinks, Ancient and Modern. In the quest for invigorating temperance drinks we might do worse than return to the old-fashioned salep—a preparation extracted from roots. This was dissolved in water, and with milk and sugar ;added, the beverage was a common drink of the middle classes before tea or coffee came into common use. So also was water porridge, the precursion of 'barley water, which is said to be one of the principal drinks served at the Royal dinner-table. It was Catherine of • Braganza, the Queen of Charles H., who introduced tea into this country. Pepys tells of drinking tea in 1660; but coffee claims the field first. By the time of Queen Anne, tea-drinking had become a general habit, and there have ..been sold at the Red Cross Sale, sonic interesting early- eighteenth century tea things The earliest tea- cups were without handles, and it was the custom to have the spoons numbered, so that guests, in having a second cup, might be sure of getting the same one back.</p>
        <p>A Training Centre for Women Chairmen. The County add Borough Councils' Qualifica- tion Act, marks an important step forward, as far 'as women. are concerned. It wilt enable a large number to become candidates for various local 'governing bodies. So far no provision has been made for the training of eligible women in the somewhat complicated duties of chair- man of a Borough Council, or a Mayor. But it is possible that in the near future, a centre may be 'formed where candidates may obtain instruction from women who have had ex- perience in social work of every kind. There are still many Boards' of Guardians without female members, and there are no women Com- missioners in Lunacy; yet much valuable work could be done by women in both these spheres. The housing of the people, the education and care of children, the welfare of young girls, the care of the sick and aged, all come "within woman's province.</p>
        <p>This Season's Short Coat. The very short skirt introduced by the Parisian dressmakers, has so far found little favour in. London. Tailored morning 'suits are decidedly- shorter, but a very slight difference is to be noted in the length, of afternoon gowns. A skirt made by one of the best houses is /moulded to the hips, gored to the knee, and certainly not more than three yards in circum- ference„ This has met with general /approval, for it is moderate, graceful, and yet quite new. Quite short coats of a different colour to the skirts are likely to have a vOgue this season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>lIERE AND THERE.</head>
        <p>lIERE AND THERE.</p>
        <p>It must make a husband feel uncomfortable to hear his wife say 30 or 40 times a day: have nothing to wear."</p>
        <p>WANliave. noticed that when a woman goes to visira dear old friend she stays longer if the friend has a new automobile than if the friend has a cross baby.</p>
        <p>The Better Way. "1 shdll tell my daughter that if she persists in marrying Jones I'll not leave her a cent." "It'll do more good to tell that to Jones.". The Feminine Nature.-</p>
        <p>Home study for Tommy had just, begun, and he found it hard to apply himself to regular hours. At bedtime one evening his father said: "Tommy, I am not at all pleased with the report your mother gives me of your conduct to-day."</p>
        <p>"No, father, I knowed you wouldn't be, and I told her so. • But she went. right ahead an' made th' report. Jest like a woman, ain't it?"</p>
        <p>IS THIS TRUE, BRUNETTES?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>Have Blondes a Corner on Sweet and Gentle</head>
        <p>Have Blondes a Corner on Sweet and Gentle</p>
        <p>Dispositions?</p>
        <p>"The loveliest thing in this world," says a distinguished. painter, "is a blonde woman. As an 'artist, I am quite read to admit the exquisite beauty of many dark women, but for myself there is nothing so beautiful as golden hair, with its shining filaments and rich shadows; nothing so fine as a pair of deep blue eyes, nothing so charming as a white skin tinted by the pink of health.. "Perhaps it is because the type is an expres- sion of character that I prefer blondes. I pre- fer the character that usually goes with golden hair and blue eyes. I-somehow quite naturally associate sweetness, gentleness, tenderness and all feminine virtues with the blonde women. From early times artists seem to have held the same view. Angels always have been painted as blondes. • A brunette cherub would seem ridiculous.</p>
        <p>"On the other hand, I associate tbe brunette woman with passionate love and hate. To my imagination, Carmen stands: as the psychologi- cal exemplar of the brunette type. No woman who loved and bated and wreaked revenge as did the cigarette girl of Seville ever could have been a blonde. Such a character could be expressed only by black hair, flashing. eyes and .glowing, swarthy skin. The brunette is the intense woman."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>WHAT JANE SAID.</head>
        <p>WHAT JANE SAID.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Bigg: "Jane, wasn't that gentleman king for me?" Jane: "No, mum; he said as the lady he wanted to see ivas about forty, and I said it couldn't be you."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Bigg : " Quite.,right, Jane; you shall have the afternoon off."</p>
        <p>Jane: "Yes, 'mum, thank you, mum; I said it couldn't be you as you was about fifty." • Mrs: Bigg: "And while you're out you can look for a new situation."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>BARN OWL.</head>
        <p>THE BARN OWL.</p>
        <p>There are several species of British owls, hut the commonest of them all is the Barn Owl—a bird which is familiarly known all over Eng- land. It has the singular appearance common to all owls; the solemn moonlike eyes, the round, puffy head, the. small curved beak, and the peculiar disc of feathers—which, radiating from the eyes, gives to the face of this bird a very old expression.. In addition to these peculiarities the barn owl has a beautifully- soft and thick coat of delicately coloured plumage; indeed, so thick and set are its soft feathers, that one might suppose it to be a much larger bird than it really is. When dead and stripped of its lovely dress, it 'turns out to be very little larger than a common pigeon-</p>
        <p>The nest -o—f- the barn owl is 'often placed in crevices, of ruined buildings,' and sometimes in hollow trees. The eggs are white and some- what rough On the surface; the young birds seem to be° nothing but 'little puffs 'of white don. The parent owls are very careful of their nestlings, feeding th6m most attentively and dashing fiercely at any on' who darts to approach too near to their home. The general colour of this bird is a beautiful pale 'buff, with a sprinkling of white and black feathers; the tail is ornamented with broad bars of grey.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>'MONKEY AND THE WIND.</head>
        <p>THE 'MONKEY AND THE WIND.</p>
        <p>This Easter fable is written as it was told by the natives of the Philippines: One day a monkey was swinging nimbly from branch to. brand" of a mango-tree. The wind</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Princest Christian's Hospital Train.</p>
        <p>The wonderful hospital train, named after Princess Christian, was sent, to Windsor for their Majesties' inspection before being shipped to France. The train is a gift to the Princess' from her friends, who have raised the funds necessary for its purchase. It is the second train of its kind built by the Birmingham Carriage Company, and the same designers were employed for both. The first train owed its origin to the energy' of l'rincess Christian,</p>
        <p>and was used in the 'Boer War. It contributed immensely to the comfort of the sick and wounded during that campaign. The second train, which' has been handed over to the War Offiee, exceeds that of its predecessor in the perfection of its arrangements for the comfort of those who will use it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>War-time Changes.</head>
        <p>War-time Changes.</p>
        <p>The Royal Academy will open its doors as usual next month, but there will be no banquet or private view. The latter usually affords the opportunity for new modes to make their debut, but there will be a break this year, as indeed iti so many other social funemns, owing to the war. Nor will 'there be a display of new fashions, and beautiful toilettes at Ascot. Had it not been for the war, the present season would have been a very brilliant one. it wilU be notable for the King's -fiftieth birthday on tine 3rd, the Prince of Wales being twenty-one three weeks later, and. for the coming out 4 Princess Mary. Princess Mary shares the Queen's love for Windsor, but it is unlikely that the Court will spend much time. there. The Royal Family will continue to; reside for the most part in London, where Queen Alexandra is closely engaged on some or other good ,work in connection with the war. She has had her nieces, the Grand Duchess George .of Russia, and Princess Margaret of Denmark staying with her at Marlborough House. The Grand 7.ochess spends a good deal of time at Harro- gate, and established a hospital there for the wounded, at ,the outbreak of the war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>HOME NOTES.</head>
        <p>HOME NOTES.</p>
        <p>Profitable Recipes and Useful Suggestions.</p>
        <p>Home Duties.</p>
        <p>' There are few duties more important than those we owe to our own families, to our hus- bands and wives, and to our children. , We sacrifice the essential to the secondary when we permit our interest to be diverted from these.</p>
        <p>Care of Silk Dress; To clean a silk dress never brush it with a stiff clothes brush. Make a pad; cover it with an old piece of velvet, and use this for rubbing it when dusty or dirty. If dust has accumulated in gathers or folds, brush with a small soft brush. Will Improve Apple Pie. , - .</p>
        <p>To improve the flavour of an aPple pie sprinkle the fruit with lemon juice after it has. been cut up and put into the pie dish, and add an ounce or more, according to the size of the pie, of fresh butter cut in small bits.</p>
        <p>To Keep Dinner Hot. If dinner has to be kept.for a late comer, put it on a plate, cover it over, and stand it over a saucepan of boiling water. , keep hot for some time without drying up.</p>
        <p>The Hands.</p>
        <p>The hands can be kept•soft when doing house- work by observing the following rules :—Wear rubber gloves when your hands are in the water and loose kid gloves when sweeping and dusting. Take one drachm of cubebs, one and one-half ounces glycerine, one-half ounce of spirits of camphor, one drachm of extract of heliotrope. nub this on the hands on retiring, and wear a loose pair of old kid gloves.. • Almond Cake.</p>
        <p>One cupful of butter, one cupful and. a half of sugar, three eggs, half a cupful of milk, two teaspoonfuls of baking powder, two cupfuls of flour. Blanch lb. almonds, split and lay aside enough to cover the loaves; chop the rest and add to the cake. After the cake is in'the pans, lay the split ones over the top, or, if preferred, they. may be used to ornament the frosting. Butter nuts may be ,substituted for almonds in this recipe. Another way is to bake the batter (Plain) in jelly-cake pans, and put together with a thick icing combined with the chopped nuts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>Oatmeal Cakes.</head>
        <p>Oatmeal Cakes.</p>
        <p>Into a bowl put as much meal as will make a cake, mix it with boiling water by the right hand while you turn the bowl with the other. When the meal is sufficiently mixed, turn i,t out on a clean board, knead it quickly with the knuckles of both hands, adding a little meal, and using the roller to work the dough to the thickness of an inch. Pare the edge, and divide</p>
        <p>sgv,oo-ecwtovoleto,-Tf(wo42AE):4v2Am)=tAvddn CHILDREN'S CORNER. 3r: ,K Conducted by UNCLE NED. iZA2As-1() Ato42,oobcv-ziYow2-..wolakWxwV-V</p>
        <p>A. HEARTY INVITATION to, join this Corner is extended to all.children who read it regularly. Application for membership, stating name, address and age, should be made to " UNCLV Examiner' Office, Bewsey-street, War- rington." Every girl or boy, whose name is in the Big Book, is entitled to enter the competi- tions held from time to time, to the winners of which useful prizes are awarded.</p>
        <p>WHY UNCLE NED IS SAD. MY CHILDREN DEAR,-</p>
        <p>I write to say It makes me sad To think how many a weary day We have not had, A solitary member who</p>
        <p>Was new. (This will not do!)</p>
        <p>So children all, whose eyes may light On what I write '- Join in our Corner (for you may!) Without delay. And now my sorry, say is said, I am,</p>
        <p>Your loving</p>
        <p>UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>'Note what you've read!)</p>
        <p>THOUGHT FOR THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>You can buy an hour's forgetfulness; but you cannot buy an hour's happiitess.—T. T. jAMEs.</p>
        <p>"ONLY RUN DOWN!"</p>
        <p>The big red- touring car struck a gentleman walking along the road. It rolled him in the mud,"rendering.him unconscious. The owner ran back, greatly- excited, after</p>
        <p>stopping. his car. "Is he dead?" he asked anxiously of the physician who was bending over the victim. "Oh, no," remarked the 'physician cheerfully, !"' lie's not dead; be's merely run down!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Useful InfOrmalion in Popular Language.</p>
        <p>Bread and Water.</p>
        <p>Recently the Public Press has told us of a pastor in the Midlands who subsisted on bread and water for a week and at the same time did his ordinary work. The gentleman, who is described as a man of fine physique, stated that he felt no bad effects from the bread.and water diet. But why should he? Wholemeal wheat bread in 'itself ig practically a complete diet, and any person in good health ought to be able to exist not only for a week but for many weeks on bread and water without any difficulty.</p>
        <p>"Catching Cold." That a common eajd in the head is catching and infectious like influenza is scarcely realised by the public as yet. If everyone would beware of the person. who is sneezing or coughing and keep his distance when obliged to converse with him colds would soon become far less common. Even when a man speaks, and especially when he speaks forcibly, he throws minute particles of saliva, More or less laden with germs, into the air. If he is suffering from ft cold at the same time these germs are infeatiOns and are capable of spreading the infection to all those who breathe these. minute particles of mucus which float about in the air for some time.</p>
        <p>Of course, the danger is much greater when the man who is suffering from a cold sneezes or coughs. The vigorous sneeze will send -a minute spray of infection a considerable distance. In a recent lecture. Professor. Hill suggested the intervention of a newspaper to be held up in front of the patient who is suffering from the cold, every time he sneezes, or coughs, or speaks. Such a crude screen would certainly assist very materially in preventing the spread of the infection, but as the Professor said, there is n still better means of guarding against infection and.that is the proper use of a large handkerchief. Persons suffering from colds should Make it an invariable practice to both sneeze and cough into a handkerchief so as to prevent, iiifecting 'others as far as possible. But the best and safest practice is for the person suffering from a cold to remain at home for two or three days, or at least while the veld is most infectious. This is not only an advan- tage to others, but equally sO to the patient, for he stands a far better Chance ot.a prompt and complete recovery. Furthermore, it is always a great advantdge to be in possession of vigorous good health, for then one is far better able to resist the invasion of germs. The maintenance of a fine physique and a high degree of bodily vigour is always a protection and enables many a man and woman to escape infection to which others succumb.— Good Health."</p>
        <p>chanced to come along, and, seeing him at his antics, said— You think you are 'very spry, friend mon- key; but I can throw you to the ground in a moment."</p>
        <p>"I am sure you cannot," answered the mon- key, dangling by his tail from a swaying branch; 7 for the 'stronger you blow, the harder I shall hold on."</p>
        <p>Then the wind tore through the mango-tree, shaking off the fruit in showers, and snapping the branch to which the monkey clung. But the monkey reached Out quickly and seized an- other branch, and laughed loudly at the wind. The wind blew harder and harder, breaking off great limbs as easily as if they had been twigs; but each time the monkey swung to the next branch, and the wind put forth its utmost strength in vain. At last, tired out, the wind thought of a new plan. Blowing very gently, it ,sang softly in the monkey's ear until it went to sleep. Then it blew a mighty blast that shook the monkey to the ground.</p>
        <p>" Stop! stop, 0 wind !" the monkey cried. "You will uproot the mango-tree and crush me. You have won !"</p>
        <p>PRAYER FOR PEACE.</p>
        <p>Hark! O'er thu. dark-bluo water;. Heard ye the din of war? The dirge of Sorrow's daughters, The death-cry from afarit</p>
        <p>Cheeks that once glowed with gladness. With fear and grief are pale; Where ehildren played; in sadness Riseth the orphan's wail</p>
        <p>Oh, , while your song resoundeth, Breathe ye for then - a prayer; While joy your home surroundeth, For weeping mourners care. .</p>
        <p>.0, Lord our God! In Mercy Shorten the day; And wth Thine arm Almighty The strife of nations stay.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>Mijurtij lititts.</head>
        <p>Mijurtij lititts.</p>
        <p>"ALL THY CHILDREN."</p>
        <p>)rimiti‘e Methodists and the Impor- tance of the Young.</p>
        <p>"how many home circles are incomplete to- day owing to the war? But if I am talking to the parent heart that sighs for a son far away, engaged on his country's service, let me say: Don't be too distressed about it if your son has obeyed the call. It is something to be proud of, it you can only see it in the correct light. It is grand to have a son who is willing to stand up for all that makes life sweet and beautiful, for all that is representative of Christian "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>BEAUTIFUL MUSIC.</head>
        <p>BEAUTIFUL MUSIC.</p>
        <p>Thus spoke the Rev. C. L. Stowe (of Greenock) at the Legh-street Primitive Metho- dist Church, Warrington, on Sunday evening, on the occasion of the Sunday school anniver- sary. The Rev. Stowe formerly ministered in this circuit, and to this circumstance is no doubt to be attributed, in part, the excellence of the ,anniversary attendances. The whole of Sunday's celebration was notable for the splen- did singing of a choir of children and adults under Ni. A. Spruce. It is bare justice to say that to listen to Air. Spruce's choir on Sunday was a genuine pleasure for a lover of music. :Mr. F. Banner capably supported at the organ. in thc afternoon a beautiful rendering Wits given of a cantata entitled " Flower 'Voices," and at both morning and evening services the hand of choristers sank special hymns, acquitting themselves in everything they undertook. With very considerable credit to themselves, as well as to their able ,eonduetor. The Rev. Stowe briefly addressed the children in the afternoon. In the morning he delivered a helpful sermon to a large congregation. During the .day the sum of ..t:l7 2s. 8-Id. was taken in collections.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>SUNDAY SCHOOL INSUFFICIENT.</head>
        <p>SUNDAY SCHOOL INSUFFICIENT.</p>
        <p>Preaching in a crowded church in the even- ing, the Rev. Stowe took as his text, "Are here all thY, children?" Dealing with his subject in its home phase, the preacher uttered the words quoted above. Proceeding to tackle the church aspect of the question, he reminded his hearers that only twenty per cent of the children who passed through the Sunday school were retained by the Church. Many attached themselves to the congregation as listeners, but they did not become identified as actual members of the Church, and it was to be feared that thousands of them lived a. life of drift. "We ought," the rev. gentleman went on. "to 'ask ourselves whether we have attached those children to the Church in early life; whether we have made them feel that the Christian Church is their spiritual home. We don't ask mothers to bring their babies to the church services, hut we do ask that they will allow their names to be put on the Church cradle-roll. In cases where this is done, the Church never loses sight of the little ones. Each year they receive a birthday card from the Church, and from the earliest possible moment in their lives they are made to feel that the Church is their home. It is not sufficient merely to send a child to Sunday school; that is primarily a teaching institu- tion. We can't afford to let our little ones miss the spell of public worship."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>MELODY AND ANALOGY.</head>
        <p>MELODY AND ANALOGY.</p>
        <p>Preacher-Nlusician s and Lectures</p>
        <p>at Bold-street.</p>
        <p>In the Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Mon- day evening, the Rev. W. Fiddian Moulton, of Cliff College, Derbyshire, gave an organ recital. foll Owed by an address on " The Parable of the Organ," the Rev. 11. Williamson presiding.</p>
        <p>NOT THE CASE: BUT THE SOUL THAT</p>
        <p>MATTERS.</p>
        <p>The exterior of a n iwrgau, said Mr. Moulton— after he had enchanted the ears of all present with a delightful selection of organ music— possessed a 'certain interest, and some organs were made more interesting than others. An organ often possessed wonderful colouring, but after all it was not the outside of the instru- ment Which was the most interesting. The application of the Divine Will to, human life might be lacking, even when a congregation possessed a fine chapel, a splendid organisation, an admirable system and plenty of money. The action of the Hol3;. Spirit may be obstructed, and that was, perhaps, the lesson of the shrink- age in their church membership.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>Parable of Diversified Service.</head>
        <p>A Parable of Diversified Service.</p>
        <p>"Something was obstructing the work of the Divine Spirit, • and the music did not come. But what an improvement, what an inspira- tion, a good organ often proved to be. The organ was a parable of diversified service. There were in some organs ,rniles, of pipes which did not sound,. The question might be asked: Are these pipes useless? The answer was in the negative, for although they did not sound themselves, they helped something else to sound. In that fact they had an analogy of their church admiiiistration. They made a great mistake if they too, rigidly divided their church officers. The preacher and Sunday school teacher might be sacred, but they were. apt to he very doubtful about the trustee, the treasurer, the chapel steward, and the chapel- keeper. . (Laughter.) They looked upon the duties of those officers as secular. and forgot that there was a definitely spiritual function in. them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>Secular Prayer Meetings; Sacred Bazaars.</head>
        <p>Secular Prayer Meetings; Sacred Bazaars.</p>
        <p>If they chose, they could make a prayer meet- ing secular, and a bazaar sacred; it depended upon the spirit in which they wetit to work. The sacred .functions were performed by the preacher, but there were some chapel-keepers who made their function as sacred as any in the church. (Hear, hear, and applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>Organ's Infinite Variety.</head>
        <p>The Organ's Infinite Variety.</p>
        <p>The organ, the speaker proceeded, possessed a great deal of Variety, and there were in it stops that would produce sounds that might be either loud or soft. Those gave the musician the foundation for his melody. There were stops, also which gave variety of tone, and stops which were used 'solely for picking out a melody. Was there not another analogy in those facts? The things which belonged to the foundation counted for most. What a pitiable thing was a man who poSsessed brilliant gifts, but no self-control. The decorative character- istics did not count, but the foundation charac- teristics told all the time. The men who kept their ends up amid many discouragements were the greatest figures in Methodism to-day. The men who did ,their .simple duty year in and year out, and who only worked for the approval of the Master were to In compared to the foundation stops of the organ; they were the foundation from which was built up the swell- ing melody of church life. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>MUSICAL PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>THE MUSICAL PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>During the evening Mr. Moulton played the following selections on the organ :—Fantasia, M. Brosig; (a) Romance and (b) Madrigal, E. H. Lemare; Spring Song and Berceuse, A. Hollins; Canzona, W. Wolstenholme. Mrs; Booth sang "From Mighty Kings" (Handel), and Miss Whitmore sang "Sun of My Soul" (Adams).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>ORGANIST IN THE PULPIT.</head>
        <p>THE ORGANIST IN THE PULPIT.</p>
        <p>. On Sunday morning and evening the organ ist- preacher occupied the pulpit at the Bold- street Church, where were held the anniversary services in connection with the Ellesmere-street Wesleyan Methodist Sunday School. There Were large congregations at each service, and the eloquent and appropriate sermons preached by the Hey. W. F. -Moulton were greatly enjoyed, as was also an address by him in the afternoon. Miss E. _Taylor and Miss •D. Cain gave solos. . At the afternoon service, special music was rendered by the choir, assisted by a number of friends. A quartet party, consisting of Mrs. Warburton and Miss Plunipton, and Messrs. Warburton and Clayton gave a number of selections, and Miss Eva Tanner (the 'Temper- ance Queen) sang solos in her usual cha,rming mann&amp; Mr. J. G. Withinshaw, of ,Penketh, occupied the chair, and Mr. F. Hickman pre- sided at the organ, whilst Mr. A. 'Prow officiated as conductor.</p>
        <p>The eulleetiwo fuf the du y relied about £lB,</p>
        <p>GUARDIANS' CHAIRMAN. Warrington Board Re-elect Mr. A. J. Jolley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
