      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>COUNTY BOUNDARY</head>
        <p>THE COUNTY BOUNDARY</p>
        <p>QUESTION.</p>
        <p>At their annual meeting on Wednesday morn- ing, the Warrington Guardians, unanimously re- elected as their chairman for the ensuing • year Mr. A. J. Jolley, who had served them so well during the past twelvemonth, presiding over their deliberations in a most businesslike manner, and displaying. unimpeachable impartiality in the manner in which he eonducted the discussions, as well as a deep knowledge of the intricacies of the Poor Law.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jolley's re-election was proposed by Mr. J. Billing° in felicitous terms, and was seconded by Mr. G. Mather.</p>
        <p>Returning thanks i'or his re-eleetiou, Mr. Jolley said be had always tried to conduct the business of the Board with fairness all round. lie hoped the ensuing year would be as pleasant as the past one had been. (Hear, bear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>VICE-CHAIRMAN ALSO RE-ELtCTED.</head>
        <p>VICE-CHAIRMAN ALSO RE-ELtCTED.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. W. Knowles proposed, Mr. W. Flanagan seconded, and it was carried unani- mously, that Mr. W. Furness be re-elected vice- chairman, proposer and seconder both paying tributes to the good work which the Vice- chairman had done as a member of the Board. BURTONWOOD TO BE 'REPRESENTED ON ASSESSMENT COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The various committees of the Board were chosen, the appointment of only one of them giving rise to -difference of opinion; viz., the Assessment Committee, on which the Guardians were asked to elect a member in the place of the Rev. T. R. Matthews, of Earlestown, who has been ill for some time, and finds it impos- sible to attend the meetings. A proposition was brought forward that the ReV. A. Mitchell be appointed in place of his brother cleric. To this Mr. W. C. Brooks proposed an amendment that Mr. W.• •j. Shaw- be elected. His ground for doing so, he said, was that the Warrington district embraced two-thirds of the population of the Union, and also had two- thirds of the rateable value and, consequently, in his opinion, it should have, more representa- tion than it at present had. He further pointed out that the whole of the outlying districts could not be represented on the committee unless the number of members was to be increased and that in his opinion, Burtonwood, which was the; parish to which the • Rev. A. M. Mitchell be- longed, had a prior claim to representation among the outlying parishes. • Mr. J. Billinge seconded, pointing out that Burtonwood was a growing district. It was an important agricultural and industrial parish, and ought to have representation on the com- mittee. as it had six years ago. On the proposition being put to the meeting, five members voted each way, and the Chair- man gave his casting vote for the amendment, with the result that Mr. Shaw was added to the committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>"AMATEUR NURSES" FOR SOLDIERS?</head>
        <p>"AMATEUR NURSES" FOR SOLDIERS?</p>
        <p>Mr. G. Mather asked whether the special com- mittee appointed to administer the new infirm- ary block, which has been placed at the dis- posal of the War Department for use as a mili- tary hospital for sick and wounded soldiers, were going to engage any amateur nurses?</p>
        <p>The Chairman: The committee will make a statement later on, if the .Board will defer the matter</p>
        <p>PICTURES AND TOYS.</p>
        <p>It was decided to accord a vote of thanks to Miss Taylor, of Paul-street, for a gift of pic- tures for the Workhouse, and Mrs. Long, of Thelwall Heyes, for presenting the Cottage Homes, ,Padgate, with a large rocking horse, a dolls' house, &amp;c. WAR RISE FOR SCRUBBERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. A. Hughes moved that the rate of pay for scrubbers engaged by the Guardians be in- creased from 30. per hour to 31d., with the usual emoluments. Ms reason for doing so, he said, was that the cost of living was much greater than it used to be. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell, in seconding. said he thought it would have been better if Mr. Hughes had .proposed a rise 1 .td. per hour, but he would give his support to I he proposition on the ground that half a loaf is better than no bread.. Tie 'did not know whether the increase proposed would be equivalent to the increase in the cost of living. The proposition was carried unanimously.. THE ALTERATION OF THE COUNTY BOUNDARIES.</p>
        <p>The Clerk (Mr. A. Bottomley) read a Hier from the Local Government Board, asking the Guardians to send along as early as possible any comments they might have to make upon the proposed l'rovisional Order for the alteration of the boundaries of Lancashire and Cheshire. Mr. Mather said that the circular from the Local Government Board did not give sufficient information as to what was actually done. An inspector of the Board field an inquiry in .Mati- chester some -Months ago, and afterwards gave his report to the 'effect that the part of Main which was in Cheshire should be given to Lancashire, and left the two counties—Lanca- shire and Cheshire—to arrange between them- selves the rest of the boundary question, as from Mani to Warrington.. The present circular, the speaker complained, gave no idea of what the two counties had dime.</p>
        <p>The Clerk explained that somb months ago it was stated by the Local Government Board that the application of the Lancashire County Council had been granted, so far as certain places were concerned. Before issuing an Order, however, they'said that they would like the Lancashire and Cheshire County Councils to meet and see if they could arrange with regard to other plots of land which were not directly considered at the inquiry, with a view to such plots being included in one Order. He took it that the two County Councils concerned had not been able ,to arrange between 'them- selves, and therefore the Local Government Board had proceeded to make an Order along the lines of the findings of tneir inspector. According to a letter which he (the speaker) read to the Board at their,luqt meeting, a plot which was' at present in ,'Che'shire would be added to the Warrington Union. Mr: G. Mather: Is that the only plot which is going in be interfered with? The Clerk: Yes. • The Chairman: The other land is, apparently, dropped.</p>
        <p>Mr Mather proposed that the clerk .be in- structed to inform the Local Government Board that the Guardians considered that the =whole area under consideration when the iatter of the alteration of boundaries was first mooted, should be transferred to Lancashire, thus making the centre of the Ship 'Canal the boundary up to the County *Borough of War- rington.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said that the feeling of the Board wao very strong in the matter in the first instance, but what effect. a restilution of the kind proposed would produce, he did not know. It would be at once said by the other side that they were equally strongly opposesl to the suggestion and the .net result .would be that matters would be left as they were, for the Local Crovermuent Board were supposed to occupy an important Position. Mr Shaw seconded Mr. Mather's prOposition, remarking that the suggestion that the centre of the Ship Canal should form the boundary seemed to be a most sensible one. The proposition was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>Revelation.</head>
        <p>A Revelation.</p>
        <p>The delicious crispness, freedom from all trace of greasiness, and the perfect digestillility, enly obtainable when ATORA.Beef ,Suet is used for frying fish and pancakes, is a revelation. Ask your grocer fur ATORA INniocii and refuel oulbtitute6.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>GERMAN CONFIDENCE WEAKENING</head>
        <p>GERMAN CONFIDENCE WEAKENING</p>
        <p>A Change of Spirit.</p>
        <p>(FROM AN EYE-WITNESS AT HEAD- QUARTERS.)</p>
        <p>The-following descriptive account, which has been communicated by an eye-witness present with Oeneral headquarters, continues and supplements the narrative published en the Bth inst., of the movements of the British Force and the French Armies in immediate touch with it</p>
        <p>April 9th</p>
        <p>It is a significant fact that although a month has passed since the action at Neuve Chapelle, the enemy has made no attempt to retaliate in any quarter, but has remained inactive, while we have established oitrselves seeurely (Hi the ground won. Looking hack over the past. four months it is instructive to note the gradual weakening of the Uerman resistance on our front. Formerly any offensive action oniour part was met with an immediate counter- stroke. - This is the first occasion on which the enemy has made no reply at all. . .</p>
        <p>_This does not mean-, of course, that, their resistance is collapsing; but the- Clertuans have none the less admitted that with the troops at their disposal on this front they are unable to avenge their defeat. This is clearly seen by a proclamation 'already quoted in the Press, issued • by the Crown . Prince llupprecht of Bavaria to his troops after the battle, in which, after admitting the defeat, he seeks to encourage them by a vague reference to a future day of reckoning, and it is no doubt with a view to keeping alive these hopes that the soldiers are told • that Field-Marshal Hindenburg will shortly appear in France with a fresh army. -PANIC AT LILLE.</p>
        <p>Some light has been thrown on the conditions lately prevailing in the area behind the German lines, which serves to confirm the impression that the general •situation is creating great anxiety. As already stated, the Germans are evacuating large numbers of the civil popula- tion by way of Switzerland, and Many of these have now returned to France. From their evi- dence it would appear that something very like a panic prevailed in Lille during the fighting at Neuve Chapelle. The large hospitals were all removed to Tournai, and a great many German officers who had been billeted in Lille went to Tournai to sleep. Both officers and men openly expressed their uneasiness. Apparently a marked change began to occur in the general demeanour of the German troops soon after Christmas. Till then they ban been absolutely confident and extremely cheerful, but this feeling has been steadily decreasing. Latterly the soldiers used to tell the French civilians that they recognised that they would be overpowered by the masses that the English were going, to place in the field, and tried to persuade them that the- English were equally bent on ruining France and grabbing every- thing for themselves.</p>
        <p>The evacuation of the inhabitants was noti- fied by means of .placards stating that the Eng- lish. being unable to beat the Germans, were trying to starve them, and that in. consequence all the 'inhabitants must be evacuated except the men of military age between 18 and 50, who are still detained. The German war levy on Lille, Roubaix and Toureoing for the months of March and April amounted to £200,000. Against this exorbitant demand the mayors pro- tested. hut the Germans replied that as the mayors paid all refugees one franc a day. and as the refugees were about to be evacuated the money thus saved could in future be devoted to German war levies. A great deal of the machinery used in the cloth factories, especially that which has copper parts is being removed to Germany.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>GERMAN CALLOUSNESS.</head>
        <p>GERMAN CALLOUSNESS.</p>
        <p>Their treatment of some of their own wounded was extremely callous. The detailed facts of one case are as follows: On the night of March 16-17, in the course of a reconnaissance made in front of the British trenches, an officer with the reconnoitring party saw ‘lights to his rear. Thinking that they were signs of a hostile patrol, he took precautions in accordance. The next night a further reconnaissance in the same direction was made by a larger force under three officers. Lights were 'again 'seen and this time cries of "Kamerad " were heard. On fur- ther search two German soldiers were found. One of them, Ludwig Opendoffer, of the 21st Bavarian Reserve Regiment, had been wounded in the head and the other, No. 2510 Edward Schmidt, of the 20th Bavarian Reserve Regi- ment, had been shot through the left eye. Both had been lying out helpless since March 12, and were in a shocking state, gangrene having set in in one case.</p>
        <p>These were the only still living men found, but it was clear that many of the bodies in the trench were those of men who had crawled in. tiinca the spot .at which they were was only 60 or 70 yards from the German line and 50 yards from where the enemy were erecting entangle- ments, it is impossible to believa that their presence was unknown to their comrades, for their cries could have been. heard 100 yards away and they were at such a distance from our front trenches that they could have been re- moved with perfect safety. Both were conveyed. back to our lines and then to hospital, where they are believed to be doing well. 'The soldiers who actually picked them up and carried them were what the enemy calls " black savages," and they performed this act of rescue under fire and at the risk of their lives. Two other Germans who had been left out in similar circumstances, No. 2898, name nnknown, of the 20th Bavarian Reserve Regiment, and Adolf Gamer, of the 16th Prussian Regiment, had been brought in on the 15th.- :The units which left these men lying on the ground are believed to have ,been the 16th and 56th Regi- ments of Prussian Infantry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>ABOUT YOUR DAUGHTER. No Longer A Child.</head>
        <p>ABOUT YOUR DAUGHTER. No Longer A Child.</p>
        <p>Perhaps you have already noticed that your daughter in her "teens" has developed a fitful temper, is restless and excitable, and often in need of gentle reproof. In that case, remember that .the 'march of years is leading her .on to womanhood, and at this time a great responsi- bility rests upon you as parents. If your daughter is pale, complains of weak- ness and depression, feels tired- out after a little exertion; if she tells you of headache or backache, or pain In the side, do not disre- gard these warnings. Your daughter needs help, for she is most probably antemicthat is bloodless. Should you notice any of these dis- turbing signs, lose no time but procure for her Dr. Williams' Pink Pills' for her unhealthy- girlhood is \ bound to lead to unhealthy woman- hood.</p>
        <p>Dr. Williams' Pink lulLs for pale people enrich the impoverished blood: of girls and women, and 'by doing so they repair waste and prevent disease. They give to siekly, droopin; girls health, brightness dint eharm, with colour in the eheeks, sparkling eyes a light step and high spirits.. Let your (laughter begin them to- day; any dealer supplies these pills; but never accept comthon pink pills; ask for Dr. Williams'. FREE.---Of interest to all girls and women • is the little book. "Plain talks to women," offered free to lady readers who send it postcard for a copy to Hints Department, 16 Holborn Viaduct, London.</p>
        <p>Secret of Popularity., The -secret of popularity is to make everybody you meet feel that you are specially interested in him. If you really feel kindly towards others, if you sincerely wish to please, you will have no difficulty in doing so." But if you are cold, indifferent, retiring, silent, selfish; if you are all wrapped up in yourself .and think only of what may advance your own interests or in- crease your uwu cutufurt, yuu uUver can become PuP War.</p>
        <p>TRAGEDY OF REJECTI'</p>
        <p>Ex-Soldier AI 10) 11 :oiled 10 1111 L4llllllll'l.</p>
        <p>- --- "It is a very sad case. This poor fell°lcril served his country in India. We '°'°d the the climatothere has a serious effect tot 7 our soldiers. Inspired with love for 1115,, try, Ito again came forward, but ultforttl'' hdi e was scharged us m 11 edically nut. j These words were addressed to a Warrington Coroner's Court on Wr",,iit morning, by Mr. S. Brighouse, who inquest on the body of lleorge illubort4,o aged 44, a wire-worker, who resided at toitl soy-road. Warrington, and who CO suicide on Monday by cutting his throat'</p>
        <p>Eight Years in !neje. The .deceased man, it was stated, wa'1;00. member of the Royal Engineers, with nid served for eight years in India. lie ail to' the War Office in December to be allowe,to ! join his old regiment, and permisOolv granted. lie was, however, after a ilj,Alt training, found to be medically unfit, a:o' accordingly discharged. His inabilitY it! in the way be had desired seems to have,l;je on his mind and caused him to end bi°</p>
        <p>Gash From Ear To Ear. I,(` Evidence was given at. the inquest itiiC ceased's sister, who deposed to finding a the kitchen on Monday morning with toi gash in .his throat extending from ear to inflicted with a razor. He was taken- v. • Workhouse Hospital, where he the'l ,) after admission. The jury returned a verdict of whilst of unsound mind." The deceased had latterly been 000° the WhitecrosS Works.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>STORY OF THE LIT BLMA DOG."</head>
        <p>THE STORY OF THE LIT BLMA DOG."</p>
        <p>Tragedy of the AA'ar.</p>
        <p>The following is token from the le-PAit cavalry officer written from the frenetic'</p>
