      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>11N THE couß Us.</head>
        <p>11N THE couß Us.</p>
        <p>STOLEN 110(YM</p>
        <p>Two Men Charged With Shdp-Lifting.</p>
        <p>orrNvo. men of the labouring class—Michael nv_atleY and Wm. Berry—who ' have no fixed de, were before the Borough, Magistrates on nd 4m.0 • aY, charged with being concerned in steal- lag - from outside the shop, 83, Bridge-street, a Pair of men's boots, valued at us. 9d., the Property of Messrs. Sterlings Ltd. - The Chief Constable (Mr. Martin Nicholls) in asking for a remand, said the police had a 1101:11nber of inquiries to complete. At five cl°e on Saturday evening one of the Prisoners was seen to " lift " a .pair of boots fsr°l3l outside Messrs. Sterling's shop. The 2esand man was with the first and, after the 2eft,' they went 'along Rose and Crown-street. ;)11,e gentleman who keeps a shop adjoining that • 81°11ging to Messrs. Sterling, saw the theft and ljneiforined the manager of the boot shop When men were arrested one of them admitted Lae theft and the other said "We could have ------„ been in London before the police got us, 'I We had liked!" One of the prisoners, pointing to his co- "cuPont of the dock said "The first time in t!'l3'. life that I ever spoke to this man was at attirday dinner-time. ' The Chief Constable: Ile was with the other until after the bobts were stolen. The men were itmanded. ,When the prisoners were brought up on •rridaV morning, Gatley, against whom there t6ere previous convictions, including another one r .stealing boots, was sentenced to a month's . prisonment with hard labour, whilst the other htan wag' fined 10s., with seven days' imprison- bent as the alternative.</p>
        <p>W. Hayward (chairman), Alderman Smethurst, and Messrs. W. Williams and C. Jackson .t tohnes.</p>
        <p>" MR. JOHN HEATON, OF</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON."</p>
        <p>Six Months for "A Man of Renown."</p>
        <p>"Disgraceful in the extreme" was the..descro- tion applied at the Borough Pollee. Court on Nitonday to the conduct of John Heaton, a talka- five middle-aged man of unkempt appearance, who was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Horsemarket-street on Saturday, last, and further with having assaulted Detec- tive Albert Skelton whilst in the execution of his duty.—Asked to plead, the prisoner said: "I didn't assault him, sir !" The evidence was to the effect that at 8.15 on Saturday night a constable, on duty in Horse- niarket-street, saw prisoner leaning against the window-sill of the Griffin Hotel. He was obviously drunk and was shouting out to passers-by. When spoken to by the constable he said "I won't go away. I am Mr. John Heaton, of Warrington; a man of renown." Told that he must go away or be arrested, he announced his .intention of staying where he was. The- constable, thereupon, took him into custody, Detective Skelton coming to his aid. Prisoner grew violent and kicked the detective several times about the legs .and body.</p>
        <p>7. Soiciething of a " Linguist." When he was got clear of the Market-place he redoubled Violence and the police officers had to call for further assistance. Eventually, prisoner was placed on a handcart and was con- veyed to the Police Station. When in the Police Station he used filthy language—" more filthy language I have never heard," said one of the witnesses—about the.Xayor and the Chief :Constable .Detective Skelton having detailed the kicks he received, the prisoner observed to him "Look here! .I am verysorry, but I haven't hurt you very much have IF"- As to " abusing the Chief Constable" he added' "I Merely said Idon't -want to see you to-night, sir, but I hope you'll come in the morning!". (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>CYCLIST Al"I'M :KM</head>
        <p>CYCLIST Al"I'M :KM</p>
        <p>What Happened In Loncl Lane.</p>
        <p>1:111(;11:"YlaYing of a cyclist in a lonely lane rin ,8e 0 41:" the County Police Court, War- of Orford Wednesday, when Percy Waywell, lane Cottages at Orford on A ' Suramoned John Farrell, ,of Sandy- pOrriflo4rtdb,. for assault and battery CoMplainant was' represented by Mr- A. Browne; Farrell was undefended. „ Mr. Browne said his client worked for a tarmer at Orford, and defendant was employed at a tannery• On Easter Sunday,. morning his Client was cycling towards the farm' where he as employe, when defendant sprang out from the hedge at the side of the road and knocked 6 l,htt from his machine. When he rose he i -nocked him down -again, lie struck him at least 46. dozen blows and said, ",Why didn't you keep Tommy out of the stable?" Complainant, aPPeared, had a younger brother; who had annoyed defendant by going into his stable. ollowing up. the account of .the assault; Mr. Browne said that his client's face was bleeding. and he had to go- home from the farm, after (lays, bandaged up. lie had to stay at home for l'ays, as he was unfit for work: He was at- tended by Dr. Dickens. Both eyes were blacked. An offer was made of an apology if the case were withdrawn, a fact which contained an admis- sion of guilt. Complainant whose eyes were bloodshot, and tt 'no flesh all round discoloured,' gave evidence of the assault. defence offered by Farrell was that he did 'to knock complainant off his bicycle, but Vit him fairly. It was a mutual "scrap." The Bench, in fining defendant 55., intimated, through the Chairman, that they were letting • off, lightly, and that lie had no right what- . to act as •lie had. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>BLACK SHEEP OF INDUSTRY.</head>
        <p>BLACK SHEEP OF INDUSTRY.</p>
        <p>AV orlimen AA ho Bring Disgrace On</p>
        <p>Others.</p>
        <p>this is one of the workmen who bring dis- VrT on the industrious workmen," said Mr. J. W.A 'layward, as. chairman of the Bench, at the paVsrington .Police Court on Wednesday, in sentence of one month's hard labour on Arzethy Donohue, a labourer, giving the ad- of a lodging-house in Friars' Green, who Charged with being drunk and disorderly I,'lll creating a disturbance in Dallam-lane on • IjsclaY evening. tiv,e,teetive-Sergeant Slimmers proved the case, "ng that the prisoner was employed at Dal- Porge, He was drunk and creating a big Nvo,;,..._llthanee at the forge, ,impeding the other atl(7len, and making himself a general nuis- rq,,.e• Ile was asked to leave the premises, but 1,1!W to do so; and was then ejected. it :le Chief Constable said that prisoner made forB',"tenient to the effect that he was looking mdle 4ecruiting Office. WI71; Hayward asked the Chief Constable (.7hN'er prisoner was willing to enlist, and the ei Constable replied that he didn't think so. of2riscner had wanted to find the Recruiting "mee he could easily have done so.</p>
        <p>A WASSAILER AND WALLFLOWER. til'lrtother -case heard at the same Court was kbat, of Herbert Glynn. a labourer, of no fixed 1 was charged with. being drunk and )rderly in Queen-street on Tuesday evening. ?C. Steele said that prisoner was leaning Ilgainst . a •wall and was very drunk, and was also uting after passers-by. Witness requested toln go away and be quiet, but he wasn't able -up when he &amp;ape away from the wall. 6entence • of one month's hard labour was vasse.ll by the Bench, which consisted of Mr. J.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs. J. Rytner Young (in .the chair), A. J. Jolley, W. T. Williams, and W. Roberts, . . Naden. _ _</p>
        <p>and 'Dr</p>
        <p>An Unfortunate Maximum—Frederick Looker, of 33. Pierpoint-street, appeared for having been drunk in Foundry-street at 10.30 the previous night. He pleaded guilty.—P.C. Barr said the man lay across the footpath, and, when.he-lifted him up Could not stand.—The Chief Constable stated that Looker had been convicted several times for drunkenness, but unfortunately the maximum fine they could inflict was •ten shil- lings. The man was an iron turner—just the class which should not. get into that condition ! —ln imposing the maximum fine; the Chairman said the man had a strange idea of his duty, and the Bench were sdrry they could not fine him more. How She Drowned Her Sorrow.—Margaret Gallagher, of 20, Queen Anne-street, Liverpool, was charged with having been drunk the pre- vious afternoon on the Central Station.—P.C. Barr proved the case.—The Chief Constable said the woman was a stranger, against whom the police had no record.—Prisoner: I Wanted to come and see my mother in Pierpoint-street .over my brother being killed. • [Crying.] I had some stout, and don't remember any more, sir. —The Chairman: Do you always have stout?— Prisoner: No, not before, • sir.-,-The Chairman: Now, look here. If we take no notice of this will you promise not to get (hunk again ?—Pris- oner: Yes, sir!—The case was accordingly dis- missed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
