      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>Cup Competition.</head>
        <p>Cup Competition.</p>
        <p>The bowling season may now be considered to be under weigh, and .afirtiady trial games have been played on most of the local greens, a specially large number of. enthusiasts lore- gathering- on the turf at the Horse and -Jockey, Winwick-road, on Saturday afternoon • and in- dulging in a number of friendly games. - The Amateur Works' Association's tourney nommenees to-day, the draw for the first round• having been made at the Horse and jockey on Monday evening, resulting. as follow</p>
        <p>This Afternoon. 2.—Armitage &amp; v. Wal v.o. 2. &amp; K. Engineers v. _Howley Tonnery. 3.30.—Warrington Tannery v. Monk, 4.ls.—Firth Mattress V. Orford Tannery. • 5.0.-31erse3r White Lead V. P. &amp; Bewsey. ,Referee, Mr. J.. Willett.</p>
        <p>Saturday, May Ist. 2.o.—Walkel's No. 1 v. Penketb Tannery. 2.4s.—Crosfield's Chemicals v. Fletcher," Russell. 3.3o.—Cheshire Lines No. 1 v. Longford No. 1. 4.ls.—Longford No. tt. v. Vulcan Engineers. s.o.—Grappenhall Tannery v. Randall Bros.' Tannery. Referee, Mr. S. Shave. Saturday, May Bth. - 2.0.—8. •A. Naylor's V. Rylandes No. 2. 2.45 Guest Bros.' Tannery V. Bewsey Wire Roi'wry. • 3.3o.—Crosfield's Cheshire V. Whitecross No. 1. 4.ls.—Assul'alce Agents No. 2 v. Cheshire Lines No. 2. ' s.o.—Crosfield's Soap. Department v. Parkinson's Limited. Referee, gr. Ir. Trafford.</p>
        <p>• Saturday, May 15th. 2.o.—Whiterross-Netting Fennell-Ft Tannery. 12.45.—Hylands's No. 1 v. Crosfield's Engineefs. 3.30.-I)lulUbels &amp; Painters v. Crosfield's V.B. 4.ls.—Assulance Agents No. 1. V. Greening's. s.o.—Vulcan Foundry v. County Asylum. • Referee, Mr. F. Dean. Whitecross Wiredrawers,Na bye. The open handicap competition will coin; mence on Miy 2211 d, and the proceeds will be • , in aid of the local war funds.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant Donald Seymour-Jones wak born in 1892, and was dducated at` Wellington College, Salop, and at the Leathersellers' Tech- nical College, London. He .received a thorough technical training also at the Cambrian Leather Works; Wrexham, making. himself familiar with the practical part of light leather work lie joined the 4th Royal Welsh Fusiliers about seven years ago as a signaller, signing on for imperial Service. Lieutenant Donald Seymour-Jones retired in May, 1914, to proceed to .America, where he was to take up an appointment On the outbreak of war, however, he at once took a commission in the above battalion, and after a period of train- ing he proceeded to France on November sth Inst. His battalion has constantly been in the thiek of ~the fighting, especially round the neigh- bourhood of La Basse. . PREPARING FOR THE FRONT.</p>
        <p>The other brother, Lieutenant Frank, is the third son of Air. A. Seymour-Jones, of Wrex- ham, and like his elder\ brothers is 'a firm be- lieVer in preparing for war .by preliminary trAning in times of peace. He was educated at Wellington College, Salop, and later con- firmed his studies at the Leather Industries Dep.' -tment of Leeds University under Professor H. R. Procter. In fact, on the outbreak of war he was studying for his degree in applied chemistry. He is also a Brown scholar. Lieutenant F. •L. Seymour-JoneS joined the Leeds University Officers Training Corps in September, 1913. and attained the rank of corporal before leaving to take up a commission ,in the Royal Welsh Fusiliers iii November last. Like his brothers', Lieutenant. F. L. Seymour- Jones is anxious to "do his bit," and will pro- ceed to the front shortly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <p>THE EXAMINEn, S k WM/AY, APRIL 24, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>FIELD OF</head>
        <p>FIELD OF</p>
        <p>To Play In The Final F The Cinrkzy " Cup.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S LAST MATCH.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S LAST MATCH.</p>
        <p>Season Ends With Away Game Again Widnes.</p>
        <p>To=day • Warrington meet Widnes at Wid- nes. !• This will be the last match of the sea- son, so far as Warrington are concerned, and it will also be their second meeting with Wid- nes this year. The last meeting between the teams was at Wilderspool on March 20th, when a draw of two points each was the re- sult. For to-day's game Warrington will be represented by the !following : Full-back.—Jolley.</p>
        <p>Threequarter-backs.—Baker,, Laws, Tranter, and Bradshaw. Half-backs.—Dainteth and Young. • Forwards.—Cox, Fearnley, W. _Cunliffe, T. Cunliffe; Green, and Harmer. The kick-off is timed for 3.30.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>IaY.IRRINGTON NVOIIKINGMEN'S</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>N.A. CENTRE.</p>
        <p>The weather is still unfavourable for the trout angler. What is really wanted is a good week of warm weather, so as to put them more on the move. The Goivy is .yielding well in numbers,- but specimens are lacking and until we get a week's good weather there will be few taken.</p>
        <p>Two of the officials paid that once favourite fishery, Acton Bridge, a. visit on .Sunday, as they had information from the right quarter that fair sport was to be obtained beyond North- wich. They paid a visit. to a resident who is always passing along the bank of the canal, and he satcl.that he had seen one or two rise opposite his cottage; one in particular was swimming in the shallows, about Goz. The officials walked from Preston Brook tunnel to Acton Bridge along the canal without seeing a sign of life in it, and at present we can hold no hope for members of it becoming fishable below ,North- wich.</p>
        <p>A general meeting was held at- headquarters on Monday. There was a fair muster. The date and places of matches were decided. the opening match is on July 26t1 in the Weaver at Barrow Lock, on the left bank of the river, the ground- to be . pegged ° out from the lock and working. towards .11artford Bridge. The annual match is on September 12th sat Newbridge, starting from the Salt Works..</p>
        <p>Specials to the 1-mount of .0 ss. are given, but a full list of dates of matches, specials, &amp;C., will appear later, when the affiliated clubs have fixed. •</p>
        <p>A general meeting is to be lield at the Bay Horse next Monday, at 7.30 p.m. A.S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>OFFICERS THREE!</head>
        <p>OFFICERS THREE!</p>
        <p>Lieutenant IL A. Semour-Jones' TWO</p>
        <p>Soldier Brothers.</p>
        <p>It ,is not generally known that Lieutenant R. Arnold Seymour-Jones, the first to be killed of the Warrington Territorial Officers serving at the Front, has two soldier-brothers, both of whom bear his.. Majesty's commission: viz.. Lieutenant Donald Seymour-Jones,' of the 4th Royal Welsh Fusiliefs, and Lieutenant Fiank L. Seymour-Jones, of the sth Royal . Welsh Fusiliers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>FOUGHT AT LA BASSE.</head>
        <p>FOUGHT AT LA BASSE.</p>
        <p>PROFESSOR'S SYMPATHY.</p>
        <p>After taking hit 4 degree as a B.Sc.. at Leeds university,, the late Lieutenant R. A. Seymour- Jones :was appointed •on the staff .of the rniversity as private assistant to Professor Procter. In this connection he specialised in L leather-tanning processes, and undertook im- portant research- work with Professor Procter, in connection with the acidity of tan liquors, the formic-mercury. hide -preservation and dis- infection process, the theory of tanning„ the application of 'electiieity to tanning, Sz.c. Tie was also a regular contributor to the trade press, and we have a very pleasant recollection of perusing his able " copy, "•on many occasions. In a sympathetic letter from Professor Procter he feelingly states:" that the late Lieutenant Seymour-Tones .was a young man good in every way, and with brains far beyond. the average, who had spent years of study and shown him- self capable of important work in industrial chemistry." It was 'after he left Leeds University that Lieutenant Seymour-Jones entered the service of Messrs. Crosfield and-Sons, of Warrington, as head physical chemist, specialising in research and did excellent work in connection with their vast and varied industry. In the works he was much liked for his geniality, and the firm tave sustained a loss which will be difficult to make up.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>STOP THAT RHEUMATISM.</head>
        <p>STOP THAT RHEUMATISM.</p>
        <p>"Stop it now, at. once! There is no need for you to suffer the pangs and agonies of Rheuma- tism, any longer. Every day, every hour almost, that you continue to suffrr is self-inflicted tor- ture." This emphatic statement is made •by Dr. Geo. E. Flimd, whose successful labours to find a suitable and effective remedy for Rheu- matism, and not merely a means of temporary relief, have made his name famous all the world _ -</p>
        <p>k For the benefit of our readers, we append the prescription which he recommends. It is : Syrup of Ginger , a , 'O2. Liquid Barkola Compound Syrup of Rhubarb 1 oz. Tincture of Gentian ,</p>
        <p>Dose: A teaspoonful after meals and at bed- time.- We have the assurance of the Doctor that this prescription (which can be dispensed by any chemist) can be 'taken with perfect safety by the most delicate man or woman. The first dose nsnally brings relief, and if persisted in for a very short time Wil4 result iii\a perfect cure of the ailment. -</p>
        <p>Scotland for Ever. Sister (writing letter to brother at the Front): "And hae ye anything else Lie 'say, father?" 'rather "Ay! Tell Donal' that if he conies ower you German waiter that gaed us a bad saxpence for change when we had a bit dinner in London a while syne, tell him—tae—tak— steady aim."—" Puna."</p>
        <p>The Great SankeiClub brought their season's fixtures to a close on Saturday, when on the Penketh Recreation Ground they, as champions of the 'Widnes and District Alliance, were opposed .by a team seleeted from the Rest of the League. The ground ,and the weather were all that could be desired for a hard and fast game, and in this respect the spectators were not disappointed. The Rest were not as strongly represented as they might have been, owing to, two of their clubs being engaged in cup-tie., The home team quickly assumed the aggressive and had the greater share of the game in the first half, for during the first half- hour's play Brindle -never handled the ball. In the second portion the visitors had rather more of the game, but were only dangerous a few times. The home team more than held their own, and at the call of .tiniA -ran out deserved winners -by four goals to hone,:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>DRAW AT STOCKTON HEA7H.</head>
        <p>DRAW AT STOCKTON HEA7H.</p>
        <p>- Notes by " Linesman."</p>
        <p>The second of the semi-final ties in the</p>
        <p>" Starkey " Cup , Competition was decided on the King and Queen ground on Saturday Owing to excitement among the players,, the game: on the whole was not of a very high standard. At the close the Walton team .were winners by two goals to one.</p>
        <p>A .sub-committee meeting of the " Starkey " Cup -Competition was held last Tuestlay .night, at the Lion Hotel, to consider a protest lodged by the Paddington Club against the Walton Club for playing an ineligible player in the semi-final tie. After the complaints had been heard and carefully considered, it was decided to award the Paddington • Club the match on the statement -of their protest. Now the Long- ford United and the Paddington Clubs will enter the final tie, after having previously been defeated in the competition, :both clubs 'being successful with their appeals against their 'opponents for playing ineligible 'players. It was decided to play the final -tie on May Ist, on the King and Queen ground, Padgate- lane.</p>
        <p>On Saturday- the meeting. of Fairfield -United and Longford United had to be postponed be- cause the former club were unable to raise a team owing to enlistments and injuries to players. It was unfortunate that this course had to be taken, as the result of this game would have, an important bearing on the championship, and if -at all possible will have to be re-arranged.</p>
        <p>At the third time of asking the Orford White Star and the Wairington Central teams met to play their postponed fixtures. The captains agreed that instead of playing two games that one should count for four points. The *play never reached a high standard, and neither team played up to their reputation. The White Star secured the verdict.</p>
        <p>Earlestown Mission. made their second jour- ney to town to oppose St. Barnabas' on the Lovely-lane ground. The game resulted in a division of points, but much annoyance was yoused by the behaviour of several of the Mission players, and ,at one time the referee threatened to stop the game.</p>
        <p>As both the Cadishead Wesleyans and the Stockton heath 'teams had gained first and second- places in the North Cheshire League, the League Committee decided that- a closing match should be played between these teams instead of the usual game between the Champions atO the Rest of the League. The game was played on the West-avenue ground, and a good crowd assembled to witness a keen and enjoyable game. The teams being very evenly matched, the visitors equalised the scores a few minutes before close of play. Mr. Grounas, the. chairman of the Stockton Heath club, had the honour of presenting the championship cup of-the-League to the captain of the Cadishead team. He made a very appropriate and suitable speech, and the captains of both teams responded in a very acceptable manner. .</p>
        <p>LEIGLE. Second Division.</p>
        <p>CHAMPIONS v. RUNNERS-UP.</p>
        <p>CADISHEAD WES.LEYANS v. STOCKTON</p>
        <p>HEATH-._</p>
        <p>The above teams met on Saturday on •the ground of the Heathens, in ideal football weather, and before a good. crowd of spectators. The following players represented their respec- tive clubs, under the control of Mr. Goddard:—</p>
        <p>Cadishead: Ellams; Thompson and Harrison; Dalby, Taylor, sen., and Taylor, jun.; Cornack, Stokes, Limes, Martin and Wright. Stockton !loath: Hooper; Chesters and Gos- ling; Jardine, Barlow and Rylance; Lloyd, Noden, Dean, Smith and Vernon. - The; .heathens won the toss. The visitors kicked off with the sun in their favour, .and at once maile a ;raid On the home citadel, but were repulsed by CheSters and Gosling. Then ploy was evenly contested for some time in mid- field. The visitors again attacked the home goal, and a good centre from Wright was handled in front of goal by Chesters. A penalty kick was given and Thompson scored with a hard drive after fifteen • minutes' play. Follow- ing this reverse some keen play was witnessed and each end was\ visited in quick succession, Lloyd being prominent for the Heathens. The visitors' forwards were often seen to advantage, but the respective defenders proved equal to the occasion, and the interval ' arrived with the score: Cadishead, 1 goal; Stockton Heath, 0. The Heathens played very vigorously on re- suming, and the sun troubled the Visitors' de- fenders. After five minutes' play Smith equalised the scores. Both teams now played hard to gain the lead, and the Heathens gradually gained the upper hand, and Ellams made .several good saves. The visitors retali- ated, but found the Heathens' .defenders very safe, and- play was again transferred to the visitors' quarters, where Vernon received .a good pass from 'Jardine and scored the second goal. The visitors responded in fine style and attacked thd home goal, and 'a few minutes .before the final wilistle Stokes equalised with a fine shot. Result: Cadishead Wesleyans, 2 goals; Stockton Heath, 2 goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>" STA I t KEY " CUP. Sent i- Final Tie. PADDINGTON v. WALTON</head>
        <p>" STA I t KEY " CUP. Sent i- Final Tie. PADDINGTON v. WALTON</p>
        <p>On! Saturday in the above competition, on the King and Queen ground, in splendid weather, the ground being in good- condition, the above teams met before a large crowd of spectators. The following .players turned out :--k• - Paddington: Harrison; Holmes and Spencer; Pritchard; Marsh and Griffiths; Garnett, Scarisbrick, Darbyshire, Wrench and Taylor. Walton: Tweedale; Be'rry and Mortimer; Siddall, Andrews and West; Green, Croft, Marsh, Hoose and Cartwright. Referee, Mr. J Kite. Paddington won the toss and the Waltonians sc4 the ball rolling. Marsh neatly robbed Hoose and Passed to Darbyshire and along with Wrench's,- carried play into the Walton goal area, where Tweedale was soon in action and cleared smartly. Mortimer was penalised and Garnett taking- the kick, Tweedale again saved in good style.. Following this Andrews was', prominent in accepting passes and breaking up the combination of Taylor and Darbyshire, and at the other end Marsh ,(Walton) with the .goal at his mercy, shot yards wide. Green was next .prominent with a good run along the touch-line, beating. Pritchard and Holmes, and testing Harrison with a good shot. 'Play was now transferred to the Walton end and Twm- dale lied to concede a corner from a shot by Taylor. This was well placed by Pritchard, but Berry cleared ,with a strong kick. noose and Cartwright combined well together, but the latter ran the ball over the line. Walton was again. penalised, Andrews being the offender, Garnett scoring from the free kick. From the centre kick the Walton forwards were seen to advantage, and a good effort by Cartwright forced Harrison to -give away a corner kick. This proved fruitless, and then West -neatly robbed Scarisbrick and transferred to Croft, and the hitter centred splendidly. Hoose gained Possession and shot hard for goal, the ball hit the upright and turned into the net. The interval score was one goal each. On resuming, the game had not been in pro- gress many minutes when, unfortunately for the Paddington team, Garnett was injrtred in a collision. with Siddall and was unable to take any further part in the game. Thus handi- capped, the Paddington men played the .one- back game. Cartwright and loose maile a good run and the 'latter centred from the touch-line, and Croft, with, only the goalkeeper to beat, shot straight at him, and' Holmes cleared. 'Taylor, trying an individual effort, was defeated by Andrews,. Marsh gained Ge lead for Walton with a good effort, and seven minutes before the, close of play Siddall got marching orders from the referee. Cartwright, Green, West and Berry were the pick of the Walton players,' and Garnett, Taylor and Harrison for the Padding- ten team: Taking the game on the whole the play was not of as high a standjird as expected. both sides being equally to Marne by poor shoot- ing in front of goal, .while the Paddington half- backs were _very weak, and the main cause of their being in arrears at the close of play. Result: Walton, 2 goals; Paddington, 1 goal. The match has since been awarded to Pad- dington on a protest.</p>
        <p>Great Sankey: Brindle; E. Glover and Kelley; 4. E. Glover, 'Millington and Payne; Whit field, Forster, Ditchfield and Banner. Rest of the League: Mitchell (Calder); Gutt- ridge (Clock Face) and Rowan '(St. •Paul's); Bennett (Calder), Hewson (CrontoA) and Par- kinson (St. Paul's); Melia (Ball o' Ditton), Ire- land (St.. Paul's), Hughes (Clock Face), • Lowe (St. Ambrose) and McLean (Calder). The home captain won the toss and decided to play with the slope in his favour, and Hughes set the ball rolling. Following the mid- field exchanges, the home team broke away on the left-wing, where Banner placed his final shot wide. From the goal-kick the visitors combined well together, but when they approached the danger zone E. clover relieved with a big kick, and the home team spoiled a good opening by getting offside. Now some good play was wit- nessed in .mid-field, but owing to the keen tack- ling of the home half-backs the visitors did not become dangerous, but they were gradually driven back to defend their own lines. Both Whitfield and-Ditchfield sent in good shots. The home team ,got a free kick for hands just out- side the penalty area, Whitfield taking the kick, sent in a .splendid shot, and Mitchell tied to put the ball over the bar; but it went under- neath, and thus ,the first goal was registered for the home teeth. From the centre kick the visitors made some good passing movements, but the home halves were equal to them, and Mil- lington passed out tp Banner, the latter trans- ferred "Play to the opposite end, and centring finely, Guttridge cleared finely from a crowd, of players. The game was now contested chiefly in the visitors' half, and another good shot by 'Banner was put over the bar by Mitchell. The ensuing corner-kick was well placed, - but eventually got away. At this period Mitchell, in the visitors' goal, made several fine clear- ances. Millington took a, free kick 30 yards from goal and scored with a splendid drive. After this second reverse the, visitors played more strenuously, and the home defenders had to stir themselves.' A shot was sent in by Lowe, and Brindle was called upon to handle for the first time during the match. Directly after this Ireland sent wide when he had a good chance of scoring. Half-time Great Sankey, 2 goals; League, 0.</p>
        <p>- The home team had to face the sun in the second half, but they made the first raid. Good combination by the visitors' forwards trans- ferred play to the home end, and from a- re- bounding ball Hughes tested Brindle, who proved to be safe. A free kick gave •relief to the home defence, but the visitors were quickly back again. Parkinson .set his forwards going. Kelley conceded a corner kick. This was got away, and then by the aid of several throws-in the home forwards got into the visitors' half, and the ball was sent out to the left and Ditch- field scored the third goal. Even play in mid- field followed, and then the home defenders gradually drove the visitors to defend their own lines, and Whitfield judiciously passed to Forster when the latter raced between the backs and scored the fourth goal. The ensuing Play was rather tame. E. Glover, Millington, Ditch- field and Whitfield were the pick of the home team; while Mitchell, Guttridge„ Hewsnn and Parkinson were the pick of the 1,-,gue. Result; Great Sankey, 4 goals; Rest of INigne, 0.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <p>MAPE YOUR WI LL on a " PERLEX " (Registered) Will-Form. Ordinary will-forms gonerally cause 088 and trouble to heirs. With the "Porlex legal mistakes are impossible. Price 116, from all stationers, stores, newsagents. bookstalk, or sent postpaid on receipt of postal order. Write-- THE REDMORE CO., /9, Sicilian Avenue, London, W.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>AVIDNES AND DISTRICT ALLIANCE.</head>
        <p>AVIDNES AND DISTRICT ALLIANCE.</p>
        <p>CHAMPIONSHIP MATCH.</p>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY v. REST OF THE LEAGUE. The Great Sankey team, as champions of the Widnes and District Alliance, were opposed by a team selected from the rest of the clubs in the c9mpetition, on the Penketh Recreation Ground. The weather was all that could be desired, and the ground was in good condition. There was a fairly good crowd of spectators when the following layers took up their' positions under the control of .31r. D. J. Davies, of Widnes:—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>XV.IIIItIN6TON AND. DISTRICT</head>
        <p>XV.IIIItIN6TON AND. DISTRICT</p>
        <p>LE.%6UE.</p>
        <p>OP.FORD WHITE STAR v. WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>CENTRAL.</p>
        <p>On the ground of the former. The White Star Won the toss and plaYed with the sun at their back. Straight from the kick-off the home team pressed, and for the first ten minutes they had the best of the exchanges, and then a. good individual effort by Hankinson, who started from the half-way line, beating all opposition and opened the score. From the centre kick good play in midfield followed, but was marred by an unfortunate accident to Brown, of the Central, and he was unable to take any further part in , the game. This left the visitors with only ten players, and the,enSuing play was more in favour of the home team. A. few minutes before the interval the\ home for- wards advanced in a body and Connor passed out to Hankinson., the latter Centred well, and the ball was handled inside the penalty area,</p>
        <p>and Connor scored frOm the kick: Half-time: White Star, 2 goals; Central, 0, On re-starting the White Star appeared to take matters more easily, and for twenty minutes play was even, after which 0: Day scored the third goal for the home. team. Another spell of even play was followed by the visitors taking up the offensive,. and Farrell scored for them, the ball hitting the post and rebounding Over the line. The remainder of the</p>
        <p>On Saturday the above teams met in their return fixture on the Lovely-lane ground, when the following players turned out St. Barnabas': Ray; P. Challinor and Long; Herbert, O'Neil and Robinson; H. Challinor, Woods, Gregson. Vernon and Lemons. Mission: H. Wallis; Winders and A. Eaton; .Alldridge, F. EatJn and Theobald ; W. Wallis, Taylor, Rothwell, Sherlock and Plumpton. Referee, Mr. B. Howell. .</p>
        <p>The home team won the, toss and the visit Ors started with the sun and wind against them. Plumpton worked the ball down the field and Rothwell meeting his centre„ was offside. The home forwards became aggressive and led -by Gregson, they attacked strongly, Vernon send- ing in a good shot, Willis turned the ball round the pest. The corner-kick was cleared by Winders, and Taylor transferred to the home jialf. Several free kicks against the visitors relieved the pressure on the home defence, and from h kick taken by Long, Gregson gained the ball and beating many opponents be transferred to Woods for the latter to beat Wallis with a splen- did shot. Following these reverses the visitors played a very bard game, but the interval arrived with the Saints leading by, 1 goal to none. s</p>
        <p>The second portion otiened.rather tamely. and then the home forwards challenged the visitors' defenders, and good play by H. Challinor and Woods placed the Mission goal in danger, when Winders relieved. Some keen tussles were wit- nessed between Vernon ad Theobald, and Taylor gaining possession, 'belt Herbert and centred to -Sherlock, who equalised the score with a neat effort. Result: St. Barnabas', goal; Earkstown Mission, 1 goal.</p>
        <p>ST. BARNABAS' v. FAIRFIELD UNITED. On Thursday night the above teams met on the Lovely-lane ground in their return League fixture. Both clubs were strongly represented, including J. E. Glover, Banner, Houghton andi E. Glover on the Saints' team; and Payne, Kelley awl Whitfield on the United team, all of these being Great Sankey players, and also registered players for the above clubs. The Saints defended the goal at the Workhouse end in the first portion. As the result!( was well known to have an, important beVrifig on the League -championship, some keen Play was witnessed by a good crowd of spectators. :At the interval no goals had been scored. . _</p>
        <p>• The second portion was strenuously; contested and the defence on both sides was hard pressed. At the end of twenty minutes' play in the second half Woods centred from the wing. Bebbington hesitated to clear from a follow up and Woods secured the leather and banged it between the uprights. The United now played desperately for the equaliser, and after Glover and Long .had cleared in fine style the United gained a fruitless corner. Not to be denied, the visitors' forwards came again, and in a 'strong attack Whitfield succeeded in making the scores equal. The play was now exciting and the Saints played up very strongly. A hard drive was sent in which hit the cross-bar; and Banner met the re-bound and netted, putting the Saints in front A keen game ended with the result: St. Burnabas', 2 goals; Fairfield United, 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <p>game was not very interesting, and ended in favour of the home team. Result: Orford White Star, 3 goals;- Warrington Central, 1 goal;</p>
        <p>ST. BARNAEAS' v. EARLESTOWN MISSION.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Earlestown 111i!..sion v. Fairfield -United; S. C. Essenhigh. Walton v. Longford United; B. Howell.</p>
        <p>Monday, the 26th. Orford White Star v. Longford.</p>
        <p>Wednesday, the 28th. Fairfield l'uited v. Longford.</p>
        <p>SHOOTING MATCH.</p>
        <p>MONKS, HALL v. FLETCHER, RUSSELL.. Teams representing Monks, Mil and Co., and Fetcher, Russell and Co., Ltd., met at the range of the former on Tuesday evening. Dis- tance 25 yards, the best eight on tach side io count. Result:—</p>
        <p>Fletcher, Russell :—W. Banks 88, n. White- ley 9(1, J. Buchanan 97, G. Legge 90, J. Phillips 94, J. Quarmby 94. D. Fleming 92. J. Towers 96, J. Gamble 73, G. MePhie 94; 908;1 less two lowest. 161-747.</p>
        <p>Monks, Hall:—A. Webb 90. G. Payne 89, J. Hartley 96. W. Kelly 84, G. Cox 88, C. Shepley 90, A. llighley 95. P. Harrison 97, A. Lester 80, IL Westmoreland 90; 899; less two lowest, 164- 735. Win for Fletcher, Russell, 12.</p>
      </div>
