      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>Parish Council's Annual Meeting.—The annual meeting of the Grappenhall Parish Council was held on Thursday evening, at the School. Mr. F. A. Frost was elected chairman for the ensuing twelve months, on the proposi- tion of Mr. Wooding, seconded by Mr. Winstan- ley.—Mr. Frost proposed and Mr. Bradford seconded that Mr. Lister-Kaye and Mr. Fox be overseers for the year.—lt was decided to hold the meetings for the ensuing year on the first Tuesdays in June, September, December and March, and to levy a small special rate to raise the sum of £5.</p>
        <p>A Dangerous Place.—" One of the most dangerous places in the district," was Mr. Lister-Kaye's description of a well-known road junction at Cartridge-lane, in proposing, at Tuesday's meeting of the Runcorn Rural Dis- trict Council, that the spot be made less danger- ous by a widening scheme involving the pur- chase by the Council of 230 square yards of land belonging to the Lymm Urban District Council. "Motor-ears," the proposer added," come down there at forty and fifty miles an hour." This statement was borne out by Mr. Broome, who seconded, and other speakers. A committee consisting of Messrs. Britch, Lister- Kayo and Pilling was appointed to meet the Lymm Urban District Council on the matter.</p>
        <p>A Sewage Complaint.—Mr. Longland, solici- tor, of Warrington, recently wrote to the Run- corn Rural District Council, on behalf of the trustees of the estate of the lath William Hall, complaining that sewage from property in Knutsford-road had been turned on to property belonging to them at the Commonside Farm. The letter, which was read at Tuesday's meeting of the Council, went on to say that the trustees had been advised that the land affected had been seriously damaged and rendered useless for the purpose of agricul- ture. They requested that immediate steps should be taken for the purpose of restoring the land to a proper condition, and for avoiding any repetition of the injury The Council decided to leave the matter to the Grappenhall Sewerage Committee, who will consider it at an early meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>SPIRITS IN WAR TIME.</head>
        <p>SPIRITS IN WAR TIME.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Those who protest against any consider- able interference with the drink trade in this time of national crisis (the gravity of which is still very generally ignored) forgot that our fore- fathers, during the war of more than a century ago, put up with very great inconveniences in order to assure a sufficient supply of wheat. For them the urgent question was to provide enough bread for the growing population. For us the question is how to supply munitions of war fast enough to meet the needs of our enormous forces. What did they do in order to ensure a sufficiency of wheat? They prohibited abso- lutely the distillation of spirits from grain in the times of scarcity, 1795-6, 1800-2, 1808-11' (Tooke, "History of Prices," Vol. 1., p. 322). If they could forego whisky in order to cheapen bread, cannot we forego it in order to accelerate the supply of munitions of war to our brave soldiers?—.T. Holland Rose, in "The Times."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 24, 1915. WAR STORIES: BY LETTER AND INTERVIEW.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 24, 1915. WAR STORIES: BY LETTER AND INTERVIEW.</p>
        <p>village whose name I cannot, of course, tell I TERRITORIAL CASUALTIES you. What " Tommy " Calls Rest. "We have just finished a six days' tour in the trenches, and have come out for a similar period of 'rest '—though I am afraid the only ` rest ' about it is a change of occupation. Instead of having to fight in the trenches, the troops go out trench digging or trench \ draining almost every night. 0 Foreign Sanitation. "As the fine weather is approaching our chief trouble will be disease—for the French and Belgians appear to have no idea of sanitation as we understand it. Their dead, both men and animals, are either left unburied or only slightly covered with soil. The habits of the people and their heaps of refuse remind one very much of the descriptions of London streets at the time of the Great Plague. Pumps, stables, middens, living rooms, bedrooms, and shops are mingled together in glorious con- fusion.</p>
        <p>The Second Official List.</p>
        <p>3 KILLED; 1 DIED OF WOUNDS; 18 WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>Two official letters containing notifications a-ith regard to casualties sustained by the War- rington Territorial Battalion of the 4th South Lancashire Regiment reached headquarters, the Drill Hall, Warrington, on Thursday morning, and their contents were immediately commu- nicated to the Press by Lieutenant Williamson. The first letter, dated April 13th, stated that No. 2384, Private A. Barker, A Company, of 11, Hardy-street, Warrington, who *as reported as having been wounded on March 27th, was not admitted to hospital, his injury only being a slight one. The second letter, dated April 18th, contained a list of casualties sustained between April sth and April 18th. The list is as follows :--</p>
        <p>Healthy Warringtonians.</p>
        <p>"So far, however, our ni;n have not suffered in health—for we have the least number of men in hospital of any Battalion in the Brigade. Unfortunately, however, the list of killed and wounded is increasing day by day, until we have now some ten per cent. of the men who left England out of the fight. Exaggerated Letters. .</p>
        <p>"The chief topic of conversation here is the duration of the war; any rumour regarding peace couventions, or overture, is eagerly seized upon and discussed for hours at a time. "The Warrington papers are very keenly sought after; and woe betide any soldier whose letter appearing in the Press contains any ex- aggeration—the chaffing he receives is an effec- tual oure for a desire for notoriety. . . ."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>SIX DAYS IN TRENCHES.</head>
        <p>SIX DAYS IN TRENCHES.</p>
        <p>Officers' Billet Shelled.</p>
        <p>KILLED AT END OF CHURCH • PARADE.</p>
        <p>A Warrington Territorial officer writes:— The Trenches, 7 N/10, Tuesday, April 13th, 1915</p>
        <p>Another letter from the trenches. This is the last day of our six days, and we are to be re- lieved this evening. The ourselves are new working together, and relieve one another every six days, changing over both to rest billets and trenches. Now I hardly know what to write about, for all our days are a repetition of duties and to a certain extent monotonous, and as their detail loses interest to us, so it must be with you. We have been lucky again with the weather. During our last stay in the rest billets it rained the whole time and was both raw and cold, so you can imagine the state of the trenches. When we came into the trenches last Wednes- day they were troughs of mud. Mud every- where and everything soaked, but fortunately during our stay we have had practically no rain, and to-day the ground is dry and hard. The ground is mostly clay, and quickly becomes a sticky quagmire, but on the other hand it soon dries up. I have just had to drop this letter. The Huns started to shell this trench with small shrapnel, and we all sought the best cover obtainable. All the shells passed over us and burst in the rear of the trench. We don't mind how near, so long as no direct hits.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <head>Billet Wrecked; Servant Killed.</head>
        <p>Billet Wrecked; Servant Killed.</p>
        <p>Writing about direct hits, the day we came to the trenches the Germans shelled a village and actually put a shell through the roof of C Com- pany's officers' billet, a 6-inch, big at that. The shell passed through the roof and first floor, and burst in the kitchen, where the officers' ser- vants were preparing a meal, killing one and seriously wounding another. The third man had a miraculous escape. He is just standing by me. The house afterwards was a wreck in- side. The officers inside escaped untouched, but lost a lot of their kit and belongings. I am told that Sergeant Goodwin was wounded the other night in the upper part of the leg— very nasty; but I am told nothing serious; and curiously, Sergeant Sollery, another of my C.L.B. lads, was taken to hospital the other night. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0263" n="art0263">
        <head>Aeroplanes Active.</head>
        <p>Aeroplanes Active.</p>
        <p>With the improvement in the weather the aeroplanes are very active. Last night we ob- served six in the air at one time. They are piloted by daring fellows, and it is an object lesson to watch their movements. Ours are certainly more active than the Germans. We can easily distinguish the British planes by the red, white and blue target on the underside of their wings. In some cases the Union Jack painted takes the place of this. The Germans have the design of the Iron Cross, black on white background, under their wings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0264" n="art0264">
        <head>Rat Shooting.</head>
        <p>Rat Shooting.</p>
        <p>Last night we had a rat shooting expedition along our trench. Four whoppers were shot, and to-night we hope to waylay a few more. There seems to be a lot of them in the old willow trees in front of the trench parapet just by our dug-out. We are just going to have a cup of tea brewed in our mess tins 'and boiled over a few twigs we had to collect, and afterwards we prepare for relief. So good-bye for the present. I keep fit and well and the battalion has practically a clean bill of health. Last month we held the record amongst the battalions here for a clean bill of health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0265" n="art0265">
        <head>Back In Billets.</head>
        <p>Back In Billets.</p>
        <p>Thursday evening, April 15th.</p>
        <p>Well, here we are back again—in 'billets safe and sound for the night; it's a pleasant rein from.. the strain of the firing line. Though</p>
        <p>called rest billets there is little-ease about them. We turned in on Tuesday midnight, out again last night digging and draining trenches, and this is the first prospect of a night's rest for some time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0266" n="art0266">
        <head>Spring in a Barren Land.</head>
        <p>Spring in a Barren Land.</p>
        <p>Friday morning, April 16th, 10.15 a.m. I was interrupted for our evening meal, and 8.30 found us all turned in.</p>
        <p>This morning is charming. I am sitting out in the sun without either cog or cap, and in the quietude of the immediate surroundings it is difficult to grasp that murderous war is wag- ing so near in the front of me. The artillery are exercising their horses; to my right men are being taught the process of sapping as ap- plied to trench work, that is, digging under- ground saps or tunnels towards the enemy trench, and from the distance one hears the rumbling of transport waggons on the Belgian highways, rough and lumpy. There is no sound of rifle or artillery fire as this is the quietest time of the day. The crackle of the rifle, which has been going on all night, ceases soon after daybreak, and the sniper appears to rest until late afternoon or night, when he resumes. The artillery have not yet commenced their daily duel. Noon usually finds this commencing, and they continue intermittently for the rest of the day, not that a bombardment or battle is in progress, only each side wants to show the other that it is still alert,and still has a supply of ammunition.</p>
        <p>All the signs of spring are here. The birds are singing, the sky is clear and a perfect blue. But the country is dead and barren, and instead of green fields there is a waste of hard brown earth, sprinkled with hosts of khaki-clad " Tommies," who swarm like bees everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0267" n="art0267">
        <head>Sudden Battle.</head>
        <p>A Sudden Battle.</p>
        <p>Since writing you last one or two incidents have happened. On Tuesday last at 1.30 early morning, a Zeppelin passed over the trenches. We saw it very high up. The droning sound of its engines drew our attention to it. We heard our aircraft guns open fire, and saw the sky flush with the explosion of shells. None of them, however, found its mark. The next day we heard that several bombs had been dropped on our rest billets, doing considerable damage. Another incident. When digging on Wednes- day night just behind the firing line, without the slightest warning a battle commenced within 1,000 yards of us. One moment all was quiet, but the next we were in the midst of an indescribable uproar. Trench mortars, hand grenades, rifle grenades and artillery all belched their deadly charges; explosions were deafening, and the ground vibrated as though in the throes of an earthquake. All this, with the fusillade of rifle and machine gun fire, turned the trenches into an inferno.</p>
        <p>What actually happened nobody here seems to know. But it appears that the Germans blew up one of our sap mines under their trenches, and this started the trouble.</p>
        <p>The affair lasted about threequarters of an hour, and 'there were we on the tip-toe of ex- pectation to be flung into the fray at any moment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0268" n="art0268">
        <head>"Little Jokes."</head>
        <p>"Little Jokes."</p>
        <p>There are two little jokes worth recording, especially to those who know the life here. A man laden with a huge amount of stores, as well as his own kit, is labouring along a narrow communicating trench up to the knees in mud and water, the trench only just wide enough to allow him to pass along. When about half-way he stops, absolutely done, and exclaims in mili- tant language: "Am I a parcels delivery van, a soldier, or a sailor?" A man carrying a sheet of corrugated iron above his head, baker fashion, is halted by a sentry, who calls the familiar "Who are you?" The retort is instaiit in broad Lancashire Army brogue: "A - muffin man, but I have lost my bell."</p>
        <p>Picture these incidents on a dark night, and you an imagine the merriment that followed. That is the way our men are tacklinglthings. No matter how dirty, hungry or irritated they are, an indomitable cheerfulness carries them through. There are no muttqrino''s here every- body is aglow with ardour ana enthusiasm, and we watch and wait with the keenness of d ter- rier waiting for a stone to be thrown, for the day to dawn when we can do our part to give the Huns a stunning blow. We have a drain of losses in these minor movements, and the aggregate of losses in a bigger move might not be greater, though it would bring the satisfac- tion of attempted success. We feel it is springtime at the Front, and it tunes us up to try and be as happy as we can in these sordid surroundings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0269" n="art0269">
        <head>FOOTBALL PATRIOTS. Orford St. John's Players Doing Their Duty.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL PATRIOTS. Orford St. John's Players Doing Their Duty.</p>
        <p>As we have previously remarked, Orford St. John's F.C. has provided 33 soldiers from 36 members; that is all the eligibles went solidly to the service of their country. Not only did they enlist but they are doing their duty in various directions in a manner that was to be expected after their early display of enthusi- astic patriotism. We have this week received copies of several letters which display the right spirit—the spirit by which the Mad Kaiser will be brought to his senses. Up to now two mem- bers of the club have been reported wounded : Rifleman T. Sudell. Driver John Devine. The following are the letters to which we refer :---</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0270" n="art0270">
        <head>SAW MATES WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>SAW MATES WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>Then Felt Like "Fighting Mac."</p>
        <p>Private Ernest Smith, 4th South Lancashire Regiment, of 21, Norris-street, Warrington, says : "I am very sorry I have not wrtten sooner, but you may know we are out to fight, not write, to a certain extent; but you may know how we are fixed. We have had another dust up in the trenches. and I can assure you we came off the best. Yes, to my sorrow, I saw two of my mates get v(ounded, one S. Daniels, and then, felt like Fighting Mae, you may know. I see our Fred (Lance-Corporal Fred Smith) is having a rough time, and I believe he is expect- ing to have a rest shortly. Yes, and you need it. I must say we held the trenches the last encounter, for six days, and luck again favoured us. We have had several shells dropped near us, but we are getting used to them. When we once get into the trenches we are as safe as a rock, but it is in the relieving where our brave lads are losing their lives. You should see the snipers; they are regular knockabouts. The Germans know our range and keep us very busy in the .dug-out that is just behind the firing line. _ _ .</p>
        <p>We are now situa-ted in a nice eomfortable village in and we have the use of a spring. So we can make tea, &amp;c, and have a rare bun struggle on bully beef and dog biscuits. Young Frank Holmes is making rapid progress and expects to be with us again shortly. Daniels is also getting well. I am losing all my pals at once; but never mind, we are doing our duty. It is very cold just at present, and we need a big fire to keep us moderated with warmth. Yes. I don't know how those poor fellows went on that were here at Christmas. I think our Fred is at - and as we are doing a lot of knocking about I may drop on him. As you may know, I have not seen him for 12 months.</p>
        <p>I am very glad you are doing your duty in a good-hearted way, and I only wish every family would do their duty as ours is. lam very glad that T. Sudell is making rapid progress. All the Orford St. John's members over here send their best respects.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0271" n="art0271">
        <head>Demand for Cake.</head>
        <p>Demand for Cake.</p>
        <p>I ,have just written home (21, Norris-street) to ask them if they will send me some more cake, &amp;c. I can tell you there is a rush when a parcel comes. Really we forget all about the war when the cake comes. All the roads are cut up with the transport, and when we have an attack it is a mere mud fight. You would be surprised to -gee the buildings blown to pieces with the shells. I was very sorry to hear about because as a battalion we are going through it When we get slutched up with mud we don't clean up as we did at . Tunbridge Wells; no, we wait till another battalion knocks it off. I thank you very much for papers, &amp;c., and must say they pass a nice hour or so over."</p>
        <p>Private Smith concludes with best luck to all his old Warrington pals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0272" n="art0272">
        <head>WOUNDED IN A BAYONET</head>
        <p>WOUNDED IN A BAYONET</p>
        <p>ATTACK.</p>
        <p>Rifleman T. Sudell, who is now in the Field Hospital, Braaford, and lives at 189, Orford- lane, has been wounded. He played for Orford St. John's at football and cricket for five seasons, and has won about seven medals with this champion junior team. Before enlisting in Kitchener's Army he was employed at Armitage and Rigby's, along with his father, as assistant loom overlooker. Only three days before he got wounded he was in the company of Private John Smith, of the Ammunition Column, so</p>
        <p>he met his injuries when he had been in the firing line only a few hours. "Of course, John," he writes to his clubmato, Private John Smith, "I am having a grand time. Oh, yes, I should say so. Well, I got wounded in a bayonet attack at St. Elor. Yes, we did go through it. lam wounded in my arm, but I am making rapid progress. It was a glorious attack that we made. Fancy me trying to stop machine-gun bullets! I am very glad you are,fn good health and doing your duty like a brave Warringtonian. I am being treated Al over here, and all being well expect to be out on furlough in a week or so. I am very glad our smart football team did enlist, becanse we have set a noble example to the other junior teams in Warrington." lie concludes and wishes to be remembered to all his Warrington friends. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0273" n="art0273">
        <head>ALL OUT FOR ONE THING.</head>
        <p>ALL OUT FOR ONE THING.</p>
        <p>Driver John Ward, R.H.A. writing to Private John Smith, Ammunition/Column, says:— "Just a line to let you know I am still living and in 'the pink. I was glad to receive your letters, &amp;c., and hope to hear again from you as soon as possible. As you may know. lam amongst a lot of strangers and that makes it worse. But never mind, we are all out for the -same thing, and that is to break the Kaiser's Big Army. I did , not know that J. Devine or T. Sudell were wounded, but I am very glad they are recovering. We are having bad weather just lately. Never mind, we can only do our best to keep ,the Germans away from our Homeland. If they got. to Warrington, think of the damage, &amp;c." lie now concludes with best respects to all his Warrington friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0274" n="art0274">
        <head>I DESERVED REST.</head>
        <p>I DESERVED REST.</p>
        <p>Germans Ran Before Fixed Bayonets.</p>
        <p>Rifleman E. Roberts, of Messrs Richmond's, writes to Private John Smith:— ,</p>
        <p>"Still living. Just had a rough time, but I can assure you we came off on top. We have had some terrible fighting since I wrote to you last. It was at about 11 o'clock on the 9th, in a small village. The Artillery started a bombardment and then we rushed the trenches. You should have seen them run when they saw us advancing with fixed swords. When we reached the trenches it was a terrible sight, as they were full of dead Germans. Then we could not get any further because they had all their machine-gums fixed, so we had to be content with what we had done. It was reported that we had killed 7,000 and taken 2,000 prisoners. Not so bad, is it? We are now having a rest, which I think we deserve. I am glad all the Orford St. John's member S are doing their duty." lie concludes with best respects to all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0275" n="art0275">
        <head>"THE GREAT PLAGUE."</head>
        <p>"THE GREAT PLAGUE."</p>
        <p>Quartermaster-Sergt. Coakley Says AVarrington Alen Are Healthy.</p>
        <p>Quartermaster-Sergeant P. J. Coakley, of the 4th South Lancashires—in peace times a teacher in St. Mary's Roman 'Oatholic Schools, Butter- 'market-street--sends another very interesting letter from " somewhere abroad" to a school- master friend in Watrington, anent "Tommy's" life Out there.</p>
        <p>"Since I last wrote," he says, after express- ing thanks for kindness shown him, "the Bat- talion has moved a fey miles to a different posi- tion in the firing line, and we are now billeted in stables and lofts. My present home is the back kitchen of a house in a beautiful little</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0276" n="art0276">
        <head>BREAD, BREAD, BREAD</head>
        <p>BREAD, BREAD, BREAD</p>
        <p>Simple Gift Which WIII Delight</p>
        <p>Warrington "Terriers."</p>
        <p>Mrs. W. Miller, of 33, Leonard-street, War- rington, has just received two interesting missives from her nephews, Privates S. and W. Miller—now serving with the 4th South Lanca- shire Territorials, to which they belonged before the outbreak of war. Private William Miller says :—" I received your parcel, and I can tell you it was welcome. I had not had any bread for a week till I got it. I have just come out of the trenches after being in for six days, so you can tell how it came in. . . . - I gave Sam some of the parcel, and he thanks you very much. . . I am keeping all right at present, but would keep a lot better at home; and I do not care how soon, for I am fed up—but not downhearted, only I know what home is. . .." FORGETS WAR IS ON!</p>
        <p>Private S. Miller states that he is in the best of health, and also describes how they have come out of the trenches.</p>
        <p>"Thank you for the parcel [which included bread]" he continues. "I received it all right, and think it very good of you to think so much about us. .. . It is a very nice parcel— just what we need. I have told my Mrs.' about it. . .</p>
        <p>"We are billeted in an old school at present, and it is a rum place, as there is hardly any roof on it. I mil in the yard writing this letter, and the sun is shining beautifully—it makes you forget there is a war on, only for the roar of the guns. . . . Thank you again for the parcel."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0277" n="art0277">
        <head>THREE HAIRBREADTH ESCAPES.</head>
        <p>THREE HAIRBREADTH ESCAPES.</p>
        <p>Private .1. V. Podmore's Coat Collar</p>
        <p>And Jacket Shot Through.</p>
        <p>Scout Joseph V. Podmore (son of Mr. W. Podmore, hairdresser, 6, Golborne-street) now at the Front with the signalling section 4th South Lancashires, writes to a Warrington gentleman: "April 17th,—I am sill keeping in the pink of condition' and in excellent spirits. . . . We are just having a few days' rest from the firing line prior to going back again for another six days' fighting. You will know that by now we have had over 70 casualties. . . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0278" n="art0278">
        <head>Hun Artillerymen.</head>
        <p>The Hun Artillerymen.</p>
        <p>If you were to come out here it would not half open your eyes—especially near the firing line. The Germans have burnt villages, farms, churches and every mortal thing they could. It has all been done by their artillery, of course; they might well call them Huns '—it is a good name for them. " Extraordinarily Lucky."</p>
        <p>"I have up to now been extraordinarily lucky from being shot I have two bullet holes in my jacket, and one bullet whizzed past ray throat as I was taking a relief party to the trenches; it took the corner off the collar of my jacket. It made me jump, I admit. It is not very Pleasant for a bullet to whizz past you; but it makes me think I shall never be hit at all now, and I am looking forward to being home in a short time. The war cannot possibly last much longer—it is _too complicated. . . .</p>
        <p>"Will you send me a- parcel with some cigarettes in it? . . . "</p>
        <p>" .11'ST AS EAGER TO DO THEIR BIT."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0279" n="art0279">
        <head>Seasoned Campaigner On "The New</head>
        <p>Seasoned Campaigner On "The New</p>
        <p>Hands."</p>
        <p>Private James McHugh, who is serving at the Front with the Sherwood Foresters, writing to his wife from "somewhere in France," under date April 16th, says that his regiment have lost a lot of men, and adds "But the Chaps that are coming up now are just as eager to do their little bit for the old country." Prior to being called up with the Reserve, Private McHugh lived with his wife at 98, Pierpoint-street, Warrington, and worked as a furnace man at Bewsey Forge. In the course of his letter to his wife he utters a mild grumble at the fact that the papers seem too apt to shower praise on the Territorials and to forget the Regular Tommy Atkins. "You may take it from me" he says "that the 'Tommie? who have been out here since last September, and who have had to spend all the winter—in rain, frost and snow, and frequently up to the waist in water—are entitled to some of the credit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0280" n="art0280">
        <head>Nineteen-Day Stretch in the Trenches.</head>
        <p>A Nineteen-Day Stretch in the Trenches.</p>
        <p>“The brigade to which lam attached stuck in the trenches for nineteen days on end. I have read 60/110 of the .soldiers' stories that have appeared in print. The man who wrote that you, could put houses in the holes made by the German 'Jack Johnsons' Was stretching it a bit. They do make terrible holes right enough, but not as big as all that. . . . I have been in the south and have now come to the north, and I could not describe the terrible sights I have seen in my journeyings. At one place we visited I saw little children with arms and legs blown off, and old men and women running for their lives from the Germans. I can assure you that they were pleased when they saw us marching into their village. . . ” Happy " Mitchell With the Ambulance.</p>
        <p>"The weather here is a little better now, but I should not like to have to spend another winter out here. I have only met one Warring- tonian since I got oat; viz., ' Happy ' Mitchell, from Mersey-street. He belongs to the 18th Field Ambulance, and I'll bet he could tell some tales, as he has been going to and from the firing line for the last seven months. Cons&amp; quently he has had the handling of a few of our wounded heroes. . . . I hope the Warring- ton Territorials will,have the best of luck, and that it may be my good fortune to drop across them." •</p>
        <p>The writer concludes with the hope that his wife will send him copies of "The Examiner." and a few Woodbines.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0281" n="art0281">
        <head>SHOT AFTER SERUCE.</head>
        <p>SHOT AFTER SERUCE.</p>
        <p>" Terrier " Tells Of A Sunday Episode.</p>
        <p>Writing to his mother and father, Mr. and Mrs. Taylor, of 1, Kendrick-street, Private Charles Taylor—who will he twenty years of age next July, who formerly Worked at Sme- thurst's. Foundry, and who is now in the machine gun section of the Warrington Terri- torials—describes a drum-head service at Which the " Terriers " attended, and incidentally men- tions that among the hymns sung was the well- known "Fight the good fight." At the close of the 'service the worshippers sang God save the, King." "And," adds the writer, "I'll het the Germans weren't half ratt3;, for they would be able to hear the singing quite plainly: Just</p>
        <p>Continued in next column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0282" n="art0282">
        <head>KILLED IN ACTION.</head>
        <p>KILLED IN ACTION.</p>
        <p>2506, Private W. Reeves, C Company; shrap- nel wounds in the head; April 7th. Home address: 1, Barley-street, Newton-le-Willows. 2517, Private J. Sixsmith, D Company; bullet wound in the left side of the chest; received 12.15 p.m., April Bth. Home address: 48, John. street, Warrington. 1895, Lance-Corporal Wallace Leslie, D Com- pany; bullet through head; received six a.m., April 12th. Home address: 50, Mersey-street, Warrington. DIED OF WOUNDS.</p>
        <p>2140, Private J. Jones, B Company; bullet through abdomen; received April 10th; died same day. Home address: Old Bottom, Har- wich.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0283" n="art0283">
        <head>WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>2487, Private W. G. Baskerville, C Company; shrapnel wounds in the left arm; received April 7th. Home address: 32, Bridge-street, Earlestown.</p>
        <p>2558, Private B. Homer, C Company; bullet wound in the nose; received April 7th. Home address: New Cottages, Orford. 2505, Private G. Plant, C Company; bullet wound in the buttock; received April 7th. HOElle address: The Haws, Ashton-road, Newton-le- Willows.</p>
        <p>1753, Private J. Broadhurst, D Company; bullet wound in the upper arm) received April 7th. Home address: 71, Lovely-lane, Warring- ton.</p>
        <p>1752, Private W. Robinson, D Company; bullet wound in the lower leg; received April 7th. Home address: 127, Bostock-street, Warrington. 1552, Private L. Gough, A Company; bullet wound in the lower leg; received April 7th. Home address: 15, Percy-street, Warrington. 1469, Sergeant H. S. Goodwin, C Company; bullet wound in the lower leg; received April 10th. Home address: 9, Froghall-lane, Warring- ton. _ _</p>
        <p>152. Sergeant C. H. Walker, B Company; bullet wound in the leg; received April 11th. Home address: 2, Marsh-street, Warrington. 2445, Private F. H. Parkinson, A Company; bullet wound in left side of the neck; received April 11th. Home address; Knutsford-road, Grappenhall. 2259, Private J. P. Mitchell, C Company; bullet wound in the chest; received April 12th. Home address: 10, Union-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>-2-676, -Private i. Lloyd, B Company bullet wound in the left side of the head; received April 13th. Home address: 1. Enstace-street, Warrington. 2296, Private R. Woods, A Company; bullet wound in left thigh; received April 12th. [The official roll contains the names of several Territorials bearing the name Woods, but no R. Woods can be traced; nor is the number correctly given.]</p>
        <p>2389, Private T. Dalton, B Company; bullet wounds in the left shoulder and forearm; received April 13th. Home address: 112, Forster-street, Warrington. 2503. Lance-Corporal Slinn, C Company; bullet wound in the back; received April 13th. Home address: Market-street. Newton-le-Willows.</p>
        <p>64. Lance-Corporal A. Woods, C Company; bullet wound in back; received April 14th. Home address: 10, Nora-street, Warrington. 1337. Private J. Holmes, C Company: bullet wound in the right thigh; received April 14th. Home address: 33. George-street, Golborne. WOUNDED, BUT NOT ADMITTED TO</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0284" n="art0284">
        <head>HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>Second-Lieutenant L. Holden, B Company: in- juries to face caused by fragments of shattered periscope: received April 7th. 1794, Private A. Macdonald, D Company: bullet wound in the side of the head: received April 9th. Home address: 46, Station-street, Crewe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0285" n="art0285">
        <head>REFUGEES ENTERTAINED. Co-operative Society As Hosts At Big Tea Party.</head>
        <p>REFUGEES ENTERTAINED. Co-operative Society As Hosts At Big Tea Party.</p>
        <p>There were gay scenes at the Co-operative Hall on Thursday evening, when the Co-op- erative Society lavishly entertained represen- tatives from all the Belgian Refugees' Homes in the town—as well as twenty from Penketh and 8 from Padgate. About 150 attended alto- gether, and there were also present: Messrs. J. Upson, Smith, W. H. Sherburn, Waldman. Ferguson, Massey, Sinclair, Rushton, Falkner, Wright and J. Jarman (secretary); from the Co-operative Committee; and from the Belgian Refugees' Committee: Mesdames Roberts, Vere Davies, Geary, Johnson and 11. Jolley; Miss Broadbent; and Messrs. C. E. Parker, J. Fair- hurst and W. Furness. Alderman Bennett also attended.</p>
        <p>After an excellent repast the guests W(1( regaled with chocolates and cigarettes, and Mr. John Upson addressed the gathering, Miss Broadbent interpreting :—" It is with great pleasure," he said, "that I; as President of the Co-operative Society, give you a very hearty welcome here to-night. (Applause.) We want you to be very happy. We know that the past with many of you must be Very black; that the future must also be dark with anxiety and doubt. We feel that, while you appear to be very happy here and now, black care must yet sit upon many of you. This is true, we are sure, especially of those among you who are old and middle-aged. But we hope that you will for a few hours forget the dark past, and that you will cease to entertain the fore- boding. The past is dead; the morrow is un- born. We are only in the present, and we hope that you will be happy while you are with us." (Loud applause.) The capital programme which followed had been arranged by Mr. A. E. Trow, who also acted as accompanist. The artistes were : Madame Gertrude H. Brookes (of Manchester —who, accompanied by Mr. Bennett North, sang delightfully in French); Mrs. C. Fairhurst (with Mr. Trow a pianofort duettist); Mr. Bradford Barton; and De Meglis (conjurer and expert paper-manipulator). A brass quar- tet from the Penketh Tannery Band opened the programme with rousing selections from the National Anthems of the Allies, and also contributed other fine items.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0286" n="art0286">
        <head>GALLANT TERRITORIAL.</head>
        <p>GALLANT TERRITORIAL.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Coroner's Court yesterday, a jury highly commended the gallantry of Pte. Thomas Blenkinsop Jackson. of the 2nd/4th South Lancashire Regiment, who, on Wednesday, made a gallant, but vain, attempt to rescue Ernest Stockton, aged 30, a wiredrawer, of 136, Church-street, who threw himself into the River Mersey near the Top Cut Fields and was drowned. The jury in returning a verdict .)f "Suicide whilst of unsound mind," asked the Coroner to bring Private Jackson's bravery to the notice of the Royal Humane Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0287" n="art0287">
        <head>WAR STORlES—ccntinued.</head>
        <p>WAR STORlES—ccntinued.</p>
        <p>, as the service was over Private Parkinson was hit." •</p>
        <p>In a subsequent letter the writer says: "On April 14th the Germans blew one of our trenches up, but they were sorry for it afterwards, for our guns went off, and . . . well, lam sorry for those Germans. For about half an hour our guns poured shells into their trenches from all sides. . . . As I write we are in a large house, having a concert, and some very good tal- ent we have got." In one of his recent letters he wrote home, Private Taylor asked his mother to do all in her power to persuade his young men friends to "join up," adding that he felt convinced that if they only realised the situation and what the Regulars had had to put up with, they would not be long before. they lent their aid.</p>
        <p>• • •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0288" n="art0288">
        <p>J</p>
        <p>THIRD BATTALION.</p>
        <p>More Men Needed.</p>
        <p>Major Crosfield has written to Captaie Townroe, who is in charge of the recruiting staff for the new (third) Territorial Battalion of the South Lancashire Regiment, saying that he hopes the men, and especially the unmarried men of Warrington, will come forward in large numbers, and so help their pals to suppress German militarism. The need for men lS urgent.</p>
        <p>It has been decided that the new Battalion will not be sent to Tunbridge Wells, but to a camping ground not far from a wall-kmiwn watering place.</p>
        <p>There-i;4- a growing response to the appeal fa recruits, this week's figures being better than those of last week.</p>
        <p>The badges with the statement "On Service.; This house has sent a soldier" has caught on," and many windows now proclaim the homes that are doing "their bit." Captain Townroe has made another tour of the picture houses, and has exposed the fallacy of the idea that the war will soon be over, and that no more men will be needed by showing that, except in the Carpathians, the Cermane,:'.. still hold, roughly speaking, the same lineii that' they held 'Six months ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0289" n="art0289">
        <head>RALLY.</head>
        <p>A RALLY.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening a recruiting rally wag held for the new Territorial Battalion in the Earlestown district. Messrs. Crosfield's Band generously gave their services, and the attrac- tions of hearing this celebrated prize band attracted large open-air audiences at Ashfon-in- Makerfield and Earlestown. Captain Townroo had the help of Mr. E. J. Radford, a well-known local orator, and ,of Lieut. Walter Stone, of Newton-le-Willows, an officer of the Loyal North Lancashire Regiment, who was wounded at Ypres in arm and legs.</p>
        <p>Lieut. Stone's words, pithy and direct, made a distinct impression. He said at the two open- air meetings and at the picture palaces visited:i "Are you content to sit there wakching pictures while men are dying for you in France? There are plenty yet who ought to go. Are you afraid? We've all got to die sometime. Take an es- ample from the Bantams. They are below the standard, but they're gone like men. And you big unmarried chaps sit here and watch pic- tures !"</p>
        <p>It is hoped that the rally, which attracted several thousands, will stimulate recruiting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0290" n="art0290">
        <head>PH I LOIVIATHS AND WAR. Generous Contributions To Comforts Funds.</head>
        <p>PH I LOIVIATHS AND WAR. Generous Contributions To Comforts Funds.</p>
        <p>The annual general meeting of the Warring- ton Philomathic Society was held in the Trinity Chambers, Bridge-street, on Wednesday even- ing. A representative gathering of members was presided over by Mr. Norman Nield (presi- dent).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0291" n="art0291">
        <head>WAR SYLLABUS.</head>
        <p>WAR SYLLABUS.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. G. Burgess (hon. secretary) presented his report of the work of the session. The ex- traordinary position of affairs in Europe, he said, had naturally had its effect/. The subject of every meeting had had some bearing upon the international situation, and the council had taken great care to cover, as far as possible, the whole ground without any overlapping. The meetings had never before reached so uniformly high a standard, and it was much to be regretted that a larger number of members had not taken advantage of them. He hoped that when there was a more settled state of affairs in the outside world the enthusiasm of members would in- crease—for it was upon such enthusiasm that the success of the society depended.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0292" n="art0292">
        <head>For Fumiferous Fighters.</head>
        <p>For Fumiferous Fighters.</p>
        <p>The usnal annual dinner had not, been held- A sum of £4 15s. id. and a quantity of cigar- ettes had been collected at the ordinary meet- ings of the society towards the Tobacco Funds for the Army and Navy; a part having been sent to the Mayor of Warrington's Fund and a part to the London Fund for wounded sailors oil board the hospital ships. fr The Secretary's report was adopted, upon the proposition of Mr. W. 11. Tickell, seconded IT Mr. J. Walsh.</p>
        <p>Mr. D. J. Dunn (the hon. treasurer) presented his accounts, showing a very satisfactory bal- ance in hand. These were adopted, upon the proposition of Messrs. Rathbono and Woods.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-04-24_WidnesExaminer_art0293" n="art0293">
        <head>OFFICERS ELECTED.</head>
        <p>OFFICERS ELECTED.</p>
        <p>The following officers were elected for sessiori 1915-16: President, Mr. N. Nield ; vice-president, Mr. D. J. Dunn; lion. secretary, Mr. E. G. Burgess; hon. treasurer, Mr. H. Dearden. The members of the Council are: Messrs. A. H. Rathbone, J. Walsh, and C. S. Woods; hoe- auditors, Messrs. H. P. , Dallow and W. IL Tickell, and hon. lanternist, Mr. H. H. Taylor. Hearty votes of thanks were accorded to Mr* Taylor for his services at the lantern during: the past session, and to Mr. Nield for his able presidency of the society.</p>
        <p>Printed and' published by John Walker and Co.' (Warrington). Limited, at the EXAUDIS General Printing Works, Bewsev-street, War" rington; also published by. them at t.. 13. EXAMINIE Offices, 2, Victoria,road, Widnes; Db. George-street, St. Helens; 3, Market-street, Earlestown, all in the County of Lancaster: and at Church-street, Runcorn, in the Could/ of Chester.</p>
        <p>1.1•••■IJ</p>
      </div>
