        <p>"Just a few lines to let you know that I am still living, though I have been wounded. This may have happened for the best; anyway the hurt is not much. It happened on Tuesday, the 13th, just as I was cooking my dinner in the trenches. A bullet came right through the sandbags and into my arm. I wondered what was up! My arm dropped!! Then I knew !! ! Well, I am bandaged up, and here I am! So that's all right! Of course, you don't know exactly where I have been, as we are not allowed to tell. But I may say that we have been in Belgium all the time. You can't imagine what it is like out there. One cannot describe it! It is too awful !!" The writer concludes by referring to the Vicar of Runcorn as "a toff." LANCE-CORPORAL A. WOODS. Bullet Passes Clean Through Ills Left Arm. The news that Lance-Corporal Albert Woods had been wounded' was -first received locally by his wife, at 10, Nora-street, Warrington. It reached her on Sunday morning—a postcard from him saying that he was wounded, was going down to the base, and progressing well. On Tuesday morning, together with a field postcard, came this letter:- 17th April,—Just a few lines to let you know that I am going on all right. I was sent down to Boulogne last night, and I am in hospital here—as you will know by now. I was wounded in my left arm, the bullet going clean through and slightly grazing my chest. I can tell you that I felt thankful to God that it is no worse; so buck up, and don't be upset about it. Let us hope for the best. .. . Hoping to see you soon and tell you of my many adventures—l have gone through some since coming out here! ... LONG ARMY SERVICE. Forty-one years of age, Lance-Corporal Woods married nearly twenty years ago; and has for 28 years been employed at Ellesmere-street as a wire-weaver by Messrs. Locker. With his</p>
        <p>family he attends the Wesleyan Mission in that street. It is n years since the Lance-Corporal joined the Lancashire Volunteers, to which he belonged until transferring to the Territorials on, their . inauguration. He had not seen active service before the great war, but holds the long service medal—which he received six years ago. He is now in C Company. When in D Com- pany, about five years back, he gained the high distinction of carrying off the company silver challenge cup for range' shooting at Appleton. Before carrying off "for keeps" this handsome prize—which was offered by Mr. Malin Edelsten, then Captain of the Company—it was necessary. for him to win it for four years. This Lce.-Corpl. Woods did. In addition to this fine trophy, he has on various occasions also gained many prizes</p>
