        <p>flays' leave at Christmas. Since then he has Paot written home, and full particulars of his injury are now most eagerly awaited by his relatives. HOW PRIVATE SIXSMITH DIED. Stretcher-Bearers .Were ,Unable To Come For Many Hours. Private William Miller writes from the Front:— "14th April,—You will have heard of the killed and wounded. We have lost two killed, and I was in the same trenches. It upset is all. The first was Sixsmith; he was a nice fellow. He got 'shot quite simply, and we had a time. The officer and I did all we could for him. We kept him alive until the stretcher- bearers came, but they cannot come till dark —and he got shot about 12.301" lANCE-CORPORAL LESLIE'S LAST ACT. Shot Through Being Too Zealous : Only Smiled At Danger. Private William Miller, in a letter to his aunt at 33, Leonard-street, refers to the Terri- torial casualties. "14th April,—Walter Leslie, Mersey-street, did not live long," said the Private. "He got hit in the head—that was on Monday morning about seven o'clock. He was having a shot, and stopped up too long. He was told about it, but only smiled. He had a few shots in the same</p>
